      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>&amp;roc mortals talk so much that nobody hears what they say. WE are glad the war is over in China; also glad that it was over in China. IN 'ME SAME WAY.—He: "They married in haste." She: "And, of course' repented at leisure?" He: "No." She: "No?" He: "No. They repented the same way. As OTHERS SEE Us.—Cobwigger : "You possess that rare gift of being able to tell the moment your clothes begin to look shabby." Merritt: "That's easily acquired. As soon as the bootblacks stop calling Shine, sir?' after me, I know it is time to order a new suit."</p>
        <p>NOT MUCH Risx.—Examining Physician (for insurance company) : "I'm afraid we can't take you, sir. You are too great a risk." Applicant (resignedly) "Well, perhaps I am. The fact is that when I get sick I never send for a doctor. I just keep quiet until I get well." Examining Physician: " Eh ? Urn—we'll take you." Sun: "The man I marry must be 'only a little lower than the angels." He (suddenly flop- ping) : "Here I am on my knees a little lower than one of them." "HENRY, you look very pale. What's the trouble?" "I was stung to the quick by an adder this afternoon." "How did it happen ? ' "Why, I dropped in at the bank, and the bookkeeper told me my account was overdrawn." Too HARSH.—Fair Devotee: "I don't see any way to raise our church debt, except to have a lottery." Minister (shocked) : "That will never have my sanction, madame—never, unless you call it by some other name." CHAINING A BEAUTY.—Jinks : "Everybody pre- dicted that Hardhead would have trouble after he married that vain beauty; but she never leaves her home unless he is with her. How does he manage?" Winks: "He filled the house with mirrors." • IT WAS A CAMOMILE.—" I could have laughed outright," said Mrs. Brown, "when we were going round Barnum and Bailey's menagerie on Friday." "Why, what happened ?" asked Mrs. Jones. "Well, Mrs. Smith called an animal a seraph—of course, she meant a giraffe; but the fun of it was, it wasn't a giraffe at all—it was a camomile.!" STRANGE that a man cannot see as far forward as he can backward. But it is also lucky for him that he cannot, for if he could see far ahead he would probably be afraid to go on. WHERE SILENCE IS BEST.—" What should a fellow say," asked the young man, anxiously, "when his wife asks him if he would marry again if she were to die?" "Say nothing, of course," answered the elderly adviser. "If he says he would she'll think he doesn't like her. If he announces his intention of staying single she will have the idea that he is tired of matrimony." A BARE HorE.—" Didn't you tell that young man I was out ? " said the young woman. "Oi did, miss," replied Bridget. "But he towld me to come back an' ax yez again, so's to be sure Oi hadn't misunderstood his name." HE HAD TO.—The Man of the Rouse: "You are not a cousin, or anything like that, of our fair hostess, are you?" "No ; nothing of that sort." "Well, did you ever attend such a stupid affair in your life ? ' "Ye-es, a good many. I—l'm her husband, you see, and I have to." As George folded the fair young creature to his heart, a (full, crackling sound smote his ears. " Ah, it's good-bye to those sixpenny cigars in my vest pocket," he said, grimly: "but never mind the expense at a moment like this !" IT is generally the gun that wasn't loaded that does the mischief, and it is oftentimes the man</p>
        <p>who hasn't anything in him that hits the mark.</p>
        <p>A HOPELESS CASE.—Visitor "What is his pecu- liar form of insanity ? " Warder of Insane Asylum: "He thinks he can find the man who stole his umbrella." FILLED THE REQUIE,EMENT.—Seedy Individual: "How much will you loan me on this suit of clothes ? " Pawnbroker: "Nothing. I advance money simply on entire wardrobes." Seedy 0 Individual: "Well, this is my entire wardrobe." IT is doubtful if there would be any enjoyment for good people in this world if there were no bad people with whose bad actions they could contrast s their own goodness. GETTING EVEN.—Austere lady: "Madam, your child is annoying me with his attention 1 I wish you would keep it to yourself." Mother (suppres- sing her anger) : "Oh ! you must excuse him. He thinks you are his grandmother, and he is very fond of her." A NOVEL FEATURE.—Parke : "Saw a remark- able thing at a séance recently. I was on the committee, and we fastened the medium securely in the cabinet—bound him hand and foot—there was no collusion of any kind." Clarks : "How can you tell there wasn't?" Parke : "Because he couldn't get out." IT was night. The wind swept down the deserted street in fitful gusts, and inky black- ness covered the sky. Crouching behind a tree and concealed within its deep shadow was the form of a. man. His eyes gleamed with a baleful light, and he held a gleaming knife, six inches long, that being the accepted length of his breath. For a long time he waited thus, until at last a step was heard, and as the unwary, belated pedestrian approached, the would-be murderer was about to plunge the deadly weapon into his victim's breast, when he suddenly paused, and as the knife dropped from his nerveless hand, he hissed through his clenched teeth: "No ! I'll be hanged if I do ! " SOUND AND FURY. An English gentleman travelling in France was very much annoyed at the •slowness of the pace, and wishing to get the coachman to increase the speed, he tried to speak to him in French; but he spoke the language very badly, and, as the coachman did not understand, the gentleman lost patience. At last he thought that if he could not make him undestand, he misht at least frighten him by using some high-sounding words, and he roared into the ear of the coachman, "Westmoreland, Cumberland, Northumberland, Durham t" This had the desired effect. Cabby immediately whipped up his horses and drove full speed to a place where he could deposit a man whom he believed to be crazy. THEN THE FIGHT BEGAN.—An old Kentucky woman was summoned as a witness to tell what she knew about a fight at her house several nights before, in which three or four people were killed. ,She mounted the stand with evident reluctance and many misgivings, and when questioned by the -court as to what she knew about the matter, said: "Well, jedge, the fast I knowed about it was when Bill Sanders called Tom Smith a liar, en' Tom knocked him down with a stick of wood. One of Bill's friends then hit Tom with a knife. Sam Jones, who was a friend of Tom's, then shot the other fellow, en' two more shot him, en' three or four others got cut right smart by somebody. That naturally caused some excitement, jedge, en' thee they commencca</p>
        <p>The 'Royal Laundresses say Critchley's " STAVCH GLOSS Is unequAlled for imparting a beautiful finish to starched linen ic., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr.—. Corn- wall. says :---" My wife will not fin 13h our linen without your " March Gloss.'" From Mrs —, Bradford :—Der sir.— lam pleased to Inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes end Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It ,should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., andifid., by most starch sellers. If not in stock wi;l procure it. T. 'CRITCHLEr, Blackburn, ProprietOr.—Ada.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAI.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAI.</p>
        <p>-- The Unitarians, who de not ordinarily manifest much zeal as propagandists, have inaugurated what is termed a "forward movement" in Lon- don. The programme consists of the de- livery of five Sunday evening lectures simul- taneously in all the Unitarian places of worship in the metropolis, on Protestant principles as understood by that body. The first of the series was delivered on Sunday night in seventeen differ- ent districts, the subject being "The Church of the Early Christians." The other topics to be treated are "The Priest—Ancient and Modern " ; " What the Reformation did for Men," " The Further Reforma- tion Needed," and "Principles and Doctrines of Unitarians."</p>
        <p>It is said that America is somewhat excited over the recent marriage of Mrs. Vanderbilt, the mother of the Duchess of Marlborough, who was divorced some time age from Mr.Vanderbilt,upon the ground of incompatibility of temper, and was married re- cently to Mr. Oliver Belmont, son of the late Mr. August Belmont, the New York banker. After all, such a marriage is not surprising, as in the United States—of all places in the world—divorce is only an incident, and re-marriage an episode in a career. A good story is told of a several times divorced and re-married lady meeting a friend in Paris whom she had not seen for several years. She had almost forgotten him, and when he claimed acquaintance on the ground of having met her in New York, she answered airily, "Oh, yes I remember now, of I course. Let me see, who was I then?"</p>
        <p>The annual report of the association for the em- ployment of reserve soldiers gives some interesting , information. The number who return to civil lifel every year is about 16,000, and they are all young fellows under 26, and in the prime of health and strength. They are simply turned adrift upon society, to sink or swim according to their luck or energy, and the former is often their fate. For the most part they are unable to follow any trade, and the humbler grades of the labour market being already overstocked, do not afford them an outlet. The association comes to the aid of the best of the men, and it has helped about a fourth of those who retired last year to obtain employment. This leaves 12,000 men still stranded and idle. Of those who have no: yet obtained permanent situations 400 have found temporary employment in Sir A.Harris's military spectacle at Olympia, where their pre- cision, knowledge, and smartness are particularly serviceable. It is unfortunate, however, that the Government, in creating a short service system, has not been able to connect with it some outlet for the men after leaving the ranks.</p>
        <p>-- Poor Mr. Kipling ! We see that the Philadelphia Board of Education has resolved to exclude several of his books from the Public Library because of "the profanity of some of their characters." A re- quest has since been submitted to the board for a similar interdict of " Trilby " because of its "im- moral teaching."</p>
        <p>--- The arrangements which Lord Salisbury has made with France with regard to Siam will be severely called in question as soon as Parliament meets. The fact that the concession made by the Tory Premier is ten times greater than was ever contemplated by Lord Rosebery's Cabinet is well calculated to place Mr. Curzon, Lord Salisbury's Under Secretary, in an amusingly awkward position. The speeches he made in opposition to the Siam frontier may be worked as an inexhaustible quarry by anyone who things it worth while to expose Mr. Curzon's incon- sistency, and to contrast Tory denunciations of the flaccidity falsely attributed to Lord Rogehery with the wholesale yieldings of a Government which seems to give up every- thing other Powers choose to ask. Mr Labouchere, of course, will not join with those who object to Lord Salisnury's complacency to French aggrandise- ment, as he has expressed himself well content with the Siam settlement. But Mr. Labouchere is none the less capable of appreciating the humour of the situation as it affects Mr. Curzon. As to Sir Ellis Ashmead-Bartlett, it is quite certain that he will no longer fix with his glittering eye a Trea- sury Bench that cravenly neglects British interests and fritters away British prestige. But although nobody is likely to take the trouble to look up the revilings in which Sir Ellis Ashmead-Bartlett mis- takenly attributed to Lord Rosebery the policy of " scuttle " which Lord Salisbury has carried out, it is impossible to repress a smile at Sir Ellis Ash- mead-Bartlett's pitiable plight now that the curses he hurled at the Liberals come home to roost among his own party leaders. _ _</p>
        <p>-- Some members of the speculative ring in the City have been buoying themselves up with the belief that the Chartered Company stands to the soil of the vast territory it administers in the same rela- tion as an owner of land in this country does to his property. In other words, they contend that the Company might sell the surface and the mineral rights to any company or individual who might be willing to purchase them—say, to Mr. Rhodes or Mr. Barn ato. The object of this contention is ob- vious. It is to establish in the Company an inher- ent right to the. property of a territory as vast as the half of Europe, and to fix a basis of compensa- tion in the event of the Government cancelling the charter. The same contention always lays a found- ation on which to bolster up the price of the Char- tered Company's shares in the market. Now,what- ever Lord Knutsford may have intended, and whatever the charter may provide, it :is quite cer- tnin that in the granting of the charter the Fng- lish people had no idea they were conferring rights of absolute ownership on Mr. Rhodes, the Duke of Fife, and the Company generally. The charter re- serves power to the Government, in certain events to revoke it and to annul all the rights and privi- leges it confers. And one of these events would he the implication of the Company in the recent invasion of the Transvaal. That event has brought us face to face with the folly of having called the Company into existence at alt. But the Stock Exchange view of its legal rights is not likely to he the view of the Government ; it will not be the view of the English people; and one can hardly imagine that any court of law will convert a pub- lie trust into a private property.</p>
        <p>A curious proof of the disapproval with which the recent conduct of the German Emperor is regarded by Germans resident in this rountry is afforded by an incident connected with His Imperial Majesty's birthday. It was proposed at a large German club in London, where the members are in about equal proportions connected with commerce and the liberal professions, to hold a dinner in cele- bration of the Emperor's birthday, and a list was opened for that purpose. Only eleven members. however, signified their willingness to attend, and nearly three times that number. I aril told, wrote to say that if the dinner was held they would resign their membership of the club. In these circum- stances the dinner was abandoned. It is probable that many of those who abstained from joining in the proposed celebration did PO simply because they thought the present an inopportune moment for an unofficial body of Germans to pay special honour to a Sovereign who had roused such a deep feeling of resentment among the people whose hospitality they enjoy, but the threat of resignation points to the existence of a strong feeling of disapproval of the Emperor's action—a feeling which is. I believe. most widely entertained among Germans engaged in commercial pursuits, whose business affairs have been most injuriously affected by the strained Ma- I tions between the two countries.</p>
        <p>The Empress of Germany put her foot in it the other day, if so plebeian an expression may be used in ~-onnection with so august a lady. The Court jackdaw has been chattering about it ever since,and if the voluble bird is to be trusted, her Imperial Majesty was scolded by his Imperial Majesty just like an ordinary every day husband. The cause of the trouble arose from the fact that the Empress does not love Catholics; perhaps the Emperor has no great affection for the Church. of Rome either, but he rules over too many Cattiolics to have public opinions on the subject, and William IT. has always been most respectful to the doctrines of Rome. A Catholic prelate asked her Majesty to contribute to a certain Catholic charity, and after flatly raf using she added, "I have no sympathy with such doe- trines." "The Emperor heard all about it before evening, and her Majesty was treated to a curtain lecture, with a " again 'climax. When the august lady again ap- peared in public her tearful eyes and pink nese pathetically attested that the life of an Empress is not without its domestic jars." Thus says an enter- prising American journal.</p>
        <p>RUPTURES.—REYNOLDS' Patent Imperceptible Indiarubber Stem Pad TRUSS. Suitable in all cases. Perfect Fit Ensured. Write or call for particulars.— REYNOLDS. 10, Slater-treet (a few doors off Bold-street), Liverpool. Open on Saturday until? i..m.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>'THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 1. 1806</head>
        <p>'THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY FEBRUARY 1. 1806</p>
        <p>visiting ionable and experienced furrier the other day,l disctivered, with regard to the tiny carcases we have all bt,n,hanging round our necks for the last year or two,that ilo heads are used at all. A piece of harm- less, necessary cork,a couple of glass eyes,and any scrap of fur that will serve the purpose, are sufficient where- with to make up the head for a becoming boa.</p>
        <p>During the past month many have been the comments in numbers of papers and journals upon Leap Year, and the attraction it is supposed to have for those of our sex who do not happen to °be of the "New Woman" persuasion. It seems, by the way,that the ancient cus- tom allowing the lady to " propose" was accompanied by what jurists call a sanction. Any gentleman who ex- ercised his" courtesy of refusal " was bound to present the rejected suitor with a silk dress. Now, although there are a few, a very few who would like to see the right to propose conferred upon women by the unwritten laws of society, the majority of us have no sympathy with the idea, and for a very good reason—it is not necessary. There are numberless little ways in which a woman can discover if a man reciprocates her feelings, and show him she reurns his affection for her without the indignity and crude horror of a "proposal." and Leap Year or no Leap Year, it is very certain no self- respecting girl would take advantage of its so called " privilege." For morning and early afternoon it is impossible to dress the hair too simply. It should be just waved loosely back from the forehead to form a sort of halo round the face, and drawn high at the back in a tight little twist. But for evening the question of hairdress- ing is a much more important affair. Our hair, to be correct, must be well waved, dressed as loosely as pos- sible at the sides and kept in position by side combs. At the back it must be dressed high, but not on the top of the head, and so arranged as to form loops to look like the petals of flowers.—The existence or non-exist- ence of a fringe is purely optional, but if we do choose to cover our forehead it must be with small round curls neatly arranged. No such thing as a fuzzy cloud of hair is permissible at the present moment. Very light paste ornaments, mounted on tortoiseshell pins are in great favour for the hair cost now, as are also beautifully modelled dragon-flies ia diamonds, with opals in the wings. When studying an exceptionally well dressed head the other night, at the theatre, which happened to be further adorned with this lovely, shim- mering irridescent, sun reflecting insect, I could not help thinking how much more appropriate it is to stimu- late this particular insect than the beetles or reptiles we have lately indulged in. Many of the combs with high heads of cut out tortoiseshell, in really fine old lace work design, which adorn so many fashionable heads at the present moment, are just what we want to help us in our somewhat elaborate hairdressing operations, and they are particularly becoming to the majority of women.</p>
        <p>Speaking of tortoiseshell reminds me that there has recently appeared upon the scenes an imitation so per- fect as to deceive all but the most experienced eye. This is good news for those of us who have a weakness for tortoiseshell toilet appurtenances, monogrammed with silver. Embossed silver-backed brushes are now much too general to be desirable, and the fastidious are turn- ing their attention to other ware, amongst which will undoubtedly be counted the new " tortoisesliellette." But to return to my subject, viz , hairdressing, the question of how to alleviate the sorrows of the straight- tressed majority is still an impossible one. Fluids, pins, hot irons, all have done their best,or worst, in the cause of fluffiness,but one coarsens,another breaks,and all more or less ruin the hair. Again,where shall we find a much more decoratively ignominious predicament than that of her whose front locks are corkscrewed up in steel pinsior leathern sausages or what you will, during the proba- tionary period that must needs elapse ere the curve desirable be arrived at, and the premeditated curls combed out to carelessness? Rare indeed is the woman whose hair curls and waves by the grace of Nature, and happy indeed is her lot. She need never have a town) or veil,she may enjoy a wet west wind.a warm rain,the pleasant moisture of sea air and sea full in her face and never have to pay the price of such desperate delights in lank unloveliness of coiffure. There is, just now,a tendency to run into extremes in the matter of pointed footgear, but I would strongly urge those of my readers who cannot indulge in the very best of boots and shoes, to carefully avoid this par- ticular , departure. Pointed toes require specially fas- hioned lasts and the very nicest and mist immaculate padding to keep the toes from turning upwards. Cheap shoemaking cannot afford to give either the time or care to the proper manipulation of these pointed toes,and the effect is not unfrequently a most undesirable and ludi- crous one. If only the average woman would indulge in boot-trees, however, much trouble with regard to the shape of our footgear might be avoided, for these trees not only secure an even surface for polishing, but they force out all the creases. High heels should on no account be indulged in as they are not only in bad taste but unhealthy. These high-heeled shoes first appeared in the reign of Louis XIV. They were often five inches in height, and walking was thus made very difficult and painful, but the sacrifice seems to have been cheerfully undertaken. Several West-end bootsellers have recently assured me, when questioned upon this particular point,that the feet of our eountrywomen are growing larger. Frequently they sell boots of a size and strength nowadays that would have shocked the fashionable woman to have looked upon, much less worn, a few years back, and from these facts we learn that not only are women more sensible and consequently more inclined to wear boots which promote ease and comfort than they once were, but also that, as women have on the average increased very greatly in general physique of late years,so too have their feet, in proportion, increased in size. A good plan whereby to prevent the odour of strong vegetables like cabbage, turnip and onion from permeat- ing every nook and cranny of the house while cooking is to place a large slice of bread on top. The bread absorbs every particle of odour, and although soft, remains in condition to be easily removed by means of a skimmer.</p>
        <p>C RANGE CASE is delicious for afternoon tea. Here is the receipe :—Cream together three ounces of butter, three ounces of castor sugar, then add three well-beaten eggs, when thoroughly beaten sift in gradually the grated rind of an orange and half a pound of flour,with which is mixed a teaspoonful of baking-powder. Bat well and then place in a greased tin and bake in a quick oven for an hour.</p>
        <p>RABBIT CROXESICIES make a nice breakfast dish. Take any pieces of cold rabbit and mince them finely, add a heaped up teaspoonful of grated ham and place in a saucepan with some melted butter, enough to moisten it, and a small quantity of grated lemon peel. Let it thoroughly boil, stirring all the time, then pour it in a soup-plate and set aside to get quite cold. Roll up into small sausage shapes, dip each in butter, fry a pale colour and serve hot.</p>
        <p>" MADGE:'</p>
        <p>In Spain a baby's face is swept with a pine tree bough to bring good luck. Leather gloves were first used at the beginning of the seventeenth century. It is said that cats are the most difficult animals of all to train for performances. Celery acts upon the nervous system, and is a cure for rheumatism and neuralgia. The man who kills two organ grinders where one lived before is a public benefactor. The rose among the Romans was the emblem of becrecy ; hence the phrase, " sub rosa." According to the computation of the Russian chrono- logists, the creation took place B c. 5508. An American jury has decided that the bagpipe is merely a medieval instrument of torture. Pneumonia usually attacks the healthy, robust person more often than it does the weak thin person. One of the latest novelties is a phonographic cloc which calls the hours instead of striking them. Blondes should avoid the lighter shades of blue, which are apt to give an ashy hue to the complexion. A Turkish turban of the largest size contains from ten to twenty yards of the finest and softest muslin. Of the four thousand two hundred kinds of flowers which grow in Europe, only four hundred and twenty, ro ten per cent., are scented. Running makes a person warm because a greater amount of air is breathed in, causing the blood to pass more rapidly through the lungs. In India and the East Indies the natives mix white ants with flour, and make them into pastry, which is considered to be highly nutritious. It is not generally known that rats cannot resist sun- flower seeds. A trap baited with these seeds is the most efficacious method of catching rats. The average man takes five and a half pounds of food and drink each day, which amounts to a ton of solid and liquid nourishment every year. Norway even now uses corn for coin, while whales' teeth are used by the Fijians, red feathers by some of the South Sea Islanders, and salt in Abyssinia.</p>
        <p>To prevent articles of silverware from tarnishing, first warm them and paint them over with a thin solution of collodion in alcohol, using a wide soft brush for the purpose. Every guest at a Norwegian wedding used to bring the bride a present. In many partE a keg of butter was the usual gift, and if a marriage took pace in the winter, salted or frozen meat was offered.</p>
        <p>-- A MINUTE WITH YOU PLEASE Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back Dropsy, Wind and ;Water Complaint, Diseases of Kidneys Bladder Stone. Sciatica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a po-itive Cure In Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and If not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices Is. 10. if all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps, from HoLnuoru's Medical Hall, Checkheaten, Yorks. Don't be put off, if you cannot get them write to the Proprietor and a box will be seat next post. —Advt.</p>
        <p>" Made in America," and none the less entertaining on that occasion. Sixty-two years ago a young German couple pledged their troth, the girl.however. emigrating to the Statas shortly afterwards. Christian Schultz thus lost sight of his old flame until quite recently, when he, in turn, crossed the Atlantic, found her, and married her. He is now eighty-two, and she eighty. Surely some kind deity should act the Jupiter to their Philemon and Baucis. Eleliogabalus divorced his wife because he dis- coversd a mole upon her body. A Hindu,according to a writer in the " Green Bag," possessed a somewhat simi- , lar right. If he discovered any natural defect upon his ' wife immediately after the marriage the Indian law enabled him to leave her, and the father of the woraan was fined for his deceit in not making the blemish known. If no children were born, a wife could be re- placed by another in the eighth year ; if none of the children lived, the husband could take another wife ia the tenth year ;if daughters only were born, he was free to marry in the eleventh year. A piece of news comes from Alabama. An enterprising book agent went to that State from Connecticut, and soon found means of utilising the negro's enthusiasm in religion to his own gain. Finding in all the illustrated Bibles that the pictures of the angels were in white, he conceived the idea of having a Bible in which all the illustrations should present the angelic host as negroes. Latest reports say that he is selling the Bibles as fast as he can get them delivered. It is often said that " the Devil is not as black as he is painted," but it now seems that the opposite applies to the angels.</p>
        <p>Early Rising Causes Insanity. In some recent paragraphs we, says "Science Sift- ings," pointed out that early rising is not the blessing we have been popularly taught to believe. Dr. Seldon H. Tolcott, a specialist in mental diseases, who hails from Middleton, New York, goes even further than we did. He calls attention to the relative frequency with which farmers and their families become insane, and he believes that this insanity is mostly due to the inhuman hours at which they are in the habit of getting up. Pos- sibly some may be disposed to think that early rising is rather a prima facie evidence of insanity, than a cause of it, but, without going so far as this,we take it that at the most alienists will agiee that the foolish feeling of superior virtue which the habit of early rising is apt to engender is akin to the exalted mental state which often precedes what Auguste Comte (speaking of himself) called a crise ceribrale. Many psychologists have a true collector's enthusiasm for new species, and the " British Medical Journal" hopes that what we venture to call " rnatutinal mania" may find a place in the next classification of mental diseases that may be proposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAREST NELLIE.,</p>
        <p>There seems just a little verging of taste towards the saathetic style of gown for evening wear, though not exactly for ball dresses. it is probably a reaction from the stiff skirts of outdoor dresses that look almost as if they were capable of standing and walking alone. I saw, for instance, a very quaintly graceful gown of soft green velvet or the humbler velveteen which is now quite on a par with velvet as to finish and colouring. The skirt open in front over a ceinture of sof; cream silk printed all over with tiny sprays of green and amber flowers, and the sleeves, fichu, and sash are also of this silk. In makes a kind of picture dress. Then there is another in similar style, made of creamy satin veiled with cream lace, the design of the lace being out- lined with sequins. There is a corselet and revers of rich golden-brown velvet, the corselet edged with gold embroidery on a cream ground, more of the sequin outlined lace falling from under the revers and ...Team satin sleeves without any lace, but dotted all over with gold sequins.</p>
        <p>The following neat costume, though avowedly atended for a skating dress, is admirable for ordinary outdoor wear, as the skirt is well-shaped and clears the ground frankly,. It can be made of five yards of cheviot serge, fifty-two inches wide, and a stylish blue-grey tint is the newest and pret- tiest. The smart little coat has revers faced with blueorey bengaline, fastened back with medium- sized steel buttons, and the waistcoat, which, for a chilly mortal, should be made of lining flannelette, with close-fitting long sleeves, has a plain vest of the fancy " Trilby " velvet. The skirt and coat are edged with very narrow dark fur, or black Thibet fleece; or the effect will be quite nice if the skirt is finished with blue-grey Amazon binding, and the coat with two rows of machine-stitching. I must try to describe to you a beautiful winter gown I saw the other day :—The hue is true bishop's purple, the cloth of quite a satiny surface. The skirt is severely plain, and the bodice slightly</p>
        <p>so. The revers, neckband, yoke, ceniture, and the lower parts of the sleeves are of rich purple velvet, edged and relieved with chinchilla fur in bands about an inch and a half wide.</p>
        <p>A handsome bonnet for an elderly lady is of petunia, with folds and bows across the front, secured by Rhine stone or paste buckles. Similar bows are at the back, with sprays of fine old lace, mounted in upstanding loops, on one side of the full velvet crown, mixed with ostrich tips, these and the rather broad velvet strings being black. Another black velvet bonnet has the centre of the crown of orange velvet, loops of orange velvet mixed with the black ostrich feathers on the left side, and square fan-shaped bows in front, secured in place with diamond crescents. It is rumoured, however, that hats are going to drop out of fashion to a certain extent, and will be succeeded by rather large bonnets. It is quite certain that ladies who chiefly wear bonnets have complained for months that milliners paid no attention to them, and it is equally certain that the bonnets with " widespread ' trimmings almost over the ears have proved becoming to very few faces. A small bonnet looks very smart for a dressy occasion, and if ladies will only insist on having them, demand is sure to create supply. We have all succumbed to the mania now, so I</p>
        <p>JENNIIL</p>
        <p>send you a description of the Trilby hat. It is of grey felt encircled with a ribbon of shut iniroir velvet, finished off with a bow secured with a buckle in strass. There is a cluster of grey and lirown ostrich tips, relieved wiAli red ospreys. Your loving</p>
