      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>- - -- The February Drawing Room will not take place owing to the death of Prince Henry of Battenberg and there is very little doubt that this sad event will seriously affect the Lcndon season. Mourning, despite the fact that no command or even request en the. part of her Majesty has been issued to the general publicos much en evidence, and the fashionable world presents at the present moment a decidedly sombre atpect. Two or three yeats ago crape was almost entirely.' discarded, but the sym- bolical fabric is once again enjoying considerable favour. Chiffon, however, is recognised as a correct deep mourn- ing fabric,as is also the beautiful craps-like silken gauze so much employed for evening gowns. Sable may ac- company the very deepest mourning, but caracule, astrachan, and broadtail all appear more in keeping with the garments of woe. Caracule skirts are putting in an appearance again, accompanied by tight-fitting bodices of velvet. I would, however, advise my readers not to indulge in this parti- cular mode for owing to the near approach of spring it can be of short duration only. Apropos of sable, when</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>REASONABLE EXCUSE FOR NON-VACCINATION.</head>
        <p>REASONABLE EXCUSE FOR NON-VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>A MOTHER actually stated recently that she objected to having her son taught to read, because, she eaid, "If he learns to read he may read bad books and so become a bad bay." Her objection was over-ruled, and the boy was sent to school. A listener in the Justice's court to which the mother had been brought said to her as she was leaving. " I suppose you would object to his being vaccinated. too ?" To the surprise of the questioner and those about, she answered," No, I would not ; I'm not afraid of vaccination, because I have a medicine to give my children nnw when they are vaccinated." An intelligent-looking, man standing near asked, " What is it you give P Is it Vaccination Virus Vanquisher ?" "No," she answered." it's V.V.V." The poor uneducated ' woman had naturally learned the shortest name for this medicine, and did not even know the longer one, nor the meaning of the letters V.V.V. She knew from ex- perience that V.V.V. removed all the dangers of vaccina- tion, and that was enough to keep her from making an excuse for non-vaccination. Her more intelligent ques- tioner knew all about it. arid explained to those who, knowing nothing of the medicine, were inclined to laugh at the poor earnest woman that Vaccination Virus Van- quisher. or V.V.V., was a great medical discovery, and that it was sadly needed in a country where vaccination was pn.formed upon more than one million persons each year. " Its action," he continued " is as follows : I3elne a powerful antiseptic, i.e., powerful in killing certain germs,though harmless to the child,it destroys the germs of the diseases of vaccination that are carried from one arm to arm with the vaccine lymph, though it does not injure the lymph itself. Thus it actually makes all kinds of lymph act as though they were pure and free from the noxious germs found in nine out of every ten samples examined. It also lessens the ordinary fever of pure vaccination by rapidly carrying away the inflam- matory products which always follow the operation." The Court-room closed, and many who stopped to laugh at the ignorance of the poor mother went away feeling that through her they had learned of a remedy of the greatest value te themselves and their relations and friends. We may add that it can be obtained from all Chemists at 24. 91. per brittle.er from the Agents,Mes4rs. i Barclay and Sons, Limited, 95, FArringdon-street, Lin- don ; or from Vaccination Virus Vanquisher Company, Limiteo, 31. Snow Hill, London, E.C., from whom also a free pamphlet may be obtained by all who apply.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>UNITED STATES AND TEE ARMENIAN REIGN OF TERROR.</head>
        <p>THE UNITED STATES AND TEE ARMENIAN REIGN OF TERROR.</p>
        <p>(FROM THE" WESTMINSTER GAZETTE.") The most remarkable expression of American senti- ment on this matter is to be found in the report of the great mass meeting held in Chicago, under the presid- ency of the Mayor of that city, on January 5. On the morning of that day the " Times-Herald," one nf the most typical and influential newpapers in the States, published a long article from its well-informed Washinn- ton c.)rrepondent announcing that President Cleveland was thinking of intervening by force of arms in Turkey. The article has as a conspicuous scare-head, " We May Fight Turkey." followed by a second title, " Big Fleet of Warships to Bring Abdul Hamid to Time," which is followed after five other equally startling headlines by , the following significant line, " Waiting only for a Word from Minister Terrell to Force the Dardanelles." The correspondent wrote :—"lf the North Atlantic Squadron should be sent to Stnyrna to join Admiral Selfridge, the latter would have a fleet so powerful that the Turkish warshlas would undoubtedl7 remain on the farther side to the Dardanelles rather than try to interfere with him. The following are the ships : The Maine, of 6,680 tons, four 10-inch and six 6-inch guns. 12-inch armour, speed 17 knots ; the New York. of 8,150 tons, six 8-inch and twelve 4-inch guns, 10-inch and 4-inch armour. speed 21 kno,s ; the Columbia and the Minneapolis. 7,350 tons each,one 8-inch, two 8-inch, and eight 4-inch guns each. speed more than 22 knots ; the San Francisco of 4,080 tons, twelve 6-inch guns, speed 20 knots ; the Raleigh and the Cincinnati of 3,180 tons, one 6-inch and ten 5- inch nuns each, speed 19 knots ; the Marblehead and the Mcntgomery, of 2,000 tons each. nine 5-inch guns each, speed 19 knots. As all the United States ships are armed with the latest style of high-power guns, the 4-inch and 5-inch being rapid firers, and as they are in a state of thorough discipline,it is wholly probable that the Turkish navy would regard as a very unsafe anchorage the har- bour of Smyrna or any other port where our fleet might happen to assemble." The mass meeting held that evening was passionately ' enthusiastic in its welcome of this intelligence. Mr. Adarres,an ex-Congressman,was one of the first speakers. He said :—" The policy of Europe den-lands to-day the maintenance of the Ottoman Empire. and is responsible for the atrocites perpetrated in Armenia, as it was; for the Bulgarian horrors that made Gladstone tremble, as he remembered that God is just, and for the massacre of Selo, that stirred the indignant eloquence of Daniel Webster. The Sultan of Turkey:represents Russian,jeol- ously of England, and English jealousy of Ressia. With- out these his blood-stained throne would crumble in an instant. Without these the so-called Ottoman Emnire would cease to pollute the pages of history. This morning's Times-Herald ' makes the statement that the American Navy is to make a great display and force the Dardanelles. -(Anplause.) If this statement is true, and it ought to be, the Government is about to take a I splendid part in a good cause." I The Rev. Dr. Gray, one of the most influential Meth o- nist ministers in Chicago, made a direct appeal for Anglo-American co-operation :—" I ask you," he said,to " extend a friendly and brotherly hand even to England and say to her that America is ready to join hands with her to do all that peace-lcving nemle may do to rescue our Christian brethren of Armenia from utter extermin- ation. Let us ask our kinsfofk across the sea to join with Us in doing all that may be done for the relief of the Armenian, whom the murderous, treacherous, and lying Turk has left bleeding on the highway of nations." General Howard, who followed, lost his right arm while fighting for the Union in the great Civil War, but neither his age nor the loss ot his arm had abated his fiery enthusiasm. He declared,amid the long-continued applause of the excited audience, that if he followed his natural inclination he would like to lead a column of men straight through Asiatic Turkey, and with 80.000 :oldiers he would be afraid of nobody. But General Howard believed that by the co-operation of England. America, and Russia it would be possible to effect the deliverance of Armenia without war. (No American,be it noted, ever leaves Russia cut. Their appeals are always to England and Russia). General Howard de- clared—" That is better than to fly at each other's throats. If England will join us in this, it will be the morning star of a new era in the history of the Anglo- Saxon nations, and in the history, of men. Who knows, Mr. President, who knews that this offer, originating in this city, of union with the people of England in a work of mercy which appeals to every British, as it does to every American heart, may not he the first colour in the East of a new and glorious day ?" The speech which was received with the most over- whelming enthusiasm was the eloquent and passionate appeal of the Rev. Dr. Henson to Russia and England "0 Russia, bath thy bear no claws? 0 England, hath thy lion no teeth, or is there nothing but gold that can make him roar? Oh, for an Oliver Cromwell in London and an Admiral Blake upon the Bosphorus, and, as sure as God lives, the Moslem sword would soon cease to drink the blood of Christians in Asia Minor 0 land of Hampden and Howard and Milton, wake thee from thy slumbers, and hasten to rescue thy poor brothers who are stretching out imploring hands to one who has power to save!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>HIDDEN HAND;</head>
        <p>THE HIDDEN HAND;</p>
        <p>CAPITOLA THE MADCAP. I3Y Mi. E. D. E. N. SOUTHWORT H.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XX ll.—(Con!inued). As the troop of miscellaneou.dy-armed negroes running down the hill were still making eve " hideous" with yells of "Black Donald:" "Black Donald 1" and C ipitola still clinging and hanging on at the back of his neck continued to cry: "I've caught him! Lye caught him! help! help!" some- thing like the truth flashed in a blinding way upon Old Hurricane's perceptions. Roaring forth something between a recognition and defiance, the old man threw up his fat arms, and as fast as age and obesity would permit, ran up the bill to intercept the outlaw. There was no time for trilling now I The army of negroes was at his heels; the old veteran in his path; the girl clinging a dead weight to his jacket behind. An idea suddenly struck him which he wondered had not done so before—quickly un- buttoning and throwing off his garment he dropped both captor and jacket behind him on the ground. And before Capitola had picked her self up. Black Donald, bending his huge head and shoulders for- ward and mak.ng a battering-ram of himself, ran with all his force and butted Old Herricane in the Stomach, pitched him into the horse-pond, leaped over the park fence and disappeared in the forest. What a scene! what a row followed the escape and flight of the famous outlaw 1 Who could imagine, far less describe it I—a general tempest in which every individual was a particular storm. There stood the baffled Capitols', extricating her head from the pea-jacket, and with her eyes fairly flashing out sparks of anger, exclaiming: "Oh, wretches! wretches that you are if you'd been worth salt you could have caught him while I clung to him so 1" There wallowed Old Hurricane, spluttering, floundering, half-drowning, in the horse-pond, making the most frantic efforts to curse and swear as he struggled to get out. There stood the crowd of negroes brought to a midden stand by a panic of horror at seeing the dignity of their master so outraged. And most frenzied of all, there ran Wool around and around the margin of the pond, in a state of violent perplexity how to get his master out without half-drowning himself. " Biurr-urr-rr 1 flitch! filch! Bitirr-ur ! splut- tered and sneezed and strangled Old Hurricane, as he floundered to the edge of the pond. "Blurr-urr- rr ! Help me out, you scoundrel! I'll break every bone in your—flitch I—body! Do you hear me— ea-arlish I—villain you! flitch flitch! ca-snish 1 eh-h!" Wool, with his eyes starting from his head, and his hair standing up with horrors of all sorts, plunged at last into the water and pulled his old master up upon his feet. Ca-sniih 1 ca-snish I biurr-rr filch !—what are you gapiiik there for as if you'd raised the devil, you crowd of born fools 1" howled Old Hurricane, as soon as he could get the water out of his mouth and nose—" what ave you standing there for ? after him after him, I say! Scour the woods in every direction! His freedom to any man who brings me Black Donald, dead or alive !—Wool!" "Yes, sir," said that functionary who was busying himself with squeezing the water out of his master's garments. "Wool, let me alone! take the fleetest horse in the it ble 1 ride for your life to the Court House I Tell Keepe to have new bills posted everywhere, offering an additional five hundred dollars for the apprehension of that—that—that "—for the want of a word strong enough to express himself, Old Hurricane suddenly stopped, and for lack of his stick to make silence emphatic, he seized his grey hair with both hands and groaned aloud. Wool waited no second bidding, but flew to do his errand. Capitola came to the old man's side, saying: "Uncle, hadn't you better hurry home—you'll take cold." "Cold I—Cold demmy 1 I never was so hot in my life 1" cried the old man; "b'.- Jemmy! you're right! and run to the house, Ca:1,12, ;a, and tell Mrs. Condiment to have me a full of dry clothes before the fire in my chamber. Go, child! every man-jack is off after Black Donald, and there is nobody but you, and Condiment, and the house- maids to take care of me. Stop, look for my stick first; where did that black demon throw it 2 demmy I I'd as well be without my legs!" Capitola picked up the old man's cane and hat, and put the one on his head and the other in his hand, and then hastened to find Mrs. Condiment, and tell her to prepare to receive her half-drowned patron. She found the old lady scarcely recovered from the effects of her recent fright, hat ready on the instant to make every effort on behalf of Old Hurricane, who presently arrived dripping wet at the house. Leaving the old gentleman to the care of his housekeeper, we must follow Black Donald. Hatless and coatless, with his long black hair and beard blown by the wind, the outlaw made tracks for his retreat—occasionally stopping to turn and get breath, and send a shout of laughter at his baffled pursuers. '1 he same night, at the usual hour, the gang met at their rendezvous, the deserted inn, beside the old road through the forest. They were in the midst of their orgies around the supper-table, when the well-known ringing step of the leader sounded under the back windows without, the door was burst open, and the captain, hatless, coatless, with his dark elf locks flying, and every sign of haste and disorder, rushed into the room. He was met by a general rising outcry:—"Hi! hillo 1 what's up 'V' exclaimed every man, starting to his feet and laying hands upon secret arms, pre- pared for instant resistance. For a moment Black Donald stood with his leonine head turned and looking back over his stalwart shoulders, as if in expectation of pursuit, and then, with a loud laugh, tuned to his men, exclaiming: "Ho! you thought me followed! so I have been but not as close as hound to heel "In fact, captain, you look as if you'd but escaped with your skin this time 1" said Hall. "Faith! the captain looks well peeled I" said</p>
        <p>Stephen. "Worse than that, boys worse than that I Your chief has not only lest his pack. his hat and his coat, but—his heart! Not only are the outworks battered, but the citadel itself is taken! Not only has he been captured, but captivated! and all by a little minx of a girl I—Boys. your chief is in love 1" exclaimed Biack Donald, throwing himself into his seat at the head of the table, and quaffing off a large draught of ale. " Hip hip! hurraw 1 three times three for the captain's love !" cried Hal, rising to propose the toast, which was honoured with enthusiasm. "Now tell us all about it, Captain. Who is she? where did you see her? is she fair or dark? tall or short; thin or plump; what's her name, and is she kind 2" asked Hal. "First guess where I have teen to-day. "You and your demon only know I" "I guess they also know at Hurileane Hall, for it Is there I have Leen!" Well, then, why didn't you go to perdition at once ?" exclaimed Hal, in a consternation that was reflected in every countenance present. "Why, because when I go there I intend to take you all with me and remain 1' answered Black Donald. "Tell us about the visit to Hurricane Hall," said Hal. Whereupon Black Donald commenced, and con- cealiqg only the motive of his visit, gave his comrades a very graphic, spicy and highly coloured narrative of his adventure at Hurricane Hall, and particularly of his "passages at arms" with the little witch. Capitols, whom he described as: "Such a girl I slender, petite, lithe, with bright, black ringlets dancing around a little face full of fun, frolic, mischief and spirit, and eyes quick and vivacious as those of a monkey, darting hither and thither from object to object." "The captain is in love, sure enough," said</p>
        <p>Steve. - "Bravo I here's success to the captain's love ! She's a brick I" shouted the men. "Oh, she is," assented their chief, with enthu.</p>
        <p>slam. "Long live to her three times three for the pretty witch of Hurricane Hall 1 " roared the men, rising to their feet and raising their full mugs high In the air, before pledging the toast. "That is all very well, boys; but I want more substantial compliments than words—Boys! I must have that girl ! ' "Who doubts it, captain I—of course you will take her at once if you want her," said Hal, con- fidently. "But, I must have help in taking her." "Captain, I volunteer for one 1" exclaimed Hal. "And I, for another," added Steve. "And you, Dick ? " inquired the leader, turning towards the sullen man, whose greater atrocity had gained for him the name of Demon Dick. "What is the use of vclluuteering when ,the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>captain has only to command," said thls indiviatutc; sulkily. "Ah ! when the enterprise is simply the robbing of a mail-coach, in which you all have equal Interest, then, indeed, your captain has only to command, and you to obey; but this is a more delicate matter of entering a lady's chamber and carrying her off for the captain's arms, and so should only be entrusted to those whose feelings of , devotion to the captain's person prompt them to - volunteer for the service," said Black Donald. "How elegantly our captain speaks! he ought to be a lawyer," said Steve. "The Captain knows I'm with him for everything." said Dick, sulkily. "Very well, then! for a personal service like this, a delicate service requiring devotion, I should scorn to give commands I I thank you far your offered assistance, my friends, and shall count on you three, Hal, Stephen and Richard, for the enterprise," said the captain. "Ay! ay 1 ay!" said the three men in a breath. "For the time and place and manner of the seizure of the girl, we must reflect. Let us see i there is to be a fair in the village next week, during the session of the court. Old Hurricane will be at court as usual. And for one day, at least, his servants will have a holiday to go to the fair. They I will not get home until the next morning. The house will be ill-guarded. We must find out the particular day and night when this shall be so. ° Then you three shall watch your opportunity, enter the house by stealth, conceal yourselves in ths chamber of the girl, and at midnight, when all is quiet, gag her and bring her away." "Excellent!" said Hal. "And mind, no liberty except the simple act of carrying her off is to be taken with your captain's prize," said the leader, with a threatening glare of his lion-like eye. "Oh, no! no! not for the world! She shall be as sacred from insult as though she were an angel and we saints," said Hal, both the others assenting. "And now not a word more. We will arrange the further details of this business hereafter," said the captain, as a peculiar signal was given at the door. Waving his hand for the men to keep their places, Black Donald went out and opened the back passage door, admitting Col. Le Noir. "Well," said the latter, anxiously. "Well, sir, I have contrived to see her; come Into the front room and I will tell you all about it." said the outlaw, leading the way into the old parlour that had been the scene of so many of their conspiracies. "Does Capitola Le Noir still live 1" hoarsely demanded the colonel, as the two conspirators reached the parlour. "Still live? yes; 'twas but yesterday we agreed upon her death. Give a man time. Sit down, Colonel; take this seat we will talk the matter over again." With something very like a sigh of relief, Colonel Le Noir threw himself into the offered chair. Black Donald drew another chair up and 00 down beside his patron. "Well, Colonel, I have contrived to see the girl as I told you," he began. "But you have not done the deed; when will it be done?" "Colonel, my patron, be patient. Within twelve , days I shall claim the last instalment of the ten thousand dollars agreed upon between us for this</p>
        <p>rob." "But why so long? since it is to be done, why _</p>
        <p>not have it over ai once r said Colonel Le Noir. starting up and pacing the floor impatiently. "Patience, my Colonel. The cat may play with the mouse most delightfully before devouring it." "What do you mean 2" "My Colonel, I have seen the girl under circum- stances that has fired my heart with an 'moon' trollable desire for her—" "Ha-ha-ha 1" scornfully laughed the coloneL "Black Donald, the mail-robber, burglar, outlay,. the subject of the grand passion 1" "Why not, my Colonel. Listen, you shall hear. and then you shall judge whether or not you your- self might not have been fired by the fascination of such a witch 1" said the outlaw, who straightwaY commenced and gave his patron the same account of his visit to Hurricane Hall that he had already' related to his comrades. The colonel heard the story with many a "04." " tush " and " pshaw," and when the man had con - eluded the tale, he exclaimed: "Is that all 2 Then we may continue our negotiations—l care not. Carry her off! marry' her! do as you please with her! only at the end a all—kill her! "hoarsely whispered Le Noir. This is just what I intend, Colonel." "That will do if the event be certain ; but it niel he certain. I cannot breathe freely while brother's heiress lives!" whispered Le Noir. " Well, Colonel, be content; here is my hand upon it. In six da .s C tpitola will be in my poWer. In twelve days you shall be out of hers." " It is a bargain," s lid each of the conspirators I° a breath, as they shook hands and parted—Le Noir to his home and Black Donald to join his comradd revelry.</p>
        <p>( To be continued.)</p>
