      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WHIT THE MILKMAN SAID.</head>
        <p>WHIT THE MILKMAN SAID.</p>
        <p>AN IDYLL OF EAST LONDON.</p>
        <p>" Can it be the same girl ? "—even the milkman asked the question of a lath' in Silvertown, at the East End of I London, whose daughter in less than a month, had undergone so marked a change in personal appearance as to be barely recognisable. "Yes," said the mother, " this is my daughter. You would not think she had been at death's door, would you ? But it is so. Like most of my children, she was strong enough and healthy up to a year ago, when she began to droop and 101 l about, and couldn't work, and didn't eat, and hadn't any inclination to do anything. Her father and I got into a fine way about it. We had lost one daughter, a fine young woman, and, as I said, seeing this one going the same way, we were dreadfully upset. We sent her to the sea-side, but she came back as bad as she went : the same lassitude, and loth to move. Then she began to get worse, and we had the best doctor we knew of. You can't see anybody die before your eyes without doing something to save them. He came, and came, and kept coming, and she took medicine, but with all his skill and care she did not appear to be any better, and we got tired of it. Amemia, or bloodlessness, he said, was the disorder that was killing her." " And then what did you do ? " "We didn't know what to do. We were ready in our despair to try anything, when I came across a piece of newspaper which described something exactly like my daughter's symptoms aud a cure of the person who was suffering them by Dr. William' Pink Pills for Pale People. So we laid our heads together, and her father said "try it." We did,and you see what she's like now." "She does not look like an invalid now ! " " No, indeed. It is the result of this wonderful medicine."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Peacock (whose address is 4, Randolph-road</p>
        <p>Silvertown), went on to say that a fortnight after com- mencing the new treatment her daughter wac better,and in less than a month everybody was remarking the im- provement, so marvellous was the change. "I only wish I had known of Dr. Williams' Pills when my other daughter was bad," she added," and then we might have saved her." The young man at the drug stores where Mrs. Peacock purchased the Pills said he had served her with several boxes of Pills, and witnessed the gratitude of her heart when she told him of the wonders they were working in her daughter's condition. All he knew about Dr. William's Pills was that they were selling, and that people had a box or two, got better of the ailment for which they took them, and then left off buying, from which he inferred they were cured of their troubles. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills directly enrich and purify the blood, and thus it is that they are so famous for the cure of rheumatism, scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health. They are also a splendid nerve and spinal tonic, and thus have cured many cases of paraly- sis,locomotor ataxy, neuralgia, St. Vitus' dance, and nervous headache. They are now obtainable of all chemists, and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company,4B, Flolbcrn-viaduct, London, at 21. 9d. a box, six for 1.35. 9d., but are genuine only with full name, Dr. Wil- liams' Pink Pills for Pale People. A specific for all the troubles of the female, and in men cure all cases arising from worry, overwork, or excesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>PIGTAILS AND WHAT THEY PORTEND.</head>
        <p>PIGTAILS AND WHAT THEY PORTEND.</p>
        <p>The man who should undertake to write the history of the Chinaman's pigtail would impose upon himself a task not lightly to be estimated. This peculiar and dis- tinguishing trait of the Celestial subjects of the Mongo- lian and Tartar Dynasties is of very great veneration and antiquity, and its origin will probably remain obscure. Still, some facts regarding its institution have been collected, and these will enable Europeans the better to coMprehend the significance that attaches to this hirsute appendage. One ancient Chinese historiae—a priest of some distinction in his own country—records the fact that as early as 604 the practice of shaving the hair and beard existed in some regions, while elsewhere the hair was wore divided into two pendant tails. The writer was evidently a traveller and observerifor he tells us that " there are also countries where all the hair is shaved off except that at the crown which is tied into one cue." This author also mentions that some nations plucked out the hair entirely while others cut it short, and others, again, let the hair flow loose down on the shoulders, some preferring to plait it. In some instances the frant hair is plaited and the back hair left loose. It is difficult to locate with exactness the place he names. for twelve centuries ago the Asiatic peoples were more migratory than at present, and named the lands they lived in from themselves, no matter how often they changed their residence. It is easy to determine that the people who shaved the head and beard were the Buddhist monks and nuns in India, Ceylon, Siam and China ; that the people "with two tails " were the Japanese, Coreans and some of the tribes we now call the "Independent Tartars," and that the race which shaved all but the crown andlied the hair at that spot into one cue were the Manchu Tartars or nomad dwellers 'of Manchuria. The Chinese peoples—for at that time what is now a single empire was a large number of petty warring kingdoms—wore their hair according to their pleasure. The poorer classes kept it cut short, the wealthy allowed it to grow long, and like the Roman no- bility in the reign of Nero, spent extraordinary amounts of time,trouble and money upon its cultivation. A Prince of Shon-si, about 10 A.-D., is described as wearing a crown braided with eighteen plaits of his hair, and ornamented with silver and jewels. A thousand years passed away, and the Tartar custom, which this author described as an outlandish novelty, became the custom of all China. The crown was left, but all the rest of the hair was shaven. This became the national custom at the Manchu conquest. about *.n. 1644. It did not be- come the rule in China to shave off the hair all round the crown from any religiousmotive, but simply by military compulsion. "You have to obey orders," said the con- querors, sword in hand. "if you refuse to shave accord- ing to our Tartar custom you must die, for refusal will constitute you a rebel." So the change was made from the north-east province as the conquest proceeded, till the whole nation had their heads shaved except the crown. And they have kept it np ever since. Of the 200,000.000 male citizens of the Celestial kingdom 190,500,000 sport the cue. There is a belief current that a 'Chinaman who looses his pigtail is not allowed to return to his native land, but that is arrant nonsense. A Chinaman, who cuts off his capillary appendage and appears with short hair in public is viewed exactly as an Eiagliehman would be who presented himself in the streets fully dressed with the omission of trousers ! A cue is wearing apparel ! A Mongolian with scant hair braids in it artificial hair or adds silk threads. A bald man employs a false cue that is fastened to the inside of his cap. A Coolie regards himself as well-attired when he wears bathing drawers and has his pigtail nicely combed and oiled. The Manchus, when they began their wonderful career of conquest in the thirteenth century, did not at first insist upon their conquered foes adopting the ton- sorial style as a mark of subjection. Only when they had overrun so many lands and peoples that it became difficult to distinguished vassal and serf from open foe, did they adopt the present system. The caw was forced upen Kuldja tribes in the fifteenth century, upon the people of Mongolia in the sixteenth, on Shantung at the at the beginning of the seventeenth, and on the re- mainder of China in the next C'ty years. If it be asked why did the Tartars shave, the reply will probably be correct that the custom began in religion, and was con- tinued for cleanliness, for fashion's sake, and for the comfort of the shin. That religion was the originating cause is likely, because in India, with the spread of Bud- dhism, the shaving of the entire head became very comn-on. The Egyptian priests were completely shorn, and from them the habbit of shaving off hair and beard extended to the laity. Only the women, who apparently in all the ancient civilisations were regarded and treated either as property or as domestic animals, always wore their own hair, and they were not shaved even in mourning or after death. Shaving was universal among the men, but the hair and beard were allowed to grow in times of mourning. They wore wigs instead of their natural hair, and they had a wig for the chin which could be put on and taken off at pleasure, like the wigs they wore on the bead. Yet there may be a religious element in shaving the head for the cue. The first shaving of a Chinese baby-boy is as important an event in the East as the christening of a young Calvinist or the first communion of a Catholic. Invitations are sent out to relatives and friends, and a collation provided. The child, washed, perfumed and beautifully dressed, is presented by the father to all present ,who in return make some presents in money to the youthful her. The father, grandfather or a priest then employs the razor, and the youngster is well started for both this werld and the next. In most cases the shaving is preceded by prayer. One thing should be said in favour of the cue. It is an admirable police force. A single policeman can tie a inety isi9zen criminals together and haul them to prison. -nine hundredths prefer incarceration and many stripee to losing their pigtails. This fact may have been recognised by the Manchu conquerors and induced them to adopt their system a a convenience in suppressing •insurrection and preserving law and order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 1 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MEETING OF PARR'S BANKING</head>
        <p>MEETING OF PARR'S BANKING</p>
        <p>PADGATE WESLEYAN SIINDIt' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL TEX MEETING-.</p>
        <p>- - The annual tea meeting in connection with the above Sunday school was held on Thursday last week, when about 120 persons sat down to a most enjoyable repast. The following ladies and gentle- men contributed trays for the occasion :—Mesdames James Bennett, William Bennett, Arthur Bennett, Walter Bennett, Critchley, Dean, Egerton, Evans, Edward Gandy, Harris, John Houghton, Harring- ton, Lunt, Mason, Pearson,Richardson,Royston, William Smith, Strange, Steele, Thornton, W. E. Thornton, W hite, Ward, Young and Yates, and Messrs. Hughes, James and Jackson. After tea, a public meeting was held in the schoolroom, at which there was a crowded attendance. The chair was occupied by the Rev. Owen Watkins. After singing and prayer, the annual report was read by the Secretary, Mr. J. F. White,in which it was stated that another year of quiet steady work had passed. There were 10 male and 15 female teachers, and the scholars numbered 121 girls and 106 boys, total 227 that was an increase upon the nrevious year of 33. When the school was enlarged in 1886, the number on the books was 123, so that the scholars had almost doubled, and there was every prospect of still further increase. The average attendance was 53 boys and 55 girls, total 108, in the morning, and 79 boys and 92 girls, total 171, in the afternoon. This also was a considerable improve- ment upon last year. The large number of the scholars rendered it imperative that more accommodation should be found. On Sunday afternoons after two classes had left the school, one to the chapel and the other te the preacher's vestry, the place was still uncomfortably crowded. At the last teacher's meeting Mr. William Bennett had promised to bring the matter up at the next trustee meeting, and he was hopeful that it would be found practicable to make a considerable enlarge- ment o! the school premises, and it was hoped that taere would be a hearty response. The in- come for the year had been £27 18s. 61, and the expenditure £25 12s. 6d., thus leaving a bala.nce of £2 Bs. Owing to extra expenditure necessary, it was re- gretted that they could not as on several preceeding years,ctrry over £lO for the enlargement fund. The various institutions had been carried on with success during the year,and in the library there were 300 useful books. The following were the officers for the ensuing year :—Superintendents, Mr. F. Wright and Mr. H. Ward ; treasurer, Mr. Harris ; club secretary and librarian, Mr. Ernest Andrews; secretary, Mr. J. F. White; assistant secretary,Mr. G.H. White. During the year the school had been visited by the ministers, local preachers, and othets to whom the officers and teachers offered them their heartiest thanks. The Chairman congratulated Padgate upon the admir- able report just read and also upon its secretary. He was quite sure he was speaking within bounds when he said that the school was one of the most successful in the Warrington circuit. (Applause.) Mr. Watkins then proceeded to enlarge upon the connection between the Sunday school and the church ,aod said that it was ab- solutely necessary for the welfars of the church that the Sunday school should be well supported. A miscellaneous programme was then given in which songs were rendered by several of the scholars,Mr.James Bennett,Mr. P. C. Price,and Miss Kent. Mr. W. Silcock also gave an interesting address,in which he dealt with the Sunday school work and its connection with the church. Mr. William Bennett, in a few words which he spoke, said that the meeting of the trustees would be held at the end of the month, and he would then bring forward the subject of the enlargement of the school. He also promised to give the land himself which was required for the extension, and al.a to help besides, a promise which was loudly applauded.—The meeting was brought to a termination by the usual votes of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>JUBILEE BUILDINGS, HORSEMARKET-</head>
        <p>JUBILEE BUILDINGS, HORSEMARKET-</p>
        <p>STREET.</p>
        <p>THE NEW PREMISES,</p>
        <p>Owing to the fire at Messrs. Skelton's premises I last May, an almost complete re-construction has been necessary, and the proprietors have wisely ! taken the opportunity thus afforded of adding largely to the size of the building. Now that the architectural part of the work is complete there is in area of 25,000 square feet. From front to back the building is 80ft.; frontage, 56ft. ; from the base to the ridge it is 65ft. The ce:lars have been included in the enlargement and are now double the size, partly floored with cement and , quite dry. On the shop floor the departments are ' to be similar to those under the old regim6, viz., general at one side and ironmongery at the other. New offices have been placed at the back of the shop and here as everywhere else space is abundant. They are three in number, one for the managing director, one for the secretary, and a general office. The second floor from the street is also a lofty and spacious room with numerous windows, all working on a centre swivel, which is a great convenience for many things. On this floor are to be stocked the smsllware, fire- grates, mantels and kitchen ranges. For the latter heavy goods, special provision has been made. Passing up the handsome, wide, and substantial stairway, the third floor is reached, and here the hard-ware and hollow-ware is stocked, together with a large quantity of fancy goods. The fourth floor is of similar dimensions but not so lofty. Running from the basement to a turret on the roof (about 75ft.) is a powerful lift capable of carrying several tons of goods. It is access- ible from the back of the premises by carts which can practically unload upon it Among other conveniences,Lamson's Cash Railway Rapid Wire system has been fitted up, and com- municates with all departments on the shop floor. For heating purposes the buildings are fitted with the high-pressure system. which works admirably. The. shop-floor windows are to be illuniinated with the patent regenerating lamps, while the swivel windows are fitted each with six jets, calculated to provide plenty of illum- ination. Mr. C. W. Devonport, of Stockton Heath is the builder and Mr. J. E.Wright,the architect,and the work of both these gentlemen has given every satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>HOSPITAL SUNDAY AT WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>HOSPITAL SUNDAY AT WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the committee was held at the Infirmary on Friday. The chair was occupied by the Rev. Canon Willis, and there were also present the Revs. E. C. E. Carleton, J. J. Langham. W. H. Drummond, G. D. Mason. H. V. Pic,ot, E. T. Reed, J. Dav es, Owen Watkins ; Mr. John Fairclough,J.P., Mr.A.J.Jolley (hon. secretary), Dr. Bowden (surgeon). SECRETARY'S REPORT. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY read the following report :—I have to report that five places of worship which contributed to the Hospital Sunday Fund in 1894. have failed to do so in 1895. On the other hand, five places of worship have during the past year either commenced or renewed their offertories. A comparison of the two groups shows a net gain of £7 163. 3d. The total amount received has been £224 14a. 6d., an increase over the previous year's receipts of £2l 83. Bd. The expenses have been, for the advertiaing'£9 Bs. 4d.sfor stationery and pnstages £2195., total £l2 7s. 4d., compared with £l6 103., in 1894. The Rev. W. H. Drummond asked whether it was cus tomary to communicate with the places of worship which failed to send in contributions. It might be the fault sometimes of some of the officials of the church that notice was not taken of the collection.—Mr. Jolley said that he generally did ao.—The Rev. H. V. Pigot in moving the adoption of the report said that he thought that the fact that the total amount of the offer- tory showed an increase of more than £2o,which showed that Mr. Jolley had not neglected his work during the past year.—The Rev. W. H. Drummond seconded the motion,and hoped that they would increase still more. He was sorry that the collection at his place had fallen, hut he would do his best to increase it this year.—lhe Rev. Owen Watkins supported the motion which was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF SECRETARY. On the motion of the Rev. E. C. E. Carletnn, seconded by the Rev. J. Davies, Mr. A. J. Jolley was re-elected hon. secretary of the committee. DATE OF HOSPITAL SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>Considerable discussion arose upon the date suitable Lot. ficspital Sunday, and the Rev. W. H. Drummond moved, and the Rev. J. Davies seconded, that it be held on March 22nd.—The Rev. H. V. Pivot moved as an amendment, and the Rev. Owen Watkins seconded, that it be held on March 15. The amendment was carried. The Rev. J. Davies thought that to give the various places of worship in the town an opportunity of knowing in time beforehand of the date upon which Hospital Sunday would fall, and thus enabling them to arrange their engagements so that they would not clash with the dsx, it would be advisable to fix a certain Sunday each year which shoul4 be called Hospital Sunday.—Various dates were mentioned as being favourable, but it was finally decided that the annual meeting be held early in December, and thus give sufficient time to the various denominations to make their arrangements in accor- dance. The meeting closed with a vote of thanks to the Chair- man and to the Secretary.</p>
        <p>Two men were 'killed and five injured by an explosioni of petroleum on board the Liverpool barque British Army at the Barry Dock, Cardiff, on Tuesday. The annual meeting of the Lancashire County Cricket Club was held on Tuesday in Manchester. Mr. A. N. flornby was re-elected president of the Club. The report made by the Committee with regard to the past season was highly satisfactory. The number of people who attended the different matches was 148,228, .n increase of about 51,000 on the previous season. The statement or sennunts showed a surplus balance on the season's ; of over £2,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>COMPANY AND THE ALLIANCE BANK.</head>
        <p>COMPANY AND THE ALLIANCE BANK.</p>
        <p>LORD PLAYFAIR AND THE SITUATION ABROAD. The thirty-first annual meeting of Parr's Banking Company and the Alliance Bank, Limited, was held on Thursday last week, at Cannon-st. Hotel, London. Lord Playfair, the chairman, who presided, observed that there were various favourable features in regard to the transactions of the bank during the last year. The two banks were united in 1892,and the first report after the union showed that the money lodged by the public amounted to £11,524,643. During the three and a half years that had followed that amount had been Increased to £16,895,895. The increased confidence shown by the public during that period was thus represented ty a sum of £5,371,252. (Cheers.) During 1895 the increase in the amount lodged was £3,800,000, and although they could not expect a normal increase at this rate, they hoped to maintain the great bulk of the accounts that had been opened. The increase in the amount of money lodged during the last six months was a little over £2,000,000. Of this sum they used for cash, money at call, and money upon very short notice, £1,400,000. They put in first-class investments £275,000, and they used for the usual work of the bank, advances to cus- tomers, loans and discounts, £466,000. Although they had a larger amount of money to deal with than before, they were still subject to the disadvantage that the rates of the Bank of England for discount continued low throughout the year. They had, however, been able to earn a dividend of 19 per cent. This absorbed £95,000; and of the balance they proposed to carry I forward £31,046, and to add £lO,OOO to the ' reserve fund, which now amounted to £910,000. (Hear, hear.) As a politician who had been accustomed to watch trade very closely, they mi4ht like him to say something with regard to their future prosperity. (Hear, hear.) Last year trade was showing excellent signs of recuperation. The amounts of the imports had increased by eight and three-quarter million sterling, and the amount of the export by twelve and a third million sterling. That was very satisfactory, I lconsidering' that prices were still very low, and that consequently a greater velum° of commodities must be imported and exported in order to make up these amounts. But towards the end of the year black clouds rolled over us from different parts of the world, and as traders were a very timid and cautious class of people, they had great difficulty in knowing how to run their trade during the coming year. Confidence was rudely shaken in various directions. They knew of the unhappy state of the Transvaal, they knew of our strained relations with the United States, and they knew of the unpleasant words that came from a neighbouring nation. In addi- tion to that there was the industrial war at home. These unhappy strikes were going on in Glasgow and Belfast just it a time when every shipbuilder, whether he was a master or a man, should have been at the service of 'lir country in extending her commerce and increasing hes defences. Unfortunately, that industrial war was not y( t ended. Then there was great uneasiness with regard to the state of the currency in the United States. All these causes looked as if we were to commence the year with a diminishing instead of an increasing trade. Luckily the international diffi• culties were disappearing very consideratly. I,,te had escaped a great danger in the Transvaal—a danger both to England and to the Transvaal itself. The acute stage of our differences there was now passed, and for that they had to thank the extremely skilful management of affairs by the present Colonial Secretary. (Cheers.) Then with regard to the United States perhaps he might claim some little knowledge with regard to the feeling ie that country, because he was very intimately connect with it, and went there every year. The feeling in the United States was certainly very greatly improving. The educated classes in the United States, with very few exceptions, were strongly against war .with England, or the maintaining of any hostile attitude towards England. But there were a !lumber of persons in the United States who were Jingoes, and they were extremely keen in their views. Take as an illustration a book which has probably a greater circulation in the United States than any book ever had—except the Bible he hoped. Be referred to Coyn's " Financial School." That book had a chapter expressly advocating war between England and the United States as a means of promoting the prosperity of both countries—(laughter) —or at all events the prosperity of the United States. And there was a great deal of that uneducated feeling. But it was passing away very largely, and the two Governments were showing an anxious desire to find some means of removing their differences,with regard to the Venezuela boundary—which, after all, was a very contemptible matter on which two great nations of kindred race and with so much interest in common should differ—and also of removing the impression. which was the basis of all the difficulties which arose between the United States and this country, that we had any desire:to add one inch of soil in the other hemisphere to the territories of this nation. (Hear, hear). Indeed, we were the original authors of the Monroe doctrine. Canning was believed to have suggested it to Monroe as a means of escaping great difficulties at that time, and no English politician of the slightest mark had since then shown the slightest desire to acquire new territory in the other hemisphere. Therefore, when the United States people became convinced of that, the main diffi- culty which he had with them now, and which was continually cropping up, would be removed. He had coefidence that the Govern- ment would succeed in establishing a fair means of arbitration between Great Britain and Vene- zuela, which would remove the last difficulty, and bring U 9 again into that entente cordials with the United States which in sentiment was so desirable, and which in the business relations of the two peoples wasquite indispens- able. (Cheers.) Lord Playfair concluded by moving the adoption of the report, and the motion was seconded by Sir Horace Farquhar, M.P., who observed that with the strong Government now in power this country might hope to overcome her difficulties without any chance of the horrors of war. The motion was carried unanimously. The retiring directors, Sir Thomas Frost,'Mr. P. Stubs, Colonel Gamble, C. 8., Mr. John Dun, Mr. E. W. Nix, and Mr. E. Banbury—were re-elected,and the board was heartily thanked for its services. On the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Colonel Gamble, C. 8., a vote of thanks was accorded to the manager, secretary, and the officers, and Mr. Dun and Mr. Yates responded. A vote of thanks to Lord Playfair for presiding brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>The chess tournament of St. Petersburg has resulted in the victory of Dr. Lasker. The act of dying, it is now ascertained, is absolutely free from suffering ; it is really unconscious, insenst- bility always preceding it. A recent writer says that many young English barris- ters do not make fifty pounds a year. Only a few of the oldest and best known members of the Bar earn very large sums of money. A Belfast correspendent says that the last difficulty in connection with the shipbuilding dispute was satisfac- torily surmounted on Tuesday, and all the men returned to work on Wednesday. The obstacle was the exclusion of the brassfounders from the Glasgow terms, but they have now been promised a shilling per week increase.</p>
        <p>THE TESTIMONIALS</p>
        <p>Published on behalf of Hood's Sarsaparilla. are as reliable and as worthy your confidence as if they came from your most trusted neighbour. They will stand the closest investigation. They art not purchased, nor are they from our employ6s, nor are they written up in our office. They are reliable statements from living pecple, and they prove be- yond a question the</p>
        <p>POSITIVE MERU</p>
        <p>of this great medicine. Try it. "For some time my little boy, three years old, had been troubled with eczema. Since taking Rood's Sarsaparilla he has improved wonderfully. The complaint affected his eyes severely, but now they are entirely well. He has also gained a splendid appetite. I trust this will encourage others suffer- ing similarly to give Hood's Sarsaparilla a trial." Mns. ANNIE ELLIMAN, 14, Bunhill-road, Beckenham, S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>A. BLOOD CLEANSER.</head>
        <p>A. BLOOD CLEANSER.</p>
        <p>"Messrs. C. I. Hood &amp; Co., "34, Snow Hill, London, E.G. : 1. I bought a bottle of Hood's Sarsaparilla for my son. He had pimples on his neck and body, and lam pleased to inf,arm you that one bottle has taken them all away. He seems all right now, and if at any time he or any of my family requires anything for cleansing the blood, I shall send for some more of Hood's Sarsaparilla at once. I can confidently recommend Hood's Sarsaparilla as a blood cleanser." PETER LOCKWOOD, Market-street, Paddock, Huddersfield.</p>
