      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL TEA MEETING. The twenty-fifth annual tea meeting in connec- tion with the above Mission was held on Monday</p>
        <p>evening, when about 130 persons sat down to tea. The following ladies presided at the tables :—Mrs. T. Dale, Mrs. Ashley, Mrs. Joseph Monks, jun., Mrs. Walters, Mrs. Hough, Mrs. Taylor, Mrs. George Has- sall, Mrs. Edmunds, Miss Woodward, Miss Ashley, Mrs. David Hill, Mrs. John Unsworth. After the tables had been cleared, a public meeting was held in the schoolroom at which there was a very large attendance, and the chair was occupied by the patron, Mr. John Croslield, J.P. After singing and prayer by Mr. Pawson, the Chairman called upon Mr. Ashley to read the annual report of the Sunday</p>
        <p>school. - Mr. T. ASHLEY said that the work during the past year had been of a substantial character. The membership had increased and the services at the chapel and the various organisations were very well sustained, with the result that much good had been accomplished. The year 1895 had beec marked by united feeling throughout and things had run very smoothly. They deplored the loss of one of the members of the committee of management when on December 22nd, Samuel Oxley passed peacefully away to another world. With regard to finance, they commenced the year with £2 us., and they decided to renovate the chapel at the cost of £35 9s. sd. This, with the othet expenditure, caused an outlay of £65 14,. 63. The income from the vari•Aus sources had been £6B, which thus enabled them to keep out of debt and with the balance at the end of last year, left a balance of £3 6s. During the year £9 ss. sd. had been raised for the fund for helping the poor about the district, and ET 12s. 31. had been distributed which had:turned much sorrow into gladness. They had now got the chapel in order, but there was one want which must be supplied, and he hoped that much support would be given to it. He had already a promise from one person of £5. He referred to a new organ. (Applause.) He would be glad to receive subscriptions from anyone towards this object. The committee of management for the ensuing year were : President. Mr. Nathan Rough; vice-presi- dent, Mr. Thomas Dale; treasurer. Mr. Thomas Taylor; secretary, Mr. Thomas Ashley; and Messrs James Wool- ley, David Hill, George Dale, Joseph Monks, jun., James Mills, Henry Edwards, W. Pawson, and Thomas</p>
        <p>Edmonds. Mr. Jonti CROSFIRLD, who was received with loud ap- plause. said that he was very pleased to meet them again at that annual tea meeting, which he hoped they had all enjoyed. He was also glad to hear that satis- factory report from the secretary. It was very much to the credit of the congregation that they had been able to raise such a large sum of money to re-decorate the chapel and to put it into a clean and proper order. Also they had to be thankful that there were 83 many volun- tary Sunday school teachers, both male and female who devoted themselves to the education of the young, every Sunday in the year, and as he had often said before, the parents of the children ought to be extremely grateful to those young people who devoted themselves to the good cause. (Hear, hear.) He was happy to know that harmony existed between them and that they were what he might call a happy family. (Applause.) Con- tentment. as was said in Lancashire, was a continual feast, and he hoped that they would always continue to be contented. (Applause.) He had been asked to tell them something about the tour round the world which his wife and himself made last year. He gave a short account of it at Walton Lea, in September last, and he was afraid it would be a twice told tale to any of them who were present on that occasion. But those who were not there, might like to hear a little about what they saw. It was a big journey to go round the world, but when he had finished it, it seemed to him a very short one, and that the world was very small. The world was 24,000 miles in circumference, and they travelled 28,000 miles-20,000 by st-a and 8,000 by land, and after all their enjoyment of the trip, and the wonderful things they saw, and the beautiful countries they passed through, he never was so thankful in his life as when he landed on the stage at Liverpool on the sacred soil of Britain again. (Applause.) They crossed the continent of Europe by train in 48 hours, and got to Brindisi in Italy, where they took steamer, and in four days they arrived at Egypt, at Ismailia. They then went 90 miles by train to Cairo, and went up the Nile, which was 3.000 miles long, and about four times as wide as the Mersey at Warrington bridge. The river was of a dirty yellow colour owing to the mud which spoiled what otherwise would boa beautiful stream. But the muddi- ness of the water was a godsend to the country,because when the river overflowed its banks, which it did once every year. at the time of the heavy rains in Africa, and rose some 20 feet, it overflowed the meadows and the fields on either side, and deposited the mud which was in the water, and made Egypt one of the richest coun- tries in toe world. The water also, when allowed to settle, was believed to he the best drinking water which could be found, so that they could well understand in a hot country like Egypt,surrounded by desert,what a godsend that glorious river was. It was the life of the countay. Mr. Crosfield here read an account of the Nile from an old book entitled " The Crescent and the Cross." Proceeding, he said that they went up the mighty river for seven days. and there were old ruins and temples on either side and also pyramids. It was Interesting to see the different characters of people and their dress, on the way along. They at length reached a place called Luxor, and i near Luxor was Karnak, the ancient Thebes. Thebes was a city ten miles square,and the temples which were standing there were the most beautiful temple on the earth. They went into the prin- cipal temple which it was almost impossible to describe. There were 180 columns, each 39 feet in circumference. These columns were covered with hieroglyphics, at least 6,000 years old, and yet they were as clean cut and sharp as though they were done yesterday. After leaving Eeeypt,they went across the Indian Ocean to Bombay,and afterwards to Ceylon,which was full of beautiful tropical trees and plants which only grew here in hot houses. The climate was fearfully hot. He must tell them that he met in Handy, Sergeant Ward and his wife, who were connected with that chapel. (Applause.) Mu. Ward used to be a teacher in the Sunday school, and he believed her sister was in the sewing class which Mrs. Crosfield held every Tuesday. They were very pleased to Fee someone from Warrington. (Applause.) From Ceylon they went eastwards and came to Singa.pore,then Hong Kong. and Shanghai, the latter of which was one of the filthiest and dirtiest spots, it was possible to imagine. There was not the slightest regard for sanitary matters. From Shanghai they crossed to the most beauti- ful country it was probable there was in the world. He meant Japan. Japan was a country it was almost im- possible to diecribe, it was so beautiful, and the people were better than their country. The people were con- tented, happy, well dressed and clean, th ‘y never quarrelled, he never saw a woman strike a child, and never saw a drunken man, and the men were perfectly content to carry him in a chair for miles in a blazing hot sun for 2s. a day. (Laisehter). He was a tidy weight to carry (renewed laughter).Mr. Crosfield then proceeded to describe a visit to the sacred mountain of the Japanese and said that altogether they were charmed with the country, and delighted with the people and he hoped if all was well to go again next year. (Applause.) After leaving japan, they went across the Pacific Ocean for 18 day., pasQing Honolulu on the way, and at length ar- rived at San Francisco. They then went to see the Yosemite Valley, with its cliffs, 3,000 feet in height, and also tremendous trees some twice as high as the works chimney. They then travelled five days across America which wag miserable, wearying, and fatiguine,, and ar- rivel at the Niagara Falls, the largest falls in the world. They afterwards went to New York and only stayed there a single day, but it was long enough for him, for he did not like either America or its cities. They crosaed the Atlantic Ocean and after two narrow escapes in the St. George's Channel, they landed in Liverpool at the landing stage. An interesting programme was afterwards submitted, the following by the i'ems :—Piano solo, Miss Crosfield song. " Some day," Mrs. Strange ; song, " Our Jack," Mr. Griffiths ; song." A Sweet Little Letter," Miss Elsie Ward; recitation, Mr. H. M. Ashton ; song, "The Holy City." Miss Jean; song, "The Village Smithy," Mr. Wallington ; piano solo, " Ma Micnonne," Miss Annie Ward •, song, " Come into the Garden Maud," Mr. Griffiths ; song, " Don't bury me deep," Mrs. Strange ; recitation, Mr. H. M. Ashton ; song, " There lived a King," Mr. Wallington. The programme concluded with a humorous sketch entitled "The Barber's Courtship," the parts being well taken by Miss Ashley, Miss C. Tilling, and Messrs. Kay, Hassell, Edmonds and Monks. The proceedings closed with the usual votes of thanks.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DEBATING SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>"ARMENIA AND THE EASTERN QUESTION."</p>
        <p>TWO REMARKABLE LETTERS. There was a fair attendance at the Masonic Hall, Bold-st.,Warrington, on Tuesday, when an ordinary meeting ot the Warrington Debating Society was held. Alderman Francomb, J,P., C.C., presided over an interesting discussion on "Armenia and the Eastern Question." The CHAIRMAN briefly explained his lack of knowledge on the subject before them, and at once called upon Mr. C. Lyon to open the debate. Mr. LYON'S paper was of considerable length, and dealt exhaustively with the history of the Turkish race, its descent from the flung and first establishment isa Europe in 1453. After arriving at the present position of affairs, Mr. Lyon said we had had it in our pawer to rescue the Armenians, but had preferred to leave them under the heel of the Turk. It was, however, admitted by everybody that the days of the Turkish Empire were numbered, though it looked as if all the Western Powers' were leagued together to prop up the air-bubble and prevent it coming into contact with some outside agency which would smash it up. But, nothing would in the end save it from collapsing from its internal rot- tenness. At the present rate of decrease of the Turkish population its complete extermination was only a mat- ter of some deeades. It would not satisfy him kthe speaker) to say, "drive the Turks from Constantinople "; the greatest difficulty would then have to be overcome. (Hear, hear.) What Power was to take their place ? They should look at the rival claims and give an un- biassed verdict. That Russia would like to take possession of Constantinople went without saying. He was no ad- vocate of the Russians' claims, though from their poiat of view:much was to be said for their claims. Russia had practically no seaboard, and what right had we to say she should have no seaboard? We had no right whatever. If the Turks were ex- pelled from Europe their hold on Asia Minor— practically their native country would—be atrengthened. When the division of the " sick man's" country took place, as soon it would the policy of this country towards Russia should give way to a sense of the justice of her claims. The balance of power had been the battle-cry of modern nations. The triple alliance would probably be destroyed and he himself would prefer an alliance between France, England and Russia. If Russia had been our open coe, an open foe was better than a false friend. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Lyon then read the follow- ing letter, which he had received from Mr. W. H. Quilliam, solicitor, Liverpool, the head of the Moslems in England :—" I am in receipt of yours of the 25th inst. and if time had permitted would like to have replied thereto at considerable length, but I must avoid doing so. I enclose you copy of a letter written by an American gentleman who has visited the East and which really gives in a small compass the actual condition of affairs in Armenia, but I would add to his remarks that the condition of matters in Armenia prior to the disturb- ances was practically that of the condition of Scotland about the period of the accession of William of Orange to the English Throne. Lake Vas about which so much has been said is surrounded by mountains which border upon Persia and Russia : the Kurds are mountaineers resembl- ing in a great measure the highlanders of Scotland. The Armenians are the lowlauders and are also great money- lenders and usurers and not particularly addicted to truthfulness, consequently the district was in a constant state of irritation, added to which a number of revolu- tionary committees were formed among the Armenians aided, no doubt, by money sent from Russia, and these committees fomented the ever present bitter feelings between the two races. It was the old story of Fenianism in Ireland and the setting of race and class against each other, fanned with the flame of religious bigotry. It became necessary for the Turkish Government to put down this revolution in those distant parts of its domin- ions, far away from railway communications and where , roads are few. Troops were dispatched to the scene to restore order, they were fired uuon and attacked by the revolutionists and, of course, could not do otherwise than attack in return but beyond this necessary action on the part of the military authorities, I am assured by respon- sible officials that nothing has taken place and that the stories circulated in the English press as to terrible atro- cities having been committed by the Turkish soldiery are either exaggeration or utter falsehoods. In repressing the Indian mutiny in 1857 the British soldiers were guilty of far more atrocities than ever the Turkish soldiery have perpetrated in Armenia, but then the former were of course good Christians fighting for the "Union Jack of old England" and the people butchered were only' blacks,Hindoos or Muslims,"while in Armenia because the beaten party happen to call themselves Christians, albeit that they themselves consider Protes- tants as heretics, the gospel drum is vigorously beaten and England resounds with shrieks of indignation against the Turks.—Yours faithfully, W. B. ABDULLAH Cluittiam, Sheikh-ul-Islam of the Britith Isles." (Copy of letter referred to above). "My Dear Sir,—lt is gratifying to know that you have a glimpse of the truth regarding the lamentable condition of affairs in Turkey. The missionaries and the American Board of Foreign Missions are playing a very despicable game.and they have a vigorous and unprincipled ally in the Armen- ians themselves. The trouble was inaugurated by the Armenian Revolutionary Committees of New York and London and they are working on Christian prejudice, intolerance and fanaticism to carry out a political plot. Taken in ita entirety it is the most cowardly and dis- graceful act of treachery and dishonesty to which Chris- tians have loaned their support during the century. Many of them—the majority in fact, really believe the false cablegrams are manufactured and manipulated by , the political agents of the Armenians in Europe,that ap- pear in the newspaperssand I think they fully believe the Armenians in Europe have been oppressed and massacredi because they pose as Christians ; but I am satisfied the I missionaries in Turkey know this ia absolutely false. and I that this knowledge is possessed by the Armenian Board of Foreign Missions. It is a fact capable of complete proof that the question of religion does not enter int. the subject at all. No Government in world more fully respects the religious views of its subjects, or more care- fully protects them in the exercise of their religion than does the Turkish. It has allowed a horde of ignorant, bigoted, and unprincipled Christian missionaries to over- run its territory, and the incite sedition and treason, and has not only protected thoroughly, but has given to un- grateful scam is privileges they would never dare to ask of their own Government." Councillor BENNETT said he agreed with Mr. Lyon's paper but there was som ,thing to answer in the two letters. Those letters were entitle to respect as the opinions of persons in authority but personally from the facts which had appeared lateiy, and the blue-beoks of the Foreign Office he for one could not believe for a moment that Turkey was less black than she was painted. (Hear,hear.) If they knocked a thousand per cent, off the reports they would have a minimum of horror quite sufficient to make every Englishman with a spark of sympathy boil with indignation and resolve to do what he could to end the wrongs. Mr. Lyon having in some measure blamed Lords Beaconsfield and Salis- bury for these difficulties,Mr. Bennett said it seemed to him that we might with as much reason trace them to Mr. Gladstone. He woule not whitewash the Turks in any way,but he did say that the source of our difficulties to-day was to be found in the fact that the present Government had inherited a legacy from the Gladston- ian Government which they had te fulfil. He referred to the concert of Europe, Mr. Gladstone's pet subject. He was bound to admit that we as Englishmen had a special responsibility in the matter, and that we held Cyprus to-day as a pledge of nur responsibility to main- tain order in Armenia. (Hear, hear.) He believed the Government had done its best under the circumstances, and was sure that Lord Salisbury would have put an end to the atrocities long ago if he had had his way. If Europe would not act he believed we were justified in acting alone. If we were prepared—as had been shown —to do our best where our interest was at stake we ought to do it where our h-nour was at stake. (Hear, hear, and applause.) He had no objection to Russia in- terfering and he thought our objection to them was un- called for. Mr. J. C. DALTON said Mr. Lyon seemed very much impressed with the Russians but did he know that they had persecuted Poland every bit as tnuch as the Turks had persecuted Armenia? (Applause.) Were the Russian people contented ? What about Siberia? If we handed the Armenians over to the tender mercies of the Russians their condition would be very little better. (Hear, hear, and applause.) He disputed Mr. Bennett's statement that Gladstone was to blame, and said that Salisbury was equally to blame with Gladstone, as he had also supported the concert of Europe. He believed the difficulties were caused by England's shilly-shally policy with respect to Turkey. Turkey, he ssid, should not be tolerated in a civilised age. Mr. NIELD contended that Lord Salisbury was blame- less, and that he had done the best he could under the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>The traffic from the Manchester Ship Canal last week was exceptionally heavy, and the number and tonnage of the vessels which arrived and sailed being the largest recorded for several months. As compared with the re- turns for the preceding seven days, the coastwise trade increased by 53 ships and 4,195 net registered tons. The arrivals and departures in the foreign trade were less by two, but the tonnage increased by 4,503 net regis- tered tons. Amongst the full cargoes imported were 20 of general merchandise, 17 of stone and slate, 13 of pot- tery material, eight of pig iron, two of timber, one of resin, one of logwood, and one of green fruit. Cotton was received from Galceston and Alexandria by the steamers Saturnina and Lavernock ; wool and frozen mutton from Australia,by the Gulf of Siam ; grain from, Boston, Galveston and Libau ; bauxite and esparto- grass from Marseilles and Almeria. Twenty-five general cargoes were exported—eleven of coal, two of purple ore, one of pitch, and one of salt. During the week the first direct steamer arrived from Boston, U.S.A. ; the first direct steamer arrived from Australia, bringing the largest cargo yet shipped in Manchester, including the first direct consignment of wool ; and a new service to Glasgow was inaugurated, making three independent steamship lines running between this port and the Clyde e direct.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>circumstances. It was his opinion that the moment we set foot on Turkish soil we should bring about a declara- tion by certain powers that they would resist our inter- ference to the utmost. Lord Salisbury could not proceed single•handedly, but needed some strong power to join him.Fle could then break through the concert of Europe, which was a farce. Mr. C. PLINSTON believed the Government of England was anxious to show an example of justice and right- eousnesi, but hesitated at the thought that we might come off badly in the event of war. Mr. J. GANDY said it was all very well to talk about rooting up Turkey,but who was going to take its place? If Russia took Armenia and substituted her rule for the Turkish, he thought it would be a case of " out of the frying-paia into the fire. (Hear, hear and applause.) Messrs. F. R. Roberts, E. J. Hall, Flowthorn, Kij.. gallon and the chairman also spoke,and after Mr. Lyon had replied, the debate closed.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 4th January, 1806. During the week, which was the Ist of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Deaths 21</p>
        <p>Births. Warrington 55 Latchford 5</p>
        <p>Total 6O</p>
        <p>The excess of births over deaths was 37. The death rate from all causes was equal to 20 5 per 1,000, against 16.8 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 154 the corresponding week of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 4•5 per :,000, against 4.5 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 0 8 the corresponding week of 1895. The ages of mortality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 7 6 1 1 7 1 23</p>
        <p>The deaths included-4 from measles, 1 from diphtheria, 2 from rickets, 2 from phthisis, 1 from old age, 2 from brain disease, 3 from heart disease, 1 from. lung disease, 1 from burns. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Membraneous croup. 1 case, scarlet fever 5 cases, enteric fever 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital on Jan. 4th-42.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured .44 inch. The general direc- tion of the wind was S.E. The reading of the Anemo- meter denoting 855 miles.</p>
