        <p>"WHAT IS IT?"</p>
        <p>"What's good Tea r" The youngest and the tenderest buds and le4fiets of evergreen, flowering bushes luxuriating in the sunny and humid hills and valleys of Southern Asia. That teeming land is the paradise of budding, leafy life, for ten thousand tons of rain fall on an acre, there, in one short year, and the fervid, puissant Sun, by his own light and heat combining a volume of the water with a little acid of the air and infinitely little of the soil, evolves an ever- changing verdure far richer than our cooler North can ever know. Belonging to the botanical genus Thea,' the tea-plant is so closely related to the beautiful Camellia, that even experts could not easily tell the difference.</p>
