      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>AV DOUGALL'S PATENT SELFRAISING FLOUR PREV RNTS INDIGESTION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>be closed as quickly as possible, and Parliament and the country will look to the Premier for some statesmanlike proposals for an amicable settle- ment.</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE is not wanting to show South African Troubles, that the South African troubles</p>
        <p>are not yet at an end. In his speech on Saturday, Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, referring, to the Johannesberg district, said, "I see state- ments that mines will be shut down and the miners thrown out of work. I do not believe them, but I will say this, that if they were true the only :sufferers from it, or at all events the chief sufferers, would be the persons who are responsible for these reprehensible proceedings." This warning from the Colonial Secretary was, it is now clear, very much needed. There seems to be a distinct inclination on the part of the wealthy capitalists who control most of the South African mines, and who were primarily responsible for the lawless invasion of the Transvaal by Dr. JAMESON, to bring about another crisis. They can do this by closing the mines on one pretext or another, as it is upon the mines that practically the whole population in and around Johannesberg depend for their livelihood. The capitalists of South Africa seem to be prepared to play any desperate game in order that they may accomplish the end they have set before themselves, but they must surely begin to see that, after Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN'S speech and the proclamation of warning issued by President KRUGER within the last two or three days, they may easily overstep their mark. This country is not going to allow one of its most important colonies to be dominated and disorgan- ised by a few men whose ruling passion seems to be the adding of more millions to their already fabulous wealth. Every fresh manufestation of the spirit of cupidity and lawlessness will make it more necessary for the home government to rigorously inquire into the origin of the Jamas() N raid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING. SPEECH BY SIR JOHN T. 'BRUNNER, M.P.</p>
        <p>At the Liberal Club, Regent-st. Runcorn, on Wednes- day evening, the annual meeting was held under the presidency of Sir J. T. Brunner,Bart.,M.P. There was a good attendance, and among those present were Messrs. A. R. Norman, S. Taylor, J. P., C.C., R. H. Posnett, William Handley, R. Morris, T. Dean, T. Lea, J. Clarke, H. Kirkham, and A. Riley (-cc).</p>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Sir John T. Brunner, who met with a hearty re- ception, delivered a brief address in opening the even- ing's proceedings. He said it was now stale news that the Liberals of the Northwich division did grand work last July, but the memory of it had not faded from his mind, and he had again to thank them for having done such splendid service to their cause. They had as they were aware' a majority of 152 against them in the House of Commons, but they had the fact staring them in the face that .at the last election on an average 24 Liberals voted against 25 Conservatives. It now remained for the Liberals to convert one out of 25 of their opponents, and bring them back to the party of progress. (Applause.) This should not be a very difficult task for the work was not a great one. He was happy to say that the Conservative party had already done a great deal in that direction, for as a result of their failure to carry out the promises made at the last election, many workingmen had already changed their views. This was especially the case in Lancashire in consequence of the. Government's action regarding the Indian Import Duties, and many had been converted from Toryism to Liberalism. He urged his hearers to hold to their principles as steadily and persistently as ever they had done, and if at the next election they had to fight that constituency he had net the shadow of -a doubt that Runcorn would stand where it did last July, and give a good account of itself. They were living in ring times times ; every day brought a new scare, and despite the boast of the great knowledge of foreign affairs by the Foreign Secretary, he bad not been able to keep I England out of trouble. He (Sir John Brunner) hoped that next week when Parliament met, his shortcomings ' I would be exposed and England would be put right before the world,and the people of this country would be proud of their country as unfortunately they had not been during the past few months. On one matter he was satisfied, and that was if the Liberals had been in power their action would have been different—the Government would have given a full and frank exposition of the position of affairs in Armenia. (Applause.) If England were polled to-day such a demand would be made. When the official returns were published, the outrages would be condemned not only by unqed England but by united Christendom. (Applause.) He was very satisfied that if the constituencies of the country were polled that the people would also declare by an unanimous note in favour of the setting up of a fair tribunal on the Venezuelan question, and that such a paltry subject should not lead to the suggestion of war between England and America. (Applause.) Sir John Brunner then proceeded to speak of matters relating to the internal affairs of the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ENCOURAGING REPORT BY THE SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>ENCOURAGING REPORT BY THE SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>The Secretary in presenting the report for the past year, said though some progress had been made in still further paying off the liability on the club premises, the amount of reduction had been considerably less than in the previous year and more limited than was hoped at the time of the last annual meeting. After paying all the current liabilities for the year, the debt on the club premises had been reduced by £2O 7s. The premises had been cleaned, painted, and decorated with the addition of a new lamp. After the work carried on at the Club during the year had been referred,to the report went on te say that the club proved its suitability and usefulness as a poli- tical centre during the general election. Its members thrcw theniselves heartily into the exciting contest, and the premises were nightly the rendezvous of the political enthusiasism of the district. There was no doubt that the club from its central and convenient position played no unimportant part in securing the return of their respected president by such an enlarged majority.as member of Parliament, and retaining the Northwl9h division as the one impregnable Liberal stronghold in Cheshire. (Applause.) The report was unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>'ELECTION OF OFFICERS. The election of officers of the club for the ensuing year was then proceeded with. Sir John T. Brunner, Bart., M.P., was re-elected president ; Messrs. W. Ockleston, P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., S. Taylor, J.P., C.G., A. R. Norman, R. H. Posnett, and W. Mather were appointed vice-presidents; Mr. Ockleston treasurer, and Mr. A. Riley secretary. The members to compose the committee were elected by ballot, and the result was as follows : Messrs. James Green, John Clarke, W. B. Brimelow, Samuel Bailey, William Wright,Robert Owen. John Stan. way, W. J. Cropper, James Lea' W. Handley, Wesley Cooke, Petri- Corns, and Walter Cartwright. This concluded the business of public interest.</p>
        <p>SEWING MACHINES Hand LL lOr Treadle° £4 4e with</p>
        <p>handiome walnut coyer.-J. W. DUTTON. 16 and 18.</p>
        <p>Sankey-at., Warrington.—Advt. HOUSEHOLD LINES, hemmed and marked, free of ella rge J. and W. Duutun, Sankey-at., Warrington.—Advt. CONCERT AND DRAMATIC PERFORMANCE AT WINWICK.</p>
        <p>A concert and dramatic performance were given in the Parish Church Schoolroom, Winwick, on Saturday in aid of the funds of the Winwick Cricket Club. The artistes for the concert were Mrs. R, H. Pearson (Warrington), Miss Cane (Eccles),Mr. Fred Lane (Eccles), Mr. George Murray (Manches- ter), Mr. G. Davies (Liverpool), Mr. R. H. Pearson solo violinist, Warrington), and Messrs, W. H. Payton (Warrington), and H. D. Caine (Eccles), accompanists, and for the dramatic performance Miss Lucy Mason. Miss Alice Caine, and Messrs. Wilfrid Jackson and Albert Politt. The schoolroom was crowded with a very appreciative audience,and the Rev. Canon Penrhyn, rector and rural dean, and president of the Cricket Club, presided. The Chair- man said he thought the club had done much good in promoting healthful recreation during the summer months, and also their football club in the winter. Be wished the club every success. The programme, which was an excellent one, was then proceeded with. The second part of the entertain- ment consisted of a farcical comedv,entitled "Betsy Baker ; or, too attentive by half," and was excel- lently played, the characters being—Betsy Baker, Miss Lucy Mason; Cousin Crummy, Mr. Wilfrid Jackson ; Mrs. Mouser, Miss Alice Caine; arid Mr. Marmaduke Mouser, Mr. Albert Pollitt</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA Is Ft gentle stimulant ; it does not irritate the nerves, but is s.,othing, refreshing, nourishing,invigorating, and strengthen- iggito the body.—Advt.</p>
        <p>SEWING MecntNEs Hand £2 183.6 d. Treadle £44s. with handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. Durrox 16 and 18. Sankev-st., Warrington.—Adv.</p>
        <p>-3FWING MACHLNES, Hand £2 188.6 d. Ireadle £4 4s. witr handsome walnut cover. —J . and W. DUTTOS 16 and 13, Sankey-st,. Warringtoa.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>GEORGE A-ND COMPANY-</p>
        <p>(Opposite Crane and Sons), SCOTIAN D-ROAD, LIVERPOOL. North Tram i pass Door.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>PARLIAMENT will again as- The Government and Parliament. semble on the 11th of the pre- sent month, and it may be doubted whether either the Government or their supporters will meet each other in a particularly complacent frame of mind. Conservatives have in late years made a specialite—if we may use that term—of their foreign policy. They have been so accustomed to belaud what they are pleased to call their own " spirited foreign policy" and to depreciate the foreign policy of suc- cessive Liberal Governments, that t hey have almost persuaded themselves that Tory adminstra- tion is synonymous with prestige abroad. To people who have be3n reared and natured in thi s faith the present state of our foreign affairs must be extremely discouraging. Lord SALISBURY'S occupancy of the foreign office has not only not led to any triumph, but there has, on the con- trary, been a distinct falling off from the standard maintained by Lard ROSEBERY. In regard to the Armenian question, for instance, Lord SALTS - BURY'S diplomacy has ended in utter failure. The Armenians whom he endeavoured to protect have besn murdered and outraged in increasing num- bers, and the Sultan has more and more openly displayed his intention to exterminate the luck- less Christians. The need for intervention of an effectual kind between the Turk and his victims has certainly not. been lessened by later information. The original newspaper reports of the worst kind of atrocities have been amply verified by the official blue-book which has been issued this week, after:needless delay. Some of the details of the Sasun massacres which are given by Vice-Consul HALLWARD are simply appalling, yet they are only typical of what has been done in connection with many other massacres. The official testimony establishes once for all the guilt of the Turkish authorities. Mr. HALLWARD says, for example, that 'after some Kurdish attacks had been repelled, Turkish troops began to appear in the villages. They alleged that they had come to protect the villagers, but during the night they arose and slaughtered all the inhabi- tants—men, women, and children. It is said,adds the Vice-Consul, that at first the Turkish troops hung back from the butchery, not relishing their task, but the officers read an Imperial Firman to the soldiers and urged them "not to fail in their duty." After this the work of destruction was carried through without mercy or distinction of age or sex. At one place a number of young men were bound hand and foot, laid out in a row, had brushwood piled on them,and were burnt alive. In another place bayonets were placed upright in the grouad and a hapless priest tkr3wn up in the air so that he fell upon them. In one instance a considerable number of Armenians, men, women, and children, were bayoneted, with the exception of sixty young women and girls. These were driven into a church and the soldiers were told that they could do what they liked with them and then kill them. This order was carried out to the letter. Petroleum has been used for the purpose of burning houses and their inhabitants, and a pathetic incident is narrated of a little boy run- ning from the flames with his clothes on tire and being pushed back at the end of a soldier's bayonet. These are some of the horrors—and not the worst—to which the Armenians have been subjected week after week and month aft 3r month, while British ironclads lay at the mouth of the Dardanelles. It is, of course, alleged that forcible intervention by the British Government would have led to greater evils than even the depopula- tion of Anatolia, but that, after all, is a matter of opinion. There is reason for believing that on at least one occasion Lord SALISBURY, had he been capable of prompt and decisive action, might have effectually intervened without any danger of bringing about an European war. That opportun- ity was missed and now it would appear that, so far as this country is concerned, the Armenians are abandoned to their fate. This country is specially pledged.to protect them under an agree- ment entered into by a former Tory Gav ern- meat yet the Tory Government aow in office has utterly failed to honour that pledge. The Sultan has not been coerced into decent behaviour by Lord SALISTATRY'S, forcible-feeble policy ;he has only been driven into the arms of Russia, and British reputation was never at a lower point in the East. In some other respects the Government will be placed upon its defence in regard to its foreign policy. The large concessions which have been made to France,without apparently in any way conciliat- ing that country, must, we should think, be es- pecially distasteful to a large number of Tories who were never tired of denouncing the " weak- ness of the Liberals. And, then, with regard to Venezuela, Lord SALISBURY'S methods have cer- tainly not resulted in any conspicuous success. There is now a general feeling that that question might have been better handled in another way. The PREMIER'S exhaustive discussion of the Mon- roe doctrine and its validity as a piece of inter- national law was just a little beside the mark. It is, after all, no interest of this country to depre- ciate the Monroe doctrine,which is with Americans a matter of faith. If Lord SALISBURY had said less about the Monroe doctrine in a general way, and more about its inapplicabilty to the present boundary dispute, probably there would at the present time be less friction between this county and the United States. As it is there is an open dispute between the two countries which should</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATUROA Y, FEBRUARY L 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>GIFT TO THE PARISII CHURCH.</head>
        <p>GIFT TO THE PARISII CHURCH.</p>
        <p>' The Rev. Joseph Holden vicar of Antrobus, has pre- sented a Litany desk for use in the Parish Church, as an expression of his gratitude for the many kindnesses re- ceived while he was in Runcorn. THE HEALTH OF THE VICAR OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In the Vicar's letter to his parishioners published in the " Runcorn Parish Magazine," the Rev. Canon Wood, writing from Pisa, on the 22nd January,states that they are all well, and thinks he is distinctly stronger and more himself.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN EXHIBITORS. At the Liverpool Dog Show held this week there were several Runcorn exhibitors. Mr. J. H. Weston,chemist, _ _ . _</p>
        <p>showed a young S. Bernard, and his Sir Vesto was ac- corded a reserve card in the limited class, and a v. h.c.iu the novice class. An Airdale pup was shown by Mr.</p>
        <p>Briacoe, of -Ch-nich-st., and he obtained a h.c. card. AN OLD MAN SERIOUSLY INJURED AT RUNCORN. An aged man named Bonkem, residing with his son, Harry Bonkem, at 3, Byron-st., sustained serious in- juries on Monday under somewhat distressing circum- stances. It is alleged that he was struck heavily to the ground on Waterloo Bridge by another man. Bonkem was picked up and conveyed home. Dr. McDougall was called in,andlon examination found that the man's thigh was broken. Owing to the man's advanced age the case</p>
        <p>s a most serious one. A STRANGE VISITOR TO TAR BAYER'S.</p>
        <p>A cow put in its appearance at the shop of Mr. Thomas Lea, baker and confectioner, Greenway-road, Runcorn. an Tuesday evening. The animal was making an effort to pass through the shop, when Mr. Lea, who happened to be on the premises at the time,secured it by the horns and was fortunate enough to back it through the door into the street again. Considering that the shop is stocked with numerous glasses and fancy cases, it is almost miraculous that no damage was done. BRUN'SWICIC WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE. There was a well-attended public meeting and enter- tainment in connection with this Band of Hope in Brunswick schoolroom on Monday evening, and the pro- ceedings were presided over by Mr. E. Marshall, who delivered a brief address. A varied programme was submitted, consisting of glees by the choir' and recita- sions,songs, and duem. 'lhe soloists were Mr. j.Leath- wood,Miss Agnes Jeffrey, Mr. H. Millington; and the following recited :—Misses Amelia Ashcroft. Ellen Pres- cott,Sarah Jane Sinethana, Lizzie Widders,Ethel Collinge Florence Burrows, Lily Faulkner, Martha Basnett, Helen Smetham, Miggie Faulkner, Daisy Burrows, Gerty Coventry, Masters F. J. Kakham, and John Parry. Messrs. Henry Millington and John Leathwood contri- buted a duet.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. S. RATENSCROFT.</p>
        <p>It is with regret we announce the death of Mr. Samuel Ravenscroft, of 8, Waterloo-road, Runcorn, sailmaker, at the age of 70 years. The deceased who had lived the greater part of his life in Runcorn, took an active part in conuection with Wesleyanism. For over half a century he associated himself with St. Paul's Wesleyan Church, and had held various important offices. Up to a suoit time ago he was a class leader when ill-health compelled him to reiinquish the position. He had carried on the business of a sailmaker since he was 25 years of age. His business naturally brought him into contact with seafaring men by whom he was held in great esteem and resp-xt. He leaves a widow, a son and a daughter, and many friends to mourn his loss. It may be stated that his son is the Rev. S. H. Ravenscroft, who is stationed at Durban. PRESENTATION TO MR. MICHAEL BARKER. _</p>
        <p>On Monday the staff of the Runcorn, Urban District Council, presented Mr. Michael Barker, the consulting engineer,with a testimonial as a proof of the esteem and respect in which they hold him. Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk) occupied the chair, and made the presentation, which consisted of a handsome edition of the complete works of Charles Dickens, and also a smoker's com- panion. The following description had been written in side one of the books i--" To Micuael Barker, Eq. We, the undersigned, your colleagues on the staff of the Runcorn Urban District Council hereby expres3 the high esteem ad warm regard in which we hold ycu, and beg your acceptance cf these works. Fred A. Lake, E. Mar- shall, James Wilding, J. D. Jones, Thomas Boote, James Percival, William Garratt, Walter Jnnts. J. B. Holland, William Sutton,and William Francis Salkeld, Jan.,1896.” The gentlemen who were present at the presentation spoke of the high esteem in which they held Mr. Barker. In response, the rtcipient feelingly thanked the sub- scribers for their present.</p>
        <p>STAR OF FREEDOM LODGE OF ODDFELLOWS, RUNCORN. On Wednesday evening the officers and members of this lodge met at the Egerton Arms, and partook of supper provided by the hostess, Mrs. Waterworth. After the removal of the cloth the Secretary of the Rising Sun Lodge was v )ted to the chair. Bro. Hancock proposed the toast of the Star of Freedom Lodge. He said for a long time the Star of Freedom and Rising Sun Lodge had been running a neck and neck race, but the Star had now passed them in numbers, and he hoped they would do still better in the future.—Bro. Moston, secretary of the Star of Freedom Ledge, responded. He concluded by proposing prosperity to the Halton and Run- corn District.—Bro. Massey, the di3trlct secretary, replied, and said within the last five years they had in- creased by 80 members, and the funds were augmented by £l,OOO. They had a prosperous juvenile lodge of 440 members.—Bro. Hazlehurst also spoke, stating that the members were determined to make their lodge a con- tinued success. The remainder of the evening was spent</p>
        <p>in a convivial manner. THE FATAL ACCIDENT TO A RUNCORNIAN AT CONWAY. The body of Samuel Waterworth, 16 years of age, son of John Waterworth, of Lightburn-st.. Runcorn, who, as already reported, was killed on Thursday morning last near Conway by the tiller of the flat Jacob striking him on the head, was conveyed by train to Runcorn on fri- day. On Monday, the remains were interred in Runcorn cemetery in the ptesence of a large assemblage. A funeral service was held in the Seamen's Mission at 2.30. Owing to the absence of the Rev. H. W. L. O'Rorke, one of the assistant chaplains of the Mersey Mission to Sea- men.in consequence of a mistake in the railway journey, the Rev. H. B. Firth officiated. Thera was a large con- gregation, and the service was an impressive one. A procession was afterwards formed, and it was headed by Mr. Shaw (missionary), Mr. W. Shaw, followed by Messrs. W. Cook, W. Shaw, jun., Walton, Broadhurst Price, Wardle, and Mack. Then followed the coffin en- wrapped with a Union Jack, and covered with numer- ous wreaths. The relatives and friends followed in the rear. The service at the grave-side, conducted by the Rev. H. B. Firth, was wisely brief. Floral tributes had been sent by Messrs. F. Rowlands, T. Cook (deceased's Sunday school teacher), Broadhurst, Walton,Miss Gough, brother and sister, Mrs. Matthews, and a beautiful arti- ficial wreath in glass case by the teachers, scholars, and friends at the mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Parish -Church on Monday afternoon, a marriage was solemnized in which the contractinz par- ties were Mr. John Lawton, of Clarence-st., Runcorn, son of Mr. All John Lawton, late of Stafford, and Miss Florence Marsh, daughter of the late Mr. John Marsh, of Top Locks. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. H. B. Firth. The bride was attired in a costume of lavender grey, trimmed with silk, and wore a large picture hat with ostrich feathers. The bridesmaids were Miss Ilindley, of Culcheth, near Warrington, and Miss Ravenscroft, step-sister to the bride. They each carried lovely bouquets. Mr. T. Warder acted as best man,and Mr. William Lawton (brother) was groomsman. The ceremony was performed in the presence of a large con- gregation. During the afternoon between fifty and sixty guests were entertained at the residence of the bride's ' stepfather, Captain Ravenscroft, at the Lord Rodney Inn, Church-st. The carriages were supplied by Messrs. Yar- wood. The presents included the tollowing :—Mrs. Ravenscroft, sewing machine and cheque; Mr. James Ravenscroft, handsome clock; Mrs. Lawton, chiffonier, &amp;c.; Mrs. Stephenson, bed linen; Mrs. Green, pair of vases; Mrs. Noden, house linen; Miss Ravenscroft, rug ; Mr. Warder, toilet service; Mr. Marsh, tea service; Miss Lawton, teapot and sugar basin; Miss Lizzie Lawton, set of jugs; Mrs. Ralph Ravenscroft, cruet ; Mr. Savage, table cloth ; Mr. Thomas Marsh, set of fire irons; Mrs. Peacock, set of jugs ; Mrs. Marsh, knives and forks ; Mrs. Moores, toilet covers and towels; Mr. Caygill, pair of pictures; Miss Wilde, cheese dish; Miss Flindley, set of vases; Captain Steele, clock; Miss Green,antimacassars; Mr. William Lawton, lamp; A Friend, kitchen utensils.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>On Monday the annual public meeting of the atipve society was held in the Greenway-road schoolroom.. The Rev.J. Stafford was in the chair and in a short and able speech introd aced Mrs. Osborne. Before that lady began her lecture, Mrs. Harrop presented the report which showed that during the year 23 pledges had been taken, and that the association had inaugurated Pleasant Satur- day Evening entertainments. Mrs. Osborne gave a very interestin,, and instructive History of the Women's Whiskey War." This war she said began with prayer, was bathed with the tears of women, and consecrated by gentle lives." The portraits of many of the leaders of the B.W.T.A. were thrown on the screen, including that of the woman who was alike the promo`er and first presi- dent of the movement—Mrs. Judge Thomson. It was stated that in Portland, Maine, although there was a population of 40,000 people, there was not a single grog shop or drinking saloon. There are now 900 branches of the 'V.l'. A. and during the last year 18,000 pledges were taken. Mrs. Osborne appealed for new members in a most earnest and elrquerr: manner. At the close of the lecture Mr. Reynolds proposed arid Mrs. Hilton seconded vote of thar:ks to Mrs. Osborne, to which she grace- fully replied. Mrs. Morris proposed and Mrs. Leach seconded a vote of thanks to Rev. J. Stafford. The thanks of the society are tendered to Mr. James Picktball for his skilful manipulation of the lantern.</p>
        <p>WHY SUFFER ONE MOMENT from torturing, dis- figuring skin diseases, when a warm bath with CUTS- CIIRA SOAP and a single application of CIITICURA (oint- ment), the great skin core, will afford instant relief,per- mit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy, permanent, and economical cure, when the best physicians and all other remedies fail. No preparations ever compounded in the history of medicine have performed the wonder- ful cures daily made by CIITICIIRA remedies. They are in truth the great skin cures,blood purifiers,and humour remedies of modern times,and appeal to those who have suffered long and hopelessly, with irresistible force.</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF_A FORMER RIINCORNIANI</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Fernley Brundrit, youngest son of the late Mr. William Wright Brundrit, of Runcorn, and Miss Eleanor Wilson, eldest daughter of Mr. Daniel Wilson, Sherwood, Claughton, Birkenhead, were united in the holy bonds of matrimony at St. Mark's Churcn, Birken- I head, on Wednesday afternoon. The church had been most beautifully decorated with plants and flowers, the lily predominating. At two o'clock prompt, the bride eritered the church, leaning en the arm of her father,and were preceded down the aisle by the choristers and clergy who sang the hymn commencing " 0 Father all creat- ing." The service was fully choral, and the hymm "0 perfect love," was impressively sung. The Rev. W. J. Cole, M A. (vicar of St. James', Toxteth ; brother-in-law of the bridegroom), the Rev. G. C. Decker, M.A. (vicar of the parish), and the Rev. 0. T. L. Crossley, M.A. (vicar of St. John's, Ezremont), were the officiating clergymen. The bride was elegantly attired, wearing a rich ivory-duchess satin gown, trimmed with point applique lace, with separate train, a wreath of orange blossom and veil, the latter being fastened with pearl and diamond pendant, the gift of the bridegroom. She carried an exquisite banquet of lilies of the valley. There were four maids in attendance,namely, Miss B. A. Wilson Miss F. H. Wilson, Miss E. A. Wilson (sisters), and Miss H. A. Newcombe (neice of the bridegroom). Their dresses were of apricot corded fiilk with net and lace fichues, and they wore brown velvet picture hats, and their ornaments included gold and pearl crescent brooches, the gift of the bridegroom. Lieutenant Reginald E. B. Brundrit, of Ruacorn (cousin of the bridegroom), was best man. At the conclusion of the service the organist played the 4,‘ Wedding March." A reception was held in the afternoon at the residence of the bride's father when about 200 guests attended. The newly-married couple left for London'en route for the Continent. About 100 presents were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>"BOOKS AND READING."</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Bethesda Literary and Social Class on Tuesday. the pastor. the Rev. J. Simpson,spoke on "Books and Reading." The lecturer set himself the task of answering the three questions "Why? What? an i How ?" we should read.which he accomplished in a very interesting and profitable way. A short discussion after- wards enaued,in which the subject of light literature was referred to. BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this society on Wednesday evening,an interesting paper was read by Mr. Saunders on the sub- ject " Ought pew rents to be abolished ?" The essayist contended that they should be, and in the discussion which followed, the majority supported the essayist's view. Amongst those who took part were Messrs. Big- brown, Dodd, Jeffreys, and Millington. The chairman was Mr. 'l'. Simister.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>OUTBREAK OF ANTHRAX AT ASTON.</head>
        <p>OUTBREAK OF ANTHRAX AT ASTON.</p>
        <p>It was certified to the Runcorn Police Superintendent that a pig has died on Sunday from anthrax, on Park, side Farm, Aston, in the occupation of Messrs. G. and J. Percival. Previeusly two pigs had been slaughtered,and another one,which was ailing, was immediately destroyed. On the premises are a large number of horses,cattle,and sheep,and every effm t has been made to prevent the in- fection spreading. In April last, Messrs. Percival lost nearly a hundred sheep through the same disease.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATITE SOCIETY LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Tho 67th half-yearly report of this society which has</p>
        <p>_..„ just been issued, shows that the sales amounted to £44,350 O. 7d. being an increase on the previous hal? year ot £227 103. Oil. The amount disposable, after charging all expenses,including depreciation atd interest on capital, and deducting the cheques purchased, leaves the net amount £6,285 Bs. 11d. The management com- mittee recommend a dividend of 23. 103. in the £en £43,950 of cheque account books brought in,which would amount to £6226 53.. leaving a balance of £59 33. nu. to special dividend reserve fund. SATURDAY EVHNING ENTRRTAINMENTS. Under the auspices of the Runcorn Women's Temper- ance Association, another of the series of pleasant even- ings was held on Saturday, in the Brunner Guild Hall, Runcorn. There was a good attendance. Mr. T. Wail- ing and party from Widnes, took charge of the rrogramme. The items included a pianoforte solo by Miss Wailing ; clarionet solo, Mr. Ryan : songs, Miss Barnes, Miss Hayes, Mise Lloyd, Mr. Broderick, Mr. Norris, and Mr. T. Wailing ; quartette, Gossage's prize party quartette ; violin solos, Mr. Gillion ; cornet solo, Mr. Greenwood ; and mandoline duets, Messrs. Lowe and Wailing. A short address was given by the Rev. T. Brockenbury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WESLTSTAN BOMB MISSION/I,</head>
        <p>WESLTSTAN BOMB MISSION/I,</p>
        <p>Special sermons on behalf of Wesleyan Flame Missions will be preached on Sunday, at St. Paul's Wesleyan Church, Runcorn. The Rev. C. de Putron Gliddon will occupy the pulpit at the morning service, and the Rev. W. George in the evening. A public meeting is to be held on the following Thursday, when two very capable speakers on home mission work will address the meet- ing, viz., the Rev. George Jackson, WA., of Edinburgh. and the Rev. Sampson Weaver, of Liverpool. Great success has attended Mr. Jackson's work in Edinburgh, and his account of it will be most interesting. collec. tions will be made on behalf or the Wesleyan Home Mission Fund.</p>
        <p>- MARINERS' CHURCE MISSION. On Monday evening another of the entertainments provided in this mission was given before a crowded audience under the presidency of Mrs. Forster. Miss A. Hill presided at the organ, and songs were rendered by Miss A. Turner, Miss M. Hill. Miss A. Hill ; duets by Messrs. T. J. Sweetman and Williamson; Miss A. Turner and Miss Bradshaw. Recitations were given by Miss B. Allen, Messrs. D. Smith, and E. Turner, and Mr. T. Sweetman gave a reading. A dialogue entitled "Why Tcung men ought not to marry," was given in an excel- lent manner by Messrs. Horton, Randles, Misses L. Breatman, A. Hill, M. Fenwick, B. Oates, A. Hughes,and K. Read. For NAME BROOCIIES, solid silver name brooches 100. (any name) Government stamped. Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DEBERTS, 10. CHURCH-STRIM. LIVERPOOL, where yen can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks. Jewellery. Electro-Plate, Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc.. at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- graph Watch, 65. lid.. etc.), on page 7 of this Paper. -.A dvt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CAMDEN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</head>
        <p>CAMDEN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>Therelwas a special entertainment in connection with this Band of Hope on Monday when the performance of a piece entitled" The Queen of Choice, and the Flower Queen's Court," was exceedingly well given. The pro- ceedings were presided Jver by the Rev. C. De Putron Gliddoo who made a few appropriate remarks. During the evening Mrs. C. De Putron Gliddon distributed a number of pledge cards to the members of the Band of Hope. Several choruses were well sung by a number of girls,and individual characters were taken as fol!ows : —Rose (Queen), Lily Mundy,bluebell, Beatrice Harrison. carnation, Nellie Temperley, daisy, Lizzie Duncalf, forget-me-not, Bessie Littler, pansy, Martha A. Holt, snowdrop, Lucy Hinde, tulip, Hilda Rawlinson, violet, Jennie Britland. Miss Temperley who had taken great pains in the training of the children, accompanied on the piano, and Mr. S. Bailey fulfi:led the duties of con- ductor. The proceedings throughout were very succes- ful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SEAMEN'.S INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>SEAMEN'.S INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The members of the Methodist Free Church choir, Oakland-at., Widnes, provided the entertainment given in the Seamen's Institute, Runcorn. on Tuesday even- ing. The Rev. J. Stafford presided, and there was a large attendance. The programme was of a miscellane- ous description and included songs by Mrs.Eilwards,Mrs. Bevan ; Messrs. Lowe, Broderick, Wailing, and Braidy. A banjo solo was well given by Mr. Fred Davies,and Mr. J.Green wood contributed cornet so!os. Miss Barrison and Mr. Charles Garratt recited, and mandoline duets wvre performed by Messrs. Lowe ond Wailing. Choruses were also rendered by the choir. An enjoyable evening's enter- tainment concluded with the according of a vote of thanks toithose who had contributed the programme on the mo- tion of Mr. Shaw (missionary), seconded by Mr. Byfield. It may be stated that the entertainment was given by the Widnes friends in return for one which was provided by the choir of the Runcorn branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen on Monday evening on the occasion of the Pleasant Monday evening entertainments in Oak- land-street schoolroom. The Runcorn artistes who con- tributed to the programme were Mrs. R. Shaw, Miss Bandies, Miss E. Mack, Miss L. Shaw, Messrs. S. Cross, T. Woods, Philip Shaw. .1. Moores, and R. Shaw, Master Moores, and Mr. J. Cliff. The choir was under the con- , ductorship of Mr. D. Price, and the accompanists were Mr. T. Foster, and Mr. A. ?rice.</p>
        <p>TIMELY RESCUE FROM DROWNING. A ship-keeper. from Liverpool named William Burns, of Liverpool, who had come to the district to work in connection with the discharging of the "M and E. Cann" at the Runcorn lay-by had a narrow escape from drown- ing in the canal near Weston Point on Friday evening, and would in all probability have been drowned ;had it not be for the timely assistance rendered by the police. P.C. Gregory in company with Mr. Thomas Tonks and P.C. Bancroft were on the banks of the canal at Weston Point on the night iv question when a cry for help was heard, P.C. Gregcry immediately rushed towards the place from whence the cry came, and saw that a man was in the canal a snort distance from where the M. and E. Cann was berthed. He procured a life-buoy and also ' !L rope, and with the assistance and the persons he was in company with, and also a man named Joseph Wil- liams, ho succeeded in bringing the man to the banks. He was taken on board, and with the exception of a wetting was little the worse for the immersion. This is not the first time P. C. Gregory has been instrumental in rescuing persons, and he is to be complimented upon the prompt action he took in the matter.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A CfIILX THROIIOII OVERLAYING. At Runcorn Police Station, on Tuesday afternoon,Mr. J. E. Worslev, coroner, held an inquest on the body of Samuel Miller, aged three weeks, the infant son of William Miller, 22, English-row, Runcorn, employed as a horse driver on the Bridgewater Canal. Dr.McLennan stated that he attended the mother at birth. The child wa duite healthy. He had seen the dead body and there were no marks of violence. The general appearance of the child was consistent with the child having been overlain, and that was the most probable cause of death.—Ann Miller, the mother, deposed that the child had been healthy since birth. She went to bed about half-past nine on Sunday night, and went asleep with the child on her right arm. She awakened about a quarter to four,and was about to give it the breast when she perceived that its appearance was peculiar, and she sent for her husband's mother. Tne child was quite dead. It was covered with clothes.—William Miller,the father, also gave evidence, and stated that the child lay between them in bed.— The Cot= said there was no doubt that the child had been acciiently overlain on the part of one parent or another. It was a great pity that children should be put into bed with their parents, and especially that they should be placed be- tween them. When a child was put into bed with its parents, which he held was a very dangerous thing, it ' should be placed on the outside of them. But it was far ' better to put the child in a cradle or cot, or something of that kind. Hard-working people slept soundly, and there was a very great danger that a child should be accidentally overlain, and a child was very much safer when in a crib or cot than when in bed. Of course in extremely cold weather it might be necessary that the child should have the warmth of the mother, but taken generally it was a very unsafe thing to place children in bed. A verdict that the child was "Accidentally over- lain was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>VESTRY MEETING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>VESTRY MEETING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A vestry meeting was held in Bethesda school-room on Thursday evening. Mr. J. K. Diggle presided and among those present were Messrs. E. Imison, J.P., F. A. Lake (law clerk). E. Marshall, and W. Sutton (clerk). On the motion of the chairman,seconded by Mr. Parkin- son,the fallowing resolution was passed :—'• rhat we the vestry of the township of Runcorn do sanction the bor- rowing by the Runcorn Urban District Council,acting as the Burial Board of the said town,liip, of the sum of £3.280,in order to pay off and discoarge mortgage secu- rities made by the said burial Board now outstanding and in force and bearing interest at the rate of £3 103. per cent. per annum ; and to the securing and repay- ment of the money as borrowed with interest thereon at ' the rate of £3 53. per cent, per annum,and charging the, future poor rates of the township with the repayment of such money and interest thereon."—A vote of thanks having been accorded the Chairman on the motion of Mr. Marshall, seconded by Mr. Imison, the meeting ter- minated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>FHODSHAM PETIT SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FHODSHAM PETIT SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNEsDAY.— Before Mr. A. Thomas (chairman). and Mr. T. Rigby,. _ . .</p>
        <p>LICENSING.—SamueI Davies 4-Plied for and was granted temporary permission to sell at the Railway Inn, Helsby. A F 001,1911 Am—Frederick Garner, bricksetter, Frod- sham Lordship, was summoned for assaalting Ellen Beauty- man, wife of Harry neautyman, a sailor, at present working on the Ship Canal, at Frodsharn Lordship, on the 26th Decem- ber.—Mr. Knowles, solicitor. Widnes, represented the de- fendant.—Harry Beautyman stated that about ten o'clock on the night of the date named he was coming home in com- pany with his wife and another man from the Bull's Head public-lions°. Overton, and when nearing Mr. Gaskell's shop he saw the defendant and another man talking together. The defendant shouted " Hold on lam corning your road," He then came to them and pointed a gun in the direction of them and said •• which b— shall I aim at first.'' The friend he was walking with got hold of the muzzle of the gun, and told defendant to be careful. His (witness's) wife fainted and had to be taken to bed, and she had not been able to get out of bed since. Witness went and spoke to the defendant, and told him of his wife's condition, but he continued to follow them and used most insulting language to Mrs. Beautymen. Defendant was under the influence of drink.—ln reply to Mr. Knowles witness stated that they had been dancing and not drinking. H; admitted that he had a dance with his wife during the night.—John Sallivan,3B, Station-road, Ellesmere Port, gave corroborative evidence as to the defendant's con- duct on the night in question.—For the defence Mr. Knowles thought that there had been a mistake as to the language used. lithe defendant had presented a loaded firearm he could have been charged with a more serious offence, but he was not charged with that. He (Mr. Knowles) contended tkat no assault heel been committed.—Harry Faulkner spoke to the defendant being without cartridges at the time.—The magistrates imposed a fine of 20s. and costs (£l Os. 6d.), or in default one month's imprisonment. DOGS AND THEIR COLLAR4.—The following were dealt with for not having collars on their dogs, or the names of the owners engraved on a plate thereon :—Thomas Rigby, 59, New-road, Latchford, 53. including costs; Henry Watson, butcher, Overum, 54. including costs ; and William Clarke, Church-street, William Jones, stonemason, and Joseph Hig- ginson, carter. 2s. 6d. each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>INQUEST AT RUNCOItN. A DELAYED FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>INQUEST AT RUNCOItN. A DELAYED FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>At the Seamen's Institute, Runcorn,on Saturday morn- ing, Mr. J. E. Worseley, coroner, held an inquest on the body of a seaman named John B. Johnston who died on the 19th January on board the barque" M.and E.Cann" from Nova Scotia, but which did not arrive at Runcorn lay-by until the following Friday. The body was then removed to the Seamen's Institute to await an inquest. The first witness called was Richard Johnson, of Queen-stree ;, Whitehaven, who identified the body as that of his father, 54 years of age. It was five months since he last saw him alive. He enjoyed good health, but had complained of rheumatism. Mary Johnston, Church-street,Whitehaven,stated that the deceased was her husband. She had not seen him since August. She received a letter from him three weeks ago in which he stated that he had an attack of rheumatism. Patrick Fitzgerald. master of the barque, "Id. and E. Cann," Yarmouth, Nova Scotia,stated that the deceased joined his vessel on Sept. 10th. He was an able-bodied seaman. As a rule his health was good, butte had on one or two occasions complained of pains in the stomach. About six o'clock on the evening of the 17th, deceased was taken ill. He had not been on shore, and there was no other sickness on board. Witness gave him a glass of whiskey. He was relieved from duty, and next morn- ing he still complained of being ill. On Sunday witness gave him 35 drops of laudanum and placed him hot water cloths on his stomach, which relieved him. He afterwards gave him a dose of castor oil, but he diei at seven o'clock the same evening. It was impossible to get a doctor to him. Deceased lived ten hours after the laudanum had been adminstered. He was conscious all the time. Witness duly reported the death to the Cus- toms and Sanitary Authority at Liverpool, but no action was taken by those authorities. He did not consider he had any authority to leave the body at Eastham, and be was not efficiently informed that an inquest had been fixed tø take place there on the 21st inst. Evidence was also given by the first and second mates of the vessel, corroborating the Captain's statement. Charles Porter, able-bodied seaman, who had been a friend of the deceased on board, said the Captain was very attentive to the deceased. The food was very good and fresh, and the cooking vessels were kept cleae. Dr. McDougall said from the evidence given he con- cluded that death was due to some obstruction of the bowels. There were no suspicious circumstances that he could hnd. fiThe Coroner in summing up said the man died in the Port of Liverpool on the 19th of the month, and the question naturally arose why the inquest was not held before that date. It appeared that the captain had never had a case of that description before, and never had the misfortune of having a dead body on board. Owing to a dense fog he was lying in the river, unable to com- municate with the authorities all the time he was in Liverpool. He afterwards proceeded to Eastham where he was still in ignorance of any duty lying upon him to report the death. He went to an undertaker, and ' ordered a coffin as he had received instructions that the body was to be removed to Whitehaven for interment, but the undertaker advised him that it would be better to report the matter to the police.. He reported the case but received no instructi,ms as to what he had to do with the body, and his vessel subsequently left Eastham and arrived at Runcorn. It was a matter of regret that the inquest was not held where it ought to have been held by the Liverpool city coroner, but the explanations I given oy the captain satisfied him that he had acted in ignorance, and the only regret he (the Coroner) had was that the friends of the deceased had bees put to the pain of having the body left unburied for such a length of time. The Captain. he believed, had done everything for the man that he could do. The body was in an advanced state of decomposition, and he was glad that the doctor had not considered it necessary to have a post snortent examination. A verdict of death from " Natural Causes" was returned. The funeral took place on Saturday afternoon when the body was interred in the Sailors' grave at Runcorn Cemetery. The Rev. Lambert (senior chaplain of the Mersey Mission to Seamen) was the officiating clergyman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HOMICIDE IN THE UNITED STATES.</head>
        <p>HOMICIDE IN THE UNITED STATES.</p>
        <p>STARTLING FIGURES.</p>
        <p>The statistics of homicide in the United States, annu- ally collected by the Chicago •1 Tribune," have just been issu.!d in relation to the past year 1895. From these it appears that the numberof murders (including homicide) in the States attained last year the unprecedented figure of 10,500, as compared with 9,800 in 1894, 4,290 in 1890, and 1,808 in 1885. Hence the increase of homicidal crime is of a most rapid and serious nature. The suicides in 1895 numbered 5,750, as compared with 4,912 in 1894, 2,040 in 1890, and 973 in 1885. The legal executions 1895 were 132, beinu the same as in 1894, whereas in 1890 they were 102 and 108 in 1885. The lynchings or illegal executions were 171 in 1895, as compared with 194 in 1894, 127 in 1890, and 108 m 1885. Hence there was a decrease of 23 last year as compared with 1894. The number of murders increases in far greater proportion than the executions. The legal executions for the 1,808 murders in the year 1885 were 103, whilstlfor the 10,500 murders in 1895 the executions were 132.</p>
