      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL OLD MEN'S SUPPER.—The annual old men's supper took place at the Norton Arms Hot6l, on Tuesday night. Tables were laid for forty, and an excellent repast was provided by Mr. Brich, the landlord. The following gentlemen acted as carvers: Rev. G. D. Wray, and Messrs. T. Wright, J. Stokes and H. Rowe. It is interesting to note that the oldest guest was of the ripe age of 77 years. Those whose infirmities prevented them from being present, were not forgotten, a liberal supply of the good things being sent to their homes. Dr. Car- ruthers sent a letter expressing regret at not being able to be there, and wishing all a pleasant evening and a happy new year. After supper, the men were supplied with pipes, tobacco, and punch. The well-known Pro- fessor Miller had been specially engaged to give an ex- hibition of his conjuring tricks, talking dolls, and Punch and Judy show. Mr. John Leach contributed the songs " Mind the Paint "and " The Midehipinite," which Mr. J. S. Jeacock accompanied on the piano. Mr. Wright proposed "The Queen," which toast was received with a touch of sadness on account of the recent death of Prince Henrr of Battenberg. Mr. Stokes proposed " The Bishops, and clergy of all denominations, and the vicar in particular. The Vicar proposed " The founders of the annual supper and Mr. Wright." In reply, Mr. Wright said that it was the 27th anniversary of the supper, and he was the only living person who was pre- sent at the first one. 'there had only been three deaths in their ranks during the year, which was not a very great mortality. The proceedings terminated at 10 o'clock lby the singing of the national anthem. The following is a list of the subscribers to the fund :—Sir J. T. Brunner, M.P., Sir R. M. ond Lady Brooke, Dr. Car- ruthers, Rev. G. D. Wray, Mrs. Cox, and Messrs. C. Reynolds, J.P., H. Reynolds, J.P., J. Barrow, J.P.. J. Johnstone, J. Stokes, W. Nixon, H. Rowe, C. Birch, and T. Wright. THE WESLEY GUILD.—On Monday evening, Mr. B. Crosby, oh Runcorn, delivered a very interesting address on "spectacles," which was very much appreciated. The chair was occupied by Mr. Samuel Tomlinson, sen. The attendance was as follows e---19 members, 5 com- panions, and 2 friends. WESLEYAN BAND Or HOPE.—A miscellaneous enter- tainment was given by the members, in the schoolroom. on Tuesday night. Mr. Woodier occupied the chair. Mr. James Looker gave an address. There was a large audi- ence and a good programme of songs, readings, recita- tions, etc., was gone through. - Miss Boys acted as sc. companist. A collection was taken. PAROCHIAL TEA MEETING.—Tbe annual parochial tea. meeting was held in the 'Girls' School on Thursday week, when about 200 persons were present. Trays were given by Lady Brooke. Mesdames Wray. Cox,Carruthers. e Evans, Percival, J. H. Horton, Stokes, H. Rynolds, Wrieht. T. Horton, Pritchard, F. Finch. Ackele., ,Birch. W. Nixon, Manning,C. Reynolds,Walker, and the Misses Brereton, Ward, anti Grice. Half trays were given by Mesdames Harrison, Walker, Leach, A. Walker, Walton, Hulme, Ashley, Acton, Fryer, Woodward, Finch, J. W.. Haddock,T. Houghton. Blain, B.Haddock, ated Gumon, the Misses Johnstone, Leech, Jones. Jeacock, and Short. and Mr. H. Paten. After tea the Vicar,Rev. G.D.WraY, addressed his parishioners, reviewing the work of the church during the past year, and speaking very highly of the valuable services rendered by the lay-workers. Later on there was a miscellaneous entertainment, in which the following persons took part :—Miss Walker, Messrs.Griffiths and Watson (of Manchester),and Messrs. J.Leach, H. Walton, and T.Floughton. A most enjoyabla time was spent,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>TAKING COLD.</head>
        <p>TAKING COLD.</p>
        <p>A" Cold " is not necessarily the result of a high or ow temperature. A person may go from a hot bath directly into a cold one, or into snow even, and not take cold. He may remain out in the coldest atmosphere until chilled through, and still not take cold. On the contrary,he may take cold by pouring a couple of table- spoonfuls of water upon some parts of his dress, or by standing in a door, or before a stove, or by sitting near a window or other opening, where one part of the body is colder than another part. Let it be kept in mind that uniformity of temperature over the whole body is the great thing to be looked after. It is the unequal heat upon ainerent parts of the body that produces Colds, by disturbing the uniform circulation of the blood, which in turn induzes congestion of some part. Keep the feet always dry and warm, avoid draughts of air, keep the system braced up by plenty of sleep and good food, and by a powerful Tonic, and you will be proof against a Cold and its results. The very best Medicine, either for reventing or curing a Cold, is</p>
        <p>OWILYM EVAN'S QUININE BITTERS, THE VEGETABLE TONIC, Which is the most perfect Vegetable Remedy known,and the most efficacious to strengthen the constitution, en- liven the drooping or deprested spirits,brace the Nerves, and remove all Disorders of the Stomach which are caused by Indigestion, such as Loss of Appetite, distress after Eating, Acidity of Stomach. Eructations, Heaviness and Langour. Disorders or the Liver are often caused by defective Digestion. The potency ot the gastric juice in the process of Digestion is wonderfully aided by the powerful ingredients contained in</p>
        <p>GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS, THE VEGETABLE TONIC. While the Stomach recovers its normal tone, the putrid undigested food is cleared away, and the poor dyspeptic is relieved of his load of misery, once more enjoys good health, and looks forward to happiness and long life.</p>
