      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A YOUTH'S DARING RIDS—MELANCHOLY STORY. At the Warrington Court before Messrs. Garnett, Smith, and Guest, on Tuesday an inoffensive looking youth of 18 named Robert Craven, was charged with riding on the London and North Western Railway from Chester to Warrington without paying his fare on Mon- day night ; he was further charged with endangering his life by riding on the top of a carriage.—The night stationmaster at Bank Quay said that at 2.30 that morn- ing (Tuesday) he had his attention drawn to something on the top of the coach and found defendant there. It was a wander he had not been knocked off in passing through the tunnels. He told witness that he had no ticket, and added that his father was the manager of Flint papermills.—The Chief Constable said he did net think defendant was " altogether right." In answer to questions from the bench, defendant said he had not been working for 18 months. He had to leave the Flint papermills as he was suspected of having set fire to them. His stepmother had turned him away from home as she had nine children and his father five. He further said he had been in a reformatory school and also in the Denbigh Asylum. The Chairman asked him if he thought his parents would come if they were written to. Defen- dant answered that he did not know but did not think his father cared much about him.—The magistrates re- manded him until Friday in order that enquiries might be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>VIOLENT WATCH•SNATCHER.</head>
        <p>A VIOLENT WATCH•SNATCHER.</p>
        <p>Before the Magistrates at the Warrington Borough Court, on Tuesday, Isaac Smith, a rough-locking fellow with a bad record,was charged with frequenting Arpley- at., with intent to commit a felony, on Monday.—The Chief Constable stated that a young man named John Arthur Hatton, 18 years of age and a cripple, was com- ing along tha road past Arpley Station towards the Norton Arms when he saw the defendant and another man approaching him. Just as he was level with them the defendant made a snatch at his watch-guard and tried to pull the watch out of his pocket, but the chain broke. Hatton managed to run away from the prisoner and reached Bridge Foot where he informed a constable. —John Arthur Hatton, Booth's Hill, Lymm, supported this statement. He added that he said nothing to de- fendant and he did not know him. Be was a cripple, being paralysed down one side. There was no one about to render assistance until he informed P.C. Clough, with whom he returned and pointed out defendant.— P.C.Clough stated that he apprehended defendant in Wilson Patten-street. He was violent and tried to get sway twice. Witness had to handcuff him and bring bim to the Police Station. He had since charged him and he replied that he had been drinking whiskey and did not remember anything. In witness's opinion he had had drink but knew perfectly well what he was doing.—Detective Monaghan said that defendant was a convicted thief and had 12 convictions against him for various offences.—The magistrates sent him to gaol for two months' hard labour. A MAN WITH A LONG LIST AGAINST HIM._</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor (Councillor Fairclough) in the chair. Alderman Harrison and Mr. .1. W. Hayward. a man named Thomas Wall.apparently about 34 years of age, and residing in Union-street.was brought up on remand - charged with having stolen from the person of William Norbury on the 27th July last.in Hill-street a silver lever watch and steel guard, valued at £3 10s.—The Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot) seated that the prosecutor was a farm labourer living at High Legh.and had come to Warrington on the day named presumably for pleasure. In the afternoon he entered the Higher Angel Inn. Buttennarket-at., and had some beer' but it must ' not be understeod that he tcok so much as to become drunk. About 5 o'clock he left the Higher Angel and went into Hill-st., where probably his "lower angel" prompted him to stop and listen to a barrel organ which was being played. He shurtly afterwards returned to the public-house, but on arriving there immediately missed his watch and guard. He reported the affair to the police, and a. search was made, with the result that the watch was found to have been pledged at Houghton's by the prisoner an hour after it had been missed. k hunt for Walsh revealed the fact that he had absconded, but on Sunday afternoon, the 19th inst., he was met and apprehended by Detective-Sergeant Monaghan in Scotland-road. He then said that he had "come back to get it over," and though he at first pleaded that be had" found it," he subsequently admitted stealing it from the prosecutor, adding that he had pawned it him- self and had no confederates whatever.—Prisoner expressed contrition for the offence, and appealed to the Bench for leniency, saying that he had stolen the articles when drunk. If he had not been drunk he would never have done it. In fact he did not know what he WAS doing at the time.—The Chief Constable remarked that prisoner had been 22 times before various courts, for felony, uttering base coin, drunkenness, assault, and begging.—The prisoner said it was 25 years since he had been in that court.on a charge of felony, and then it was for stealing apples. He would promise the Bench that if dealt leniently with he would reform, for he had come back for the purpose of " getting it over and living a new life.—Mr. Hayward asked the Chief Constable if he could not say something in the prisoner's favour.—Mr. Talbot replied that all he could say was that the prisoner had not given the police much trouble. When he said that, it was all, but it must be borne in mind that that favour had been shown by his (the Chief Constable) not applying for a committal. The prisoner was a travelling pugilist, and such a man who could steal a watch and chain as he had done was to be regarded as a dangerous character.—The Bench committed the prisoner to gaol for three menthe with hard labour. CEILD NEGLECT CASES.—DISOI7STING REVELATION . At the Warrington Borough Police Court,on Thursday. before the Mayor (Councillor Fairclougb), Colonel Ridg way, Alderman Sutton, and Mr. S. Chandley, Patrick Sheening and Charlotte Sheening, 3, School-brow,-,were charged with having on the 18th January and during the past six months neglected their children, Martin, Elizabeth, and John, all under 16 years of age.—The female defendant pleaded guilty.—lnspector M'Ateer, Tf.S.P.C.C., stated that he had visited the defendant's house on two occasions. On the first occasion he found the children in a filthy condition, insufficiently clothed, and in delicate health. In one bedroom there was only one bed with a tick for five of them. On the 23rd he visited the house and found the woman under the in- fluence of drink. Defendant said he was in receipt of 255. a week and he gave his wife a £l. The eldest son gave her 10s. and a daughter 4s. Altogether they were in receipt of about £2 3s. 6d. weekly and paid ls.a week rent. The Inspector asked for the woman to be dealt with severely as it was she who was to blame and not the husband. On their worships' permission he would</p>
        <p>CARTER'S LITTLE 'V- 'LLS.</p>
      </div>
