      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>withdraw the charge against him.—P.C. F. Cooper gave corroborative evidence and added that ever since he had known the woman, the last nine years, she had been drunken and quarrelsome.—P.C. Evans also supported the testimony both as to the neglect and the woman,s chPracter.—Mrs. Sheening said in defence " I am sorry, but I will turn myself over and be a different woman al- together."—She was sent to gaol for 1 month with hard labour.—James King and Sarah King, 72, Lythgoes-lane were charged with neglecting their four children,James, Richard, William, and Sarah, on the 27th January.— Mrs. King admitted the offence.—lnspector M'Ateer said i on the 27th inst. in consequence of a complaint,he visited I defendants' house in company with P.C. Meacock, and found the female defendant and fur children in the back kitchen. The children he examined were in a filthy con- dition. On the clothes of James being opened the ver- min dropped out, and Richard's body was covered with vermin bites. William, aged seven, vt as in a similar condition and almost naked. Sarah, aged 4, was sitting on a chair with only a torn chemise on. He asked Mrs. King to account for it and she said she could not tell how it was. In on- room there was a wooden bedstead with an old flock tick upon it. The inspector concluded by saying that he had not seen a worse case since he joined the sockty, for in the front room was a bucket filled with excrement, and which had not apparently been emptied for days. The stench was so bad that it turned the stomach of the constable with him.—P.C. Meacock sup- ported the inspector's statement. It made him ill to enter the house and the windows had to be opened before they could go in the trout bedroom—King pleaded loss of work as an excuse. Both defendants were committed for one month's hard labour, and the children were conveyed to the workhouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MIN OR CASES.</head>
        <p>MIN OR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE OU lIT FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Councillor li6berts and Alderman Wright. USING CORPORATION WATER WITHOUT PERMISSION.— Thomas and Walter Cross, builders, West-street.were charged with osing Corporation water without having first received permi Mon to do so, on the 7th January.—The Town Clerk stated that defendant Ind made an explanation to him which he was prepared to accept. Defendant, Walter Cross worked at the Forge night and day and the water must have been abstracted without his sanction. Ile (the Town Clerk) did not wish to press the case but brought it tip more as a warn- ing to builders of the town,—Mr. Walter Cross state I that the water had beet used while he was at the forge and during the absence of the foreman whom he had left in charge of the job. -The magistrates inflicted a penalty of 103. and coats (18s. in all), and warned defendant that a repetition of the offence would bring the full penalty of £5 and costs. CRUELTY TO artataLs.—Edward Isherwood. contractor. Tanner's-lane, was charged with having on the 10th January, ill-treated a horse by working it in an unfit state.—He pleaded guilty.—lnspector Bilson, E.S.P.C.A., said on the date named he saw the horse being driven. It was lame in both fore-feet. He ordered it to be taken out and this was at once done. Mr. Isherwood had explained to him that he was sorry the horse had been taken out and promised to get rid of it. This he had done, and witness there4re did not wish to press the case at all.—Defendant was .Itschsf'gel on payment of costs.—Rdward Lafferty. Stretton, was charged with cruelly illtreating a cow by beating the same on the 13th January. He pleaded guilty, and the Inspector in proving the case said he thought the defendant was merely hurr, iag the animal for a sale at the "Eagle and Child," and had no cruel intention. He had also borne a good char- acter for seven years.—On payment of the costs this defend- ant was also dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>MoNDlY.—Betore the Mayor (Councillor _Fairclaugh), Alder</head>
        <p>MoNDlY.—Betore the Mayor (Councillor _Fairclaugh), Alder</p>
        <p>man Harrison, and Mr. J. W. Hayward. DRUNKENNESS.—For this offence the underm -ntioned per- sons were dealt with as follows :—John Heaton (16th ap- pearance), Julia Anti Shields (30th appearance), a month's hard labour each ; Mary Grimes, James Grady. Micha 1 Murphy, Thomas Pannell, and James Walsh were fined in various amounts RAILWAY STATION LOAFERS. Patrick Magrath and Thomas Baguley. described as " cornermen." were sum- moned for obstructing the free p tssage of the footpath in Winwick•st. by standing thereon en the 26th inst.—P.C. Cooper said the 4efendants were loitering about the Central Station from eleven o'clock until 1.40. He asked them to go away, " but they simply laughed at him." and he was compelled to report them.—The Chief Constable said he had received many complaints about persons loitering near the Central Station.—Mr. Bennett.ticket collector at Bank Quay Railway Station, deposed to the misconduct of Baguley at that station on Sunday.—The Chief Constable stated that B iguley•had been sent to gaol for three months for cutting and wounding.—Baguley : This is enough to make a man eonamit a robbery or a murder. We cannot get a living.—The Mayor : You are incorrigible, both of you —Atter some dis- cussion the case was adjourned for a month on the under- stand.ng that the defendants left the town and never returned to it. CHIMNEYS ON FIRE.—John Barnes and John Hardman were each lined is. for allowing their house chimneys to be on fire. DEFRAUDING THE RAILWAY COMPANY.—John Shaw was charged with travelling o the London and North-Western Railway from St. Helens to Warrington on Saturday without having previously paid his fare.—Detective Wallace proved the case and said defendant admitted the offence and said when apprehended he thought he might be able to get through by saying he had lost his ticket He did not intend to pay and further said that it was too wet and he was too tired to walk.—Prisoner had served several terms of penal servitude. Including one for five years, another of seven, and one for 12 months.—A penalty of 4J5. and costs was imposed or in de- fault one month's hard labour. CARELESS CARTERS. —Samuel Allen John Charles Hough- ton, and Alfred Stringer were all chorged with being such a distance from their horses on Knutstord-road on the 22nd January as to have no control over them.—PC. Bell proved the case, and said that all three carts were on the wrong side of the road.—Defendants were severely admonished by the Mayor and fined ss. each. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), Mr. IL J. Guest, and Dr. T. S. Smith. WANTED IN YORIESHIRE.—Two men, Reuben Solley and Thomas Chambers, apparently of the labouring class were brought up as being wanted in the West Riding of Yorkshire for stealing fowls.—Herbert Hartley. sergeant in the West Riding constabulary, said that the robbery was committed on the 15th July. They had one man in custody at Rother- ham, and he and Setgeant Monaghan apprehended the two defendants on Monday.—The men were left in charge of Ser- g ant Hntley. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.—The following persons were deal with fur not sending their children regularly to school : John Litherland, labourer. 4 Silver-street, fined 25.; Wm. Ryder, forgeman. 6 Brwood-street. fined Zs. 6d. THURSDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Lieut.-Colonel Ridgway, Alderman Sutton, and Mr. S. Chandley. SCHOOL CASES.—For not sending their children regularly to school, the following persons were prosecuted by Mr. W. Jones. school attendance officer :—John Coyne, Silve?-squire, fined 2s. 6d. • Patrick Carey, 347 Winwick-road. Si.; John Caldwell, 18 .Laira-st., boy ordered to be birched.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PE ['TY SESSIONS. TUXBDA.Y. —Before Messrs. W. Long (in the chair), H.</p>
        <p>Tinsley, H. Talbot, C. H. Broadbent: and John White. DIVERSION OP 1 V.:OTPATR.—Mr. S. Pope. solicitor, of Manchester, applied, on behalf of the London and North- Western Railway Company. for power to divert the foetr.ath over the line on a level crossing in the township of Keekwick. He said that under a private Act of 1894, in sec:ion 13, they had power to stop up and discontinue the footpath which crossed their property, and to construct a footbridge in lieu of same. There was one point, however. which Mr. John Ashton, clerk to the Rural District Council of Runcorn. bid raised. On each side of the line there is what is known as a " clap-gate." As the railings were brought dawn to these gates the opeaings wee very narrow, and these ought to be enlarged, or else the gates removed altogether. Mr. Ashton had 'Lid the openings were only two feet wide ; but he (Mr. Pope) thought the railway company would do their utmost to make it convenient to the Rural District Council and the public generally.—The application was granted. A DINGERetre Doa.—John Henry Arnold, of Appleton, , was charged with having had in his possession on the 18th January, and other dates, a dog which was not fit to be at large owing to its savage temper.—Mr. Charles George, of Appl,ton, stated that on the 18th and the 24th January he was attackek by the dog. which tore nis clothing in several _places. He (witness) hail never teased the animal.—P.C. -Brew also bore testimony to the dog's viciousness.—Defend- ant wa, ordered to keep the dog under proper control, or it would have to be destroyei. SLEEPY DRIVEHS.—JosepIi Hardman and Thomas White- head were each fined Sc. includiug costs for having been found asleep whilst in charge of horses and drays, at Apple- ton. on divers dates. DRUNKENNEISS —For this offence at Gr .ppenhall, on the 28th December, Elizabeth Johnson was fined 2s. 6d,</p>
        <p>NEWTON rErrY SESSIONS. SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. R. Stone (in tie chair). J. W Emmett, C. B, F. Borron, S. S. Brown, and Colonel J. L.</p>
        <p>Wood. A BACKMAN ASSAULTED. —Henry Harrison and Louisa Harrison. husband and wife, were charged with having assaulted Charles Stuart at Haydock, on the 10th January.— Mr. R. Stuart prosecuted, and Mr. Riley appeared for the defendants.—The evidence went to show that complainant was what was known as a packman. It was his business to call at caftan houses for payments. On the day in question he visited the house occupied by defendants. On asking for SOMA payments which were due to him, for business trans- acted, complainant was insulted. A few words ensued, and defendants struck complainant several times on the chest and head.—Mr, Riley, in defence, absolutely denied the assault, but defendants were subsequently fined ss. and costs each. VIOLATIONS OF THE WEIGHTS AND MEASURES ACT. --The following persons were each fined lOs and costs for having bad in their possession on the 10th January for the purpose of trade scales which were not stamped I—James Barton, Newton; George Lightfoot. Newton; William Lythgoe, Newton ; and Harry Green, Newton.—For having had a pair of unjust scales in her possession on the same de. e, Ellen Rigbey, Newton, was fined 2s. 6d. and costs. AN IMPROVED HOME.—George Bannon, of Blackbrook, Haydock. was charged with having neglected his children on the 6th November and other dates.—Mr. Riley appeared for the defendant.—Inspector McAteer, of the N.S.P.C.O said that the defendant was before that court on the 28th December on the present charge. At that time the Bench thougnt it judi lions to adjourn the case for one month. He (the inspec- tor) was now pleased to state that he had visited the defend- ant's house several times since tat date, and had found an improvem„nt each time. The home and children were now quiet comfortable, and he respectfully suggested that the summons against the defendant should be withdrawn, on condition that he kept the home and children clean and corn- fortab!e in the future.—Mr. Riley corroborated what the Inspector had said with reference to the state of the home,and the summons was Withdrawn. BREACH OF THE PEACE.—James Smith, James Thompson, ' and John Thompson, were charged with committing a breach of the peace by fighting at Golborne on the 17th inst.— Defendants were bound over to keep the peace for three months in the sum of £5 each. A WARRINGTON YOUTH DEALT WITH FOR THEFT.—A youth named Patrick Teyrell, aged,lB years. living in Silver-street, Warrington. was brought up on remand from the 18th inst.</p>
        <p>10 Al. .THERIS.—Mrs. WisloW s Soothing byrup Is bee used over fifty years by millions of mothers for theirchildren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poon sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it producer natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and ths little cherub awakes "as bright as a button." Of all chemists, is. I.d. per bottle.—ildvt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 1, 1896.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <p>charged with having stolen a quantity of iron fencing, value 55.. the property of William Bennett, of Lyndhurst, Poulton. Prisoner admitted the offence. It transpired that at noon on the day in question the youth was seen by Peter Goulden, of Crossley-street, trying to pelt some feucing out of the broils which devides the borough from the county neat Bruche Bridge. tioulden interrupted him by saying that if he took the iron away he would be " tocked up." and tla it if he wanted to buy or beg it he should go to Mr. William Bennett. To this Teyrell replied that he would see Mr. Bennett about it. and 'he went towards Mr. Bennett's house, taking his handcart with hint. Being suspicious Goulden informed P.C. Millington—of the borough constabu- lary—what he had seen, and that officer subsequently stopped the prisoner in Marshhouse-lane. Having searched the I handcart he found the iron fencing, which had been hidden from view by a quanitity of rags being thrown over it. Milling- ton communicated with Sergeant Barnes. oi the county poli.a force, and the prisoner was handed over to P C. Heath.— Teyrell was dealt with under the Fist Offenders Act, and ordered to come up for judgm,mt when called upon. DRUNKENNESS.—The following persons weie each fined 2s. 65. and costs for having been guilty of drunkenness and disorderly behavour at divers times :—Ellen Powell, Newton; Mary Broderick, Hay,tock ; Cornelius Davis, Ha,ydock ; and Peter Leyland. Golborne.—For a similar offence William Jones, Haydock was tined ss. and costs. DOG AT LARGE.—For having allowed his dog to run at large unmuzzled, George Richardson, Ilayclock, Was fined 3s. 65. CHEFT OF BEEF.—A man named Thomas Donough Was ' charged with having stolen a piece_ of beef, value 95., the property of Eliza Clarke, op the 17th inst., at Newton.— William Clarke said he Was a butcher, rod resided with his mother at 35, Market-street, Newton, where they had a shop. He saw the piece of beef at 7.33 on the night in question. when it was in the shop window. He did not see it again until eleven o'clock, when he saw it in the possessi.m of P.C. Bullen, who at that time had prisaner in his custody. The beef he identified as his mother's property, and he valued it at 93.—P.C. Bullen stated that he was on duty in Market- street, on the night in question, when he saw th- prisoner take the piece of meat from the window, He (witness) at once gave chase, and prisoner threw the meat into the street, where it was picked up by P.C. Green. When apprehended and charged with the thieft prisoner replied" Not guilty."— P.C. Green corroborated, alter which prisoner was committed to take his trial at the Liverpool Quarter Session.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>- MoNnAY.—Before Mr. Philip_ Speakrnan (chairman) and Mr</p>
        <p>E. Imison. CAUSING A CHILD TO BEG. —Mien Gandy, Salkeld's Squar, was summoned for unlawfully causing her child, Kate Coffeye six years of age, to beg alms in Robert-street on the 15th January.— Defendant pleaded guilty.—Mary Ann Allen gave evidence as to the child co iiing to her house in Robert-street to beg. From the statement made by the girl she took her to ttie Police Statioh.—P.S. Brewster stated that in company with Sergeant Gill he visited the house of Ike defendant where he found her sitting btfore a fire playing dominoes with her husband. In answer to his questions. the defendant stated that she had sent the child out. He (Sergeant Brews- ter) had seen the child in the streets four days in succession. but he had been unable to find out where she lived. She was an illegitimate child, and had been to Sergeant Gill's house begging.—F. Sergeant Gill gave corroborative evidence.— Superintendent Okell informed the Bench that the defendant had been dealt with at that Court, nineteen times.—She was committed to prison for fourteen days' hard labour. LOITERERS ON THE FOOTPATH.--ifenry Moores (15), John Dutton (13). Arthur White (15), of Princess .st., were charged with obstructing the footpath in Regent-st., by loitering thereon on the 20th January.—P.C. Gregory proved the case. —Supt Okell stated that he had received complaints from tradesmen with regard to thl obstruction of footpaths.—The Chairman reprimanded the defendants, and lined them the costs. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—EIizabeth Parker. an old offender, was clsarg-d with being drunk and disorderly in Merse•-st., on the 17th January.— rho case was stated- by P.O. Gregory. She was committed to prison for one month'ir hard labour without the option of a fine. On being taken down to the cell she shouted in a loud voice that she wished " Gregory might drop detd."—William Green, for being drunk and disorderly in Churcia-st., was fined ss. Including costs SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST I YOUTR.—John Alfred Dona. a youth. 17 years of age, of Westin Point. was ordered to take his trial at Chester Assizes, on the charge of attempting to commit a rape on Selina Miles, a child, 4 years of age, at Weston Point, the previous evening.—tividence was given by Mrs. Miles. wife of Alfred Miles, salt boiler. Post Office Risw, Weston Point, her two children, P.C. Bancr)ft, aad Dr. McDougall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>FOOTbALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTbALL.</p>
        <p>CRICKETBRS should scquite the " PockEt Diary and Companion," issued by the well-known Sports Nlanti- fa .turers, GEORGE G. EussEr &amp; Co„ Ptckham, London. All the information Cricketers require is found therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet it will go in the waistcoat pocket. Leather bound, gilt edges. 6d. ; postage, 14. If you are not satisfied, G. G. B. 8; Co. will return the price.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HULL.</p>
        <p>The return match between these teams took place on Saturday, at Hull, and a more depressing day it would be difficult to have. True it did not rain in torrents like the day when Oldham were met,but the Hull ground was covered with a mist which rendered it almost im- possible to distinguish the individual players when they were on the opposite side of the field to the spectator. The field had been protected by straw.but the rain which had fallen copiously during the day had soaked it, and the result was that the ball was so greasy that it inter- fEred very much with the play. It is ten years since Warrington last visited Hull,and on that occasian.singu- tarty to state the result was exactly as on Saturday, viz., Hull two tries, Warrington one try. Hull won at Warrington at the second match of the present season by nine points to three after the most uninteresting match which has been seen at Warrington under the au,pices of the Northern Union. The match on Satur- day was not very much more interestingebut it was no doubt due to the state of the ground. Barber was un- able to accompany the team.and Scott took his place at threequarter, Nevins taking command of the team. Owing to the length of the journey,the Warrington team I had to leave the Central Station at 91.1 a.m. on Satur- , day,and did not arrive at Hull until one o'clock. They wera taken to the field in a waggonette but on the way a dire disaster almost befel them, for the axle cracked hut just before the crack had gone completely through I it was fortunately discovered, and the occupants of the 1 waggonette got out in double quick time. The rest of the journey was made in penny 'buses. Some of the party facetiously said that that was a premonition of I the result of the match. Mr. Carr, ef Batley, was the I appointed referee but as .he bad not pleased the Hull crowd a few weeks previously he did not come,and Mr. I Emmott, of Leeds,took his place. This gentleman did not adhere strictly to the new rules and the result was that the Hull half backs were continually running round the scrimmage, and yet were scarcely ever penal- ized. The following were the teams which did duty for each side : • Warrington : Heesom. full back; Lawless. Scott, Cross, and Carey, threequarters ; Foden and Higgin- botham, half backs: Taylor, Nevins (captain). Swift. Thornton, Andrews, Barrett, Fairhurst, and Burrows, II forwards. I Hull : Johnson, full back; Lampriere (captain). Griffin, Wad, and Mahoney, threequarters ; C. Town- end and J. Townend, half backs ; Doskin, Hamer, Man- self, Plugge, Wiles, Feetham, Barker and Jackets, for- wards. Nevins lost the toss, but this was of very little conse- quence for there was not much wind blowing. Taylor kicked off and there being a poor return the first scrim- mage took place in the Hull quarters. Foden smartly got possession and passing to Cross, the latter found touch at the 25 flag. A combined dribble by the Hull forwards took play to the opposite end where they found Heesom too quick for them. They were again on the warpath and seemed to mean business, when a good mark was made by Lawless who found touch near the centre. Lampriere here obtained possession and by a smart run and kick he regained all lost ground and put Hull on the aggressive. Mahoney immediately afterwards was tackled on the line and Warrington had a very narrow escape. The ensuing scrimmage was carried by Warrington, and the quarter flag was reached when a very unjustifiable free kick was given against Warrington A scrimmage had been ordered, and the ball came out. Higginbotham rushed at it, and the referee penalised him for off-side, although the ball was out of the scrimmage. Johnson took the kick and although nothing tangible came of it, a hot attack was made on the Warrington stronghold, which was at length repelled by Foden. Another round of passing initiated by J. Townend and taken part in by Griffin' Read and Mahoney ended in the latter getting over, but the final pass was palpably for- ward, and thus the point was disallowed. Lawless kicked out, and in reply to Johnson's return, Heesom made his mark and sent well in touch. Barrett got away I with a dribble to the centre line,but a sudden and unex- pected reverse was sustained at this point by Warring- ton. C. Townend got possession and passing to his brother Jack, the whole of the Warrington team appealed on the ground that it was a forward pass. but the referee was deaf to the appeals, and J Townend -made his way through the whole of his opponents and got to the line before he was floored. Mansell who was by handy, snatched up the ball and scored a try, which Johnson was unable to improve. Lawless's drop out was replied to by another sudden rush of the Third Potters, and another try was just averted by the united efforts of Heesom, Cross, Scott and Lawless. Barrett, Fairhurst and Taylor came away with a fine dribble and Taylor made a strong run and passed to Foden, who repassed to Taylor and the latter was almost over before he was floored. Warrington now attacked strongly and Cross. Lawless and Carey were almost over in turn. Repeated offside tactics by the Hull halves were at length penalised and the ball was placed for Swift who very nearly landed a goal. Half time was called soon afterwards with Hull leading by a try to nil.</p>
        <p>DON'T BB DECEIVED.—Don't think the hair can be pre served without proper nourishment; all hair specialists positively assert that pure prease is absolutely necessary for the preservation of the hair and ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OIL is without doubt th. best. It nourishes the hair, pre- vents it faldng off or turning grey eradicates scurf, and arrests baldness. Also in a golden colour for fair hair. Bottles 3s. 64., 78., 10s. 64. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OlL.—Preserves the hair. RowLesm's MACASSAR OlL.—Preveuts baldness. ROWLAND'S MACASSAR Ols,.—Prevents scurf. RowLANn's MACASSAR OlL.—Beantities the hair. Ask anywhere for ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OIL, of 20, Hatton Garden and avoid Iheap, wort Mess imitations</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>Donkin re-started the game with a very poor kic tI and Scott soon got the ball back again. Mahoney kicked I to the centre where a scrimmage took place and Lam- priere getting possession,raced down the field and looked a certain scorer until Lawless finely tackled him. The scene of operations again changed and Johnson was tackled by Lawless under the goalposts, and an anxious time ensued for the Hullites, but do what they would. Warrington could not score, and the ball was got out of danger and Hull were just congratulating themselves upon the danger being passed, when suddenly a change came over the piece. A scrimmage was ordered beyond the centre line and Higginbotham getting possession passed to Foden. The latter rushed between the brothers Townend and every three quarter in turn tried to " down " him. They all unaccountably failed to do so, and presently Foden reached the lull back. Finding he could not get round this gentleman, he kicked over his head, and managed to again get posseision of the ball, and dribbled within a ya:d or two of the line before he was caught by Johnson. Cross, however, was handy, and he promptly deposited the oval over the line just outside the posts, thus scoring the first try which War- rington has yet ga ned in Yorkshire. The place was a very easy one, but the ball was i 3,4 slippery that Foden failed to emulate his deeds of the previous week,and the try was not converted. E tch side struggled fiercely, I too fiercely to gain the winning point, and some fine forward rushes were witnessed. On one occasion Scott dove for the ball and took it from the feet of the Hull team when a try seemed an absolute certainty. The game was stopped soon afterwards owing to Scott's knee coming in contact with Griffin's head, and the latter was compelled to retire from the field for the remainder of the game. This circumstance seemed if anything to infuse fresh vigour in the home team, and the ball was worked over the line, and before any ef the Warrington players could touch down or kick dead, Jack Towaend had dropped on it. The same player failed to kiek a goal from a very difficult place. The remainder of the game was in favour of Hull, but no more scoring was done, and thus Hull won by two tries to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUNCORN v. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the Runcorn team journeyed to Rudders. field, to play off the return engagement. Rain fell throughout the match, whilst a heavy mist enveloped the ground, and at times the players could not be dis- cerned from the press box. The ground, which had been covered all tne week in the event of frost, was in a wretched condition. This militated against all back play, consequently the game was of a forward nature nature throughout. Unfortunately the visitors could not play their full strength. W. Faulkner, Lightfoot, Taylor and Gayter all being " off." whilst the home contingent had their best available team. The following played for Runcorn—J. Butter worth,full back;T.Warder H. Myers. W. Gregory. and B. West,threequarter backs; G. Robinson and J. Faulkner,half backs ; J. Langley, H. Farmer, S. Walker, G. Moores, R. Reece, H. Darlington, and F. Richardson, forwards. There was a big gathering when the teams en'ered the enclosure. Runcorn met with a good reception. Lodge set the ball in motion for the homesters, no reply being made. The opening stages were fought in the Runcorn 25. Warder and West exchanged the venue with judicious play. and an exchange of kicks between France and Butterworth saw play at the centre. A. smart kick was well followed up by Lorriman,and the visitoes were forced back into their 25, where the home backs attempted a bout of passing, but the visitors' tackling was sure, and they relieved with useful kicking into touch. Sutcliffe and Wilson broke away with a strong dribble, but Butterworth stopped them grand:y. The same player soon after kicked smartly, and Myers further improved matters for Runcorn with a plucky run, but the home forwards more than neutralized with good footwork. Sutcliffe then headed a strong dribble to Butterworth, who fielded the ball well, but failed to get it away, and on the ball coming loose the Cestrians conceded a minor. Smith replied well to the kick not, and fluctuating play ensued fer a time. Ultimately the home front division came away with a burst, and scrimmages fol- lowed in the Runcorn quarters. Owing to the mist the players could not be distinguished. The visitors were awarded a free kick for off-side play, but the homesters dribbled beautifully, and Smith picked up close to the line and passed to Pearson, who sewed a try. France made a poor attempt at goal. Keen play ensued on re- suming, the tackling on both sides being strong. The home forwards showed their superiority with good foot- work, awl drove their opponents back to their "25.' Then T. Dickenson secured from the lire-out and ran well, but an'opponent took his pass. Pearson kicked well across, and put his forwards on side,and they broke away with a strong dribble to the mouth of the Runcorn goal. When the whistle blew for half-time the score was Huddersfield, one try, three points; Runcorn, nil. On changing ends Farmer kicked off. The home for- wards replied with a long dribble, but the visitors got away. From a rush near the line Gregory scored for Runcorn, and Myers placed a goal. Tne visitors played up smartly after the kick off, and an- other rush by the forwards saw the game being hotly contested on the home, lines. From a scramble,J.Faulit ner got over for Runcorn,butMyers' kick was unsuccess- ful. Robinson got off from a subsequent scrimmage,and- passing followed. but Boothroyd secured and kicked to Butterworth, who was applauded for a grand kick into touch. Dickenson dribbled quickly up the field, but Butterworth sent the ball into touch. Chambers was next prominent witn a dribble, but the homesters could not make much headway, Butterworth kicking with good judgment drove them back. France then had his kick charged, and things looked dangerous for Hulderafield, but Boothroyd getting possession ran half the length of the field. The homesters made strenuous efforts, and France ran strongly and passed to Pearson, who scored, but Sutcliffe's kick was a poor one. Result—Runcorn, one goal, one try—eight points ; Huddersfield, two tries —six points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WIDNES v. WIGAN.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Played on the Lowerhobse ground. Widnes on Satur- day, before 4,000 spectators, about 400 of whom trav- elled from Wigan. The ground was in a sloppy state. Result : Widnes one dropped goal (four paints), Wigan one try (three points).</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE v. CHESHIRE. The first of the replayed inter-county matches be- tween the clubs forming the Northern Union was decided on Wednesday on the Broughton Rangers' ground,Lower Broughton. Upwards of 5,000 spectators were present. There were changes in the teams selected, Sudlow (St. Helens) taking Taylor's place at wing three-quarter back for Lancashire ; while Houghton, the international, was the Cheshire tail back instead of Saville, and Butter- worth and Moore played at three.qua.rter back and for- ward respectively for Warder and Mottershead. The first half was splendidly contested, and neither side could claim any advantage at half time. Cleminson kicked off, and the ball rapidly travelled from end to end. Good combination was shown by the Lancashire backs, and ground was gained by their efforts, but the Cheshire tackling was too good to allow of scoring,Wors- ley being especially prominent, whilst Houghton played faultlessly at full back. Cheshire narrowly missed scor- ing just before half time,Mysrs, Butterworth, Sands,and Worsley rushing the ball from their half of the field to the Lancashire goal line, where one of the home players kicked it dead. A five yards scrummage was formed, and Houghton kicked the ball over thc goal line and sent it dead. Half time was then called, and ends were changed. %V. Faulkner resumed play, and Lancashire at once commenced to force the game. A. Lees in the first few minutes got possession from a scrummage and passed to Chapman, who ran and passed to Sudlow.but after running to within a few yards of the Cheshire goal he was stopped by Worsley. The ball was, however, dribbled over,and Butterworth luckily kicked it dead as Chapman was falling on it. After the drop out, the attack on the visitors was resumed by Varley, A. Lees, Chapman,S. Lees. and Barber, and a passing run by the same men to Sudlow on the other wing taxed the Cheshire defence very severely. Then S. Lees took a drop at the visitors' goal, which just failed to score. On resuming from the quarter mark, Barber dribbled nicely to the Cheshire half, and Chapman If d a strong rush to the quarter. Here Sands intercepted a pass by one of the Lancashire halves and ran back to the centre. Another combined run by A. Lees, Varley, Chapman, and Sudlow again put pressure on the visitors,who conceded another minor point. Then Walker, Moores,Myers,and Robinson retaliated,and Lan- cashire were for a short time placed on the defensive Worsley, from a pass by Sands, making a good but an successful attempt to score. Coop got possession and sent the ball to neutral ground, from whence it was I scrummaged close to the Cheshire goal line. Two fine attempts were made by the Lancashire backs to get round their rivals,but touches in goal were the (Lily re- sults of these efforts. At length A. Lees passed to Variey from whom the ball went to Chapman, who, instead of passing out to Sudlow on the wing, ran finely through his opponents, and, amid loud cheering, grounded the ball over the Cheshire goal line. Coop took a difficult place kick without success. Cheshire made a good effort to get on level terms with their rivals,but only succeeded in forcing a minor point. Result : Lancashire, one try (three points) ; Cheshire, nil. Teams : Lancashire : T. Coop (Leigh), full back ; F. Barber (Warrington), S. Lees (Oldham), H. Chapman (Broughton Rangers), and T. Sudlow (St. Helens). threequarter backs; U. Varley (captain), and . Lees (Oldham), half backs; IV. Briers (St. Helens), J. Yates (Wigan), T. Cleminson, and W. Robinson (Broughton Rangers, J. Johnson (Widnes), P. Taylor (Leigh),T. Uttley (Rochdale Hornets), and E. Bonsor (Oldham), forwards. Cheshire : S. Houghton (Runcorn), full back; J. Butterworth and H. Myers (Runcorn), and P. Sands and J. Worsley (Stockport), three-quarter backs ; G.Robinson and J. Faulkner (Run- corn), half backs ; W. Faulkner (captain), S. Walker, J. Langley, H. Farmer,G. Moore (Runcorn), T. Gibson, C. Wrigley, and J. Roberts (Stockport), forwards). Re- feree : Mr. W. Hutchinson (Wakefield).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN " A " v. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S.—Played at Runcorn on Saturday. before a moderato attendance, in dirt:' weather. The homesters started,a return being made to the centre. Runcorn soon got in the visitors' territory, but Burrows relieved with a good dribble and transferred play to the home half. A free kick by Light- foot gained little ground, Nickson eventually sending to touch. Nickson soon afterwards broke away, but was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>brought down in mid-field. The visitors dribbled well' into the Runcorn half, and Warburton and Mason fol- lowed up. The ball Was kicked over the line, and Mason rushing up scored. Flampson took the kick, and though from a difficult position landed a magnificent goal. From the re-start Runcorn got in the St. Mary's half, where Hughes made his mark, but his kick was charged and followed up; but Jolley dodged his oppon- ents in fine style, and 'kicked into touch. The game was now stopped for a few minutes, owing to Jolley being hurt. On re-starting Runcorn at mice got to St. Mary's goal line, but the ball was soon got out, Jolley saving. Good passing between Nickson, Wilkinson, and Light- foot saw Runcorn attacking. The visitors found relief, ' but another rush by the homesters took play to the St. Mary's line. After a scrimmage Nickson got possession and dropped a neat goal. The visitors were now playing , on the defensive, Runcorn attacking strongly. Reece getting wissession dodged over the line and scored,Dun- bavin's kick at goal failing. Just before half-time Owen Hughes rushed over and scored Runcorn's second try. Dunbavin took the kick for goal, but the ball went out side. Flalf-time—Runcorn, one dropped goal and two tries; St. Mary's, one goal. After the interval the homesters invaded the St. Mary's half, and got to the goal line, where they had extremely hard luck in not scoring. The visitors' eventually got out into the home half. Siddall took a drop at goal,but the ball struck the upright below the bar and bounded back into play. Lightfoot got a way,but was overtaken and br4ught down. Owen Hughes had an unsuccessful try at goal from a free kick. The game was now fast and exciting, some good open play being shown on both sides. Another rush by Runcorn took play into the St. Mary's hale, the ball being kicked over the line, forcing Roughley to touch down. Matters were confined to mid- field for a while, but Nickson got hold and kicked t 3 Roughlev who replied with a kick into touch at the centre. The visitors pressed. but 0. Dunbavand took a fly kick, and following up dribbled to Roughley, who promptly kicked into touch. Runcorn again attacked, but the visitors got out again and dribbled,Jolley saving finely on two occasions. Good open play continued, 0. Hughes, F. Gayter, and Clarke being prominent among the home forwards. The homesters gradually gained ground ,and another rush by Runcorn ended in Wilkinson scoring, Dunbavin failing to improve. Final result— Runcorn, 1 dropped goal 3 tries; Warrington St. Mary's 1 goal.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " v. LEIGH "A."—Oa Saturday afternoon the Leigh " A "team visited Warrington to play their fixture with the local " A "team. Warring- ton were minus the services of Carey, Lawless, Scott and Foden, and the team was made up as follows : Full back, Spruce ; Broady, Bailey, Moores, and Foster, threequarters ; O'Brien and "Smack "Bate, half backs; G. Savage, Monks, Boyle, Hughes, E. Bailey, Saunders, Scholtze, and Proud, forwards. The game was played on the ground adjoining the rcad, and there were about 2,500 spectators. "Smack " was loudly cheered on ap- pearing on the field. Leigh kicked off and Warrington returned, and the game after traversing the field in both directions, settled down into a series of scrimmages and line outs. At last the ball got loose and was kicked towards the Warrington goal with acme of the visiting forwards following it up. Spruce secured, but was tackled near the line, and he lost possession of the ball. A score seemed certain, but just at the critical moment, Broady kicked dead. A spell of even play ensued which was terminated by Roberts scoring a try for Leigh in a good position. This was augumented into a goal. Broady. Bate and Saunders, then exchanged, but the ball was sent into touch . From another scrimmage Bate got the ball and transferred to Broady, who, after a brief run was knocked into touch near the corner flag. A free kick was given Leigh but nothing resulted and half time arrived with the visitors leading by a goal to nil. Early in the second half Warrington were again sub- jected to pressure and a dangerous scrimmage took place near the line. From this" Smack" Bate got the ball and punted up field, where Broady seized the ball and by a grand run scored an unimproved try. After an ineffectual mark by Bailey, Moores got possession of the ball and rushed for the line but he was tackled and passed when too late to Broady. L. Bailey and Broady exchanged passes and the latter made another brilliant run round his opponents and grounded the ball. Great surprise was manifested when the linesmen put his flag up and insisted that Broady had crossed the touch line when running round. Bate, O'Brien, Foster and Broady played up well in the next few minutes and the latter made another serviceable run. Time was called soon afterwards and the visitors retired victors by a goal (5 points) to Warrington a try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>FIRE OF LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE FIRE OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>I want to explain a principle of life the importance of which is over-estimated. It is the principle of com- bustion. Vitality is generated by the oxidation of car- bon. In this way you derive body-heat, and acquire the force or energy to live and resist disease. The oxygen necessary for combustion is derived from the air in three ways—by the lungs, by the skin, and from food. Carbon can only be acquired from food, and must be absorbed by the blood. It is this carbonace- ous (carbon) principle in the blood that makes it rich and pure, and encourages the making of healthy teal Without this principle the blood becomes impoverished and the system is obliged to use healthy flesh and the fatty parts of the body for oxidation. The result is loss of flesh and emaciation. The problem is very</p>
        <p>simple. One difficulty in the way of keeping up vitality is that there is comparatively little free carbon in most food. Another obstacle is in poor assimilation. It is clear that if, for any reason, vitality becomes low, all of the organs of the body will be affected, and the weakest ones will make trouble first.</p>
        <p>The most rational remedy is one that will increase vitality, and thus build up the system to its normal state. This is true of Consu•nption, Scrofula, Anatolia, and all wasting diseases. Here is a typical case : "22, ARGYLE-STREET, MILL-ROAD, "CAMBRIDGE, Sept. 21st, 1895. Messrs. SCOTT SG BROWNE, Ltd.,</p>
        <p>" 47, Farringdon Street, London. E.C. " DEAR Sins,—lt is with great pleasure that I address these few lines to you, to inform you of the great bene- fit our little daughter has derived from taking your Scott's Emulsion. I will give you a complete history of her case, so that you may know the condition she was in when she began to take the Emulsion. She was quite a healthy baby up to five months old, when she had an attack of Bronchitis, so common in this country. We got her through that and she seemed to be quite well until she was two years old, when she had an attack of inflammation of the bowels. This left her, as you may imagine, in a very weak and emaciated condition. For two years she was never well. She seemed languid, had no appetite, and did not care to go out to play like the other girls would do, but preferred staying in the house- When she was four years old she seemed to be suffering from the effects of wasting,and nothing which we could do seemed to give her any relief. We did not think and she would live. At times she seemed a little brighter, then she would go back again to her listless condition, not taking an interest in anything, and seeming to have no vitality. Our doctor said he thought she was going into Consumption. We tried everything to bring her back to health, but without avail, until a friend advised us to try your Emulsioa. We consequently bought a bottle, and began the treatment. We did not know ourselves what the Emulsion would do, but our friend had such faith in it that we could do not hing, else than try it. We did not content ourselves with buying one ibottle and then changing the treatment, but persisted n the use of Scott'a E,nalsion. A Frit we could see no great change in her conditiln. either for the better or worse, but in a few weeks she began to show signs of returning vitality ;sli increased in weight, and seemed stronger in every way. Feeling very much encouraged we kept on with the Emulsion, and the improvement in her condition became more rapid. " Her case was a very stubborn one indeed, but once she began to improve t here was no backward step, and the more vitality and strength she got from Scott's Emulsion the faster her health improved, until, thank God 1. she was perfectly well. She is now a very strong girl, goes to school regularly, ilk es to be out in the air, and is as playful as any child could be ; whereas, before she took the E nulsion, she was actually so weak that we had to wheel be:. about. "This seems like an extraordi nary case. Indeed, it is extraordinary, but it is, nevertheless, perfectly true, as many of our frionds could testify. "I feel it my duty to make this known for the good it may do te others, and remain, Dear Sirs, "Yours faithfully, " GEO. IVETT MARRITT." I said vitality comes from the oxidation of carbon. Scott's Emulsion contains more free carbon than any other known preparation. (6)</p>
        <p>Walt Whitman, Bret Harte, Mark Twain, and William D. Howells all began earning their livelihood as com- positors. There are more than five hundred separate muscles in the body, and an equal number of nerves and blood- vessels.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. AT THE Y.NI.C.A. A large number of persons attended the P.S.A. meeting on Sunday, filling every available seat. The chair was occupied by Mr. W. H. Bayley, who made a few remarks upon the growth of the society, complimenting those who had persevered in the arrangements for the meetings week by week. Mr. A. Corney delivered an address upon "Sympathy," holding the audience as if spellbound from the commencement to the close. THE WYCLIFFE P.S.A. A meeting of the above was held on Sunday afternoon in Wycliffe Congregational Church,when nearly 1,000 persons were present. The Rev. H. J. Bannister, of Derby, presided, and delivered an in- teresting address on " A Working Man," and Mr. Fred W. Hall, of Liverpool, spoke upon the temper- ance question. The soloist was Mr. A. C. Stewart, also of Liverpool. Messrs. T. Wallington and A. Healey took part in the proceedings.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENTS. On Saturday evening ,Mr. Harry Cartwright, manager at Messrs. Garnett and Sons' cabinet works, Penketh, was treated at the Infirmary at Warrington for a serious injury to three of his fingers.—John MacMillan (20), living in Erwood- street, was admitted to the same institution on Monday suffering from a severe sprain to the right foot caused while following his usual employment at the Cheshire Lines Waggon Works.—Peter Donlan (30), 18, Ashton-street, is also a patient at the lafirmary, his right foot having been seriously , injured by a fall of iron at Dallam Forge. HOUSEHOLD LINENS, hemmed and marked free of charge. J. and W. Dutton, Sankey-st., Warrington.—.4dvt. CONCERT AT KENDRICK STREET SCHOOLS. A concert which had been prepared by Councillor Smethurst's family and friends, took place in the Infants' Schoolroom, Kendrick-street, on Saturday evening. There was a good attendance. songs were contributed by Miss Richardson, Miss Ward, ad Mr. T. Amos; a duet by the Misses A. and M. Smethurst; readings by Miss Emily Brown; a violin solo by Miss Woods ; quartettes for violins, violon- cello and piano, by the Misses Brookes and the Misses Woods; and a pianoforte solo by Miss War- burton, entitled" Grand March," and which opened the proceedings of the evening. Trio performers one and all acquitted themselves with remarkable ability, and received well-earned applause. Mr. Amos was particularly successful in his song, "Queen of the Earth," which was re-demanded. The Vicar presided, and the accompaniments were skilfully played by Miss Minnie Smethurst. The audience included Councillor Smethurst and Mr. William Chantry (church wardens of St. Paul's Church), and the Rev. R. Brierley, curate. HOUSEHOLD llNENS,herntiled and marked free of charge. J. and W. Dutton. Sankey-it., Warrington.—Advt. STOVES.—Iron Merchant, GAHLA.ND, B ridge-street ANNUAL TEACHERS' MEETING AT LATCHFORD. The annual meeting of teachers connected with the Primitive Methodist Sunday School, Knutsford- road, was held on Saturday night in the school- room. The Rev. J. W. Kirkland presided, and there was a good attendance, The work of the school for the past year was reviewed, and was seen to be well up to previous years. Financially the school is in a slightly better condition, there be'ng a small balance in the hands of the treasurer after paying all expenses. The following officers were elected for the ensuing year :—Superintendent,Mr. J. Massey ; assistant, Mr. E. Mairs ; secretary, Mr. John Cross ; assistant, Mr. Joseph Cross ; treasurer, Mr. 3. Massey ; absentee visitor, Miss J. Swarbrick. Mr. Jackson was appointed representative to the Sunday School Union; Mr. E. Mairs, representative to the Warrington district quarter-day meeting; and Mr. J. Cotterill, secretary to the Bible Reading and Prayer Union. It was decided that prizes be given to the scholars for regular attendance. A special prize was offered by a female teacher for the scholar who secured the highest number of marks. Votes of thanks closed the meeting. OUTBREAK OF TYPHOID FEVER AT SANKEY BRIDGES. An outbreak of typhoid fever has occurred at Sankey Bridges, and has assumed somewhat serious proportions, several persons having been affected, and two deaths having occurred. The last death, that of a young man, took place on Nlonday, on which day also the interment took place The out- break is prinipally in Howard-st., in the Warring- ton end of the township. A dificulty has arisen with reference to the removal of the patients to the Infectious Diseases Hospital at Warrington. There was an arrangement between the Warrington Cor- poration and the Rural District Council to accept all patients from the district on payment of a cer- tain fixed sum per week for the maintenance of the patient. A short time ago however, during the absence of Dr. Sephton, medical officer to the Rural Sanitary Authority, a case was taken into the hos- , pital on the recommendation of the medical man ' who attended the patient, and on the assumption that there would be no difficulty experienced in getting the usual authority for the removal. On the return of Dr. Seph ton he would not give the neces- sary order for removal, the District Council have declined to pay the bill incurred for the treatment of the patient, and consequently the Corporation of Warrington have terminated the agreement. The result is that there is no hospital to which the patients from Sankey Bridges can be taken. The outbreak was taken into consideration at the monthly meeting of the Great Sankey School Board, which was held on Tuesday evening under the pre- sidency of Mr. W. P. Skelton. The Rev. E. S. Jack- son, one of the members of the Board, said that if the outbreak continued to increase perhaps it would be necessary to close the Board Schools. Mr. George Clarke (headmaster) observed that their school at- tendance officer had not yet made a report to the Board on the subject. If it was found necessary to close the schools, he would no doubt communicate with them. There was now a very broken atten- dance from Sankey Bridges. The discussion then I dropped.</p>
      </div>
