      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>DREADFUL COLLIERY DISASTER I'</head>
        <p>DREADFUL COLLIERY DISASTER I'</p>
        <p>WALES.</p>
        <p>The ill-fates Rhondda Valley in South Wales was on Monday morning the scene of a cqlliery disaster of con- siderable magnitude. About half-past five o'clock an explosion occurred in the Tylorstown pit of the Ferndale Colliery Company,Limited. Fortunately this was before the arrival of the day workers—seme 1,000 or 1,200 is number—or the death roll well might have been of appalling dimensions. Rescue parties were immediately I formed, and a number of the men were found to have been uninjured ; but so far as can be gathered from the incomplete accounts to hand, about fifty lives seem to have been sacrificed. There seem to have been twelve men in No. 6 pit, all of whom were killed ; 56 in No. 1 pit,of whom about 32 were saved ; and 19 in No. 8 pits of whom seven are known to be dead. About thirty bodies in all have been recovered, and is is believed that all the missing miners have been killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LAND WITHOUT ANIMALS.</head>
        <p>A LAND WITHOUT ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>Japan is a land without the domestic animals. it is this lack which strikes the stranger so forcibly, in look. ing upon Japanese landscapes. There are no cows—the Japanese neither drink milk nor eat meat. There are but few horses, and these are imported mainly for the use of the foreigners. The waggons in the city streets are pulled and pushed by coolies, and the pleasure carri- ages are drawn by men. There are but few dogs, and these are neither used as watch dogs, beasts of burden nor in hunting, except by foreigners. There are no sheers in Japan, and wool is not used in clothing, silk and cot- ton being the staples. There are no pigs—pork is an unknown article of diet, and lard is not used in cooking. There are no goats or mules, or donkeys. Wild animals there are, however, and in particular, bears of enormous size. One of these, Mr. Firick saw stuffed, in a museum, he describes as "big as an ox." Beside another stuffed museum bear is preserved, in alcohol, the mangled body of a child the bear had eaten just before being killed. War, of course, is acquainting the Japanese with the use of animals. The army have cavalry horses, and others to drag the field guns. The Empress, also, in obvious imitation of European royalties, is an expert horse- woman, and saddle horses are kept for her use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>MATRIMONIAL AGENCY TRIAL.</head>
        <p>THE MATRIMONIAL AGENCY TRIAL.</p>
        <p>The police court proceedings in the case of 4' The World's Great Marriage Association, Limited," - were brought to a close on Tuesday. On behalf cf the de- fendants several witnesses were called with a view to showing that the business of the Association was carried on in good faith. One of these was a lady with an in- come of £2OO a year and" expectations," who said she receivtd hundreds of letters through the Association and bad written about one hunired replies, but had never become engaged; another was a lady who had frequently called at the defendants' offices and had received about twenty-five personal introductions : a third, who had a capital of £4,000, bad been placed in communication with" about twenty gentlemen," none of them whom quite suited her ; and a fourth had married through the medium of the Association. The defendants were corn. mated for trial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>GENERAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Sentence of five years' penal servitude was passed on Monday by the Lord Chief Justice on Alfred Templeton Hawkins, convicted last week in the High Court, of frauds upon the National Dwellings Society,of which he was a director. 'rho first keel-plate of the Gladiator, a new type of second-class cruiser, was laid at Portsmouth Dockyard on Monday. She is expected to be ready for commis- sioning within twelve months. The Liverpool Health Committee decided on Monday to recommend to the City Council that no street in the city shall be of less width than thirty-six feet, the mini- mum at present being thirty feet. Statements have been made to a London news agency by German commercial travellers and others as to tLe enormous damage caused to German trade in Eng- land by the feeling arising out of the strained relation- ship created by the Kaiser's letter to President Kruger. The thirty-seventh birthday of the Emperor William was celebrate i throughout Germany on Monday with great eclat. On Saturday evening the Russian Amtas- sador handed the Kaiser an autograph lettcr of congra- tulation from the Czar.</p>
      </div>
