      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Xettero to tin Obitor.</head>
        <p>Xettero to tin Obitor.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED CRICKET LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln anEwer to the letter of "A Local Secretary" of last Saturday, kindly allow me the space to say that I quite agree with him,in his views as to formation of a Cricket League for neict season, and if the secretaries or their representatives will meet in the rooms of the Y.M.C.A., Friars'-green,on Wednesday evening next,the 29th inst., at eight o'clock, we .can then appoint our -officials, &amp;c., and so get the League in force at once. In the meantime,if the secretaries of the various clubs in Warrington and district will arrange fixtures with as many clubs as possible in the.district or bring with them -a list of the open dates they will have, it will make the &lt;petition of forming a League much easier.—Yours</p>
        <p>ERNESr. A. SANDERS.</p>
        <p>AN OASIS IN THE DESERT.</p>
        <p>• Sir,—The " sweet reasonableness" of " A. Learner -tomes as a pleasure niter the " vehement fanaticism " of" Zabniner." With such a one it is both pleasurable and profitable to seek further light by discussion and, cultivate the virtue of polemical amenity. It is a small soul that gets angry with an opponent for a difference of opinion ; and this smallness becomes absolutely pusilani- mous when anomymous attacks are made upon absent people. As a humanitarian,Col. Ingersoll has done,and Is doing good work in America ; and the man who calls ,him a " windbag " may thank distance and his own insignificance and and obscurity for his escape from a well merited chastisement. As an object lesson in the " Christian " love as _against " Pagan" wind," hear what the Colonel says about Christmas Day—" It is beautiful to give one day to the ideal—to have one day apart,one day for generous deeds, for good will, for gladness. One day to forget .the shadows, the rains, the storms of life ; to remember the sunshine, the happiness of youth and health. One day to forget the briars and thorns on the winding path, to remember the fruit and flowers. One day in which to feed the hungry-, to salute the poor and lowly. One day to feel the brotherhood of man. One day to get acquainted with children, to remember. the old, the . unfortunate, and the infirm. One day in which to forget yourself and think lovingly of others. One day for the family, for the fireside, for wife and children, for the love and laughter, the joy and rapture of home." Let the kind but candid reader compare those utterances of" Pagan Bob" with the "Christian " sentiments of " Zaminer " in your correspondence columns of the 11th inst., to see which leaves the 4 healthiest impression." , - Referring again to" A Learner" I wish to say that while I make no pretence toinvulnerability, he has, by admission, failed to find the joints in the mail. In com- mon with most Christians when hard pressed—in the retreat before the advance of Naturalism, 112, and my Other friend" Amos White," fall back upon the "Father- hood of God " as a "Keep "—the last stronghold of "the faith." The imperious limitation of your space and my time renders further trespass upon both just now unwise. Some future time, when both these limitations are re laxed, I may with your permission Mr. Editor, see - further light on this subject through the medium of your correspondence column. Meanwhile, let those who hold the Christian God to be the same as the God of Abraham, &amp;c., &amp;c., and of Moses. read Numbers xxxi, with especial reference to verses 15 and 18 ! ! If this was the tribal God of the Jews—it would be interesting to know what the Devil was like.—Truly yours. :JOHN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>"A LEARNER"_ AND MR. PEERS' CRITICS.</head>
        <p>"A LEARNER"_ AND MR. PEERS' CRITICS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is not my intention to continue any discussion with "A Learner" upon the points touched upon in his letter of last Saturday. With respect to his thrust at Mr. Peers' critics on the assumed ground that there was a deficiency of argument in their replies to him, my conviction is that there was little need of argument in the case. Mr. Peers' letters exhibit none; he has only dealt in bald, incorrect, and often offensive statements and these have been suitably met by other statements as far as they have gone, both decisive and authoritive. "Vivian Grey" might well ask "What proof has Mr. John Peers for his assertions?" And the " critics " might properly have rested there, and waited for his answer. "-A Learner" however has tabulated certain charges expressed or implied in Mr. Peers' statements which he considers have been but feebly met. I presume that these charges include 1. The dogmas of "the atonement, resurrection and redemption," and if so I must demur to their being characterised as the outcome of "ages of darkness and ignorance"; they are truths plainly taught by Christ and His apostles; and I cannot see how they can be regarded otherwise if the New Testament is any authority for "thoughtful men." 2. Nor can I agree with "A Learner "dismissing so mildly Mr. Peers' assertion that "Jesus of Nazareth was not known as Jesus Christ by His contemporaries, and probably never heard the name as applied to himself."! Have the documents then no historical validity for" A Learner "when he admits that this" may be verbally correct "? What say they about this matter? Why, that over and over again Jesus Himself claimed to be the Christ. In Matthew xvi., 16-20., He asked" Whom say ye that I am ?" Peter answered "Thou are the Christ,the Sonfof the:living God ."My Father,"Jesus saith "bath revealed unto t-ee." Then he charged them that they should tell no man that He was Jesus the Christ." Again to the woman of Samaria in private and to the .crowds in the Temple courts towards the close of His Ministry, and at His trial before the High Priest (Mark xv. 61-62). He distinctly affirmed Himself to be the Christ. How then may Mr. Peers be "verbally correct. 3. Upon "A Learner's" third point—namely Calvinism much might be said. It must suffice to ob serve that Iby no means admit his reflection upon Pauline Christianity. It is not at all clear that Calvinism is its logical outcome. This is a mere question of inter pretation. And, finally,l should not consent to relegate the inspired Paul to the limbo of certain critics who would place his teachings upon any lower level than that of his Master. " Have I net seen the Lord ?" he once -said; "and the gospel preached of inn is not after man— but by the revelation of Jesus Christ." Galat. I. 11-12. Yours truly,</p>
        <p>ZAMINER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>January 24.tb, 1896. TO SCEPTICAL ASTHMATICS.</head>
        <p>January 24.tb, 1896. TO SCEPTICAL ASTHMATICS.</p>
        <p>The truly marvellous cures of Asthma which have teen already effected by Dr. Rudolph Schiffmann, cer- tainly call for notice. His preparation (Schiffmann's Asthma Cure) not only produces instant relief in the most stubborn and obstinate cases, but positively cures, in proof of which, hear what Mrs. DAVISON, 14, Deere- street, Moipeths' says:—" My son 'has been a great sufferer from Asthma for nearly five years ; even in the summer he had always to wear an overcoat, and for five months last winter be was never out of the house. . lam -quite sure i worse case of Asthma could not be, and ye/ since the first night my boy used your Cure he has never lost one hour's sleep, and I have sent him out during all this severe Weather we have had (snowing or raining) and he is quite well. My son is only twelve years of age and has been under a physician's care for two years." Space prevents publishing further testimonials, but sceptical readers may, if they choose, communicate with the abeve-ramed, and they are urged to send a post card to De: 'SCITIFFMANN'S British Depot; 32A, Snow Hill, London, E.C., for a list of testimonials which are abso- lutely convincing. Packages of Schiffmann's Asthma Cure may be obtained at all Chemists for 23. 61. and 4s. 6d. per package, or from the Depot as above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>ISLAM ATROCITIES VERSUS CHRISTIAN CALLOUS-ITIES.</head>
        <p>ISLAM ATROCITIES VERSUS CHRISTIAN CALLOUS-ITIES.</p>
        <p>"A thousand shames" ! this taunt should stigmatise The" Christian Powers" who callously ignore Their doomed Armenian brethren's dying cries, Still tortured, outraged, mangled in their gore. On Civilisation, what a parody— On Christianity, what mock'ry base— That such unspeakable torturers should be Upheld and propped by "goody" England's grace! And now, through bunkum " jealousies " of State;" , Our mighty host/rill not a fin,,,Tr raise, To save those martyred Christians, whose dire fate • Out-shames, out-Neros darkest pagan days! We know that" goody-goody '? England's rage Is raised .tewthousand volts, to whitest heat,,, , At cruelty to dog, cat, bird in cage, Or Negro Savage in his dark retreat. But sithgn it comes to massacres wholesale,— • " With narheless outrages=of•Chslatian folk. . By a "damned "and perjurld protege, we fall To break or ease our tortured brother's yoke ADAM NEWSENSE. Warrington, January, 1896.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>SiTNDIY; SCHOOL ,7Paizz-utvniu,—This ceremony took ,i4litee -in the aturch • Insti.tute on Saturday afternoon. The meeting commenced with devotional exercises,after - which the Curate who presided,made a few appropriate remarks. The prizes were then distributed by Mrs. Woods, of Walton Home, each recipient behag warmly ap- plauded. A'hearty vote ot thanks, proposed by Mr. Warburton, the superintendent of the school, and se- conded by Mr. Mclntyre, was heartily accorded to Mrs. Woods for her kindness in being present. Mr. Mclntyre pooposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, and it was accorded. An entertainment, which had been arranged by Miss Hartley,was then successfully carried out,at the edd of which each scholar, according to usual custom, received a bun and an orange.</p>
