      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>For the second time this season, Warrington have had to submit to defeat at the hands of Hull, but not- withstanding the fact that the match on Saturday was played away frorn home,it was a much closer game than the former one,for whereas the Warrington match was won by Hull by nine points to three,the Hull match was only won by six points to three. There is perhaps a little comfort to be got from this, but not much. The most satisfactory piece of comfort that can be got from it is that Warrington have not gone any lower on the League table in consequence.</p>
        <p>It is a very extraordinacliing.but it is nevertheless a fact that the try scored at Hull on Saturday by Cross, was the first try scoreg„in Yorkshire during the present season by WarringtoiqOur local team have now been in Yorkshire a good n ber of times,. but never before: have they had the pleii 're of recording a try. On only one previous occasion Ilitioe,tliey Scored at all, and that was against Liversedgerithen -Swift kicked a penalty goal. Warrington have this4coted a total of six points in Yorkshire. Their record on their own ground against Yorkshire clubs is much:different and on only one occa- sion have they beenxitlecLind-that wasby Leeds. They have scored an Aggregate of 31::) point* against,the Tykes at Warringtowthikagason:- The mat.ekat,rifugom,Saturday as compltely spoiled by the 4tatelPf, theground.--'it Taetalm6s,t irepqs4iNe to stand qpiaeid,aclurate passing was very difficult. The mist which.'hung oVftylh44 groid also` --rtrn'dkred' the' in- divid4l,Riarts 1Q distinguished" and,thus took alot"C Interest away. inking the‘plas-a'6ohole, eriliiiucky to Wse,and a draW would have been titih ',More r presentatii,e of the gatria4 ttlYhere Warrington beat the Hullires wa*forward, altiaough the Hull pack wirkthe-heavie} of the,two-,,,tilt Warrington, were niudtD:ztluittker: Abs.—Hutt halves/wexe,offiside almnst thaivifizilatftkngamoithe refgrge 49145'44y not paying mucltalitell.tiOa-Ao3kie pox rules,- , .71777; -5;11' 41 (C:', Coining to inclisilNidaqlJirgrAs 4'04; Burrows Nevins,and'Fairlitmtivg,theAnostspnaitient Warring- ton forwards, while Dtinl4,4.Feetti-am,and the red9ubt- able Jackets were the shining lights in the opposing pack. Foden was more prominent than Higginbotham, and the try scored by Warrington was practically his, though Cross put the finishing touch on. Cross was the best threequarter, and Carey also put on some good work,while Heesom was in his best form at full back, and was in advance of Johnson, the opposing full back.</p>
        <p>The journey to Hull is the longest which Warrincden had to make during the present season, and it would be a heavy expense on the club if many journeys like that were made. The team were travelling altogether eight hours and twenty-five minutes, and were only in Hall four hours and twenty minutes. The amount of travell- ing done by the Hull club is simply appalling. Hull is situated in a very isolated Position, and the nearest club ground to them is Wakefield Trinity, and this is 50 miles away. They make eleven journeys into Lancashire and Cheshire, and the nearest ground in these counties to them is Stcckport,a distance of about 94 miles. Runcorn and St. Helens are their longest journeys, and these are over 120 miles. It is a very common thing for the Hull players to get back after their matches some time in the morning of Sunday.</p>
        <p>There were no great surprises in any of the matches on Saturday. It is a strange thing how unfortunate Broughton Rangers are against Lancashire clubs. They almost annihilate the Yorkshire teams opposed to them, be they at the top or the bottom of the table, as witness their crushing defeats of Manningham, Liversedge, and Leeds, and yet, whenever they tackle a Lancashire club, they are utterly unable to win. They have met Lanca- shire 'teams twelve times this season, and they have yet to win their first match in the Lancashire section. They have drawn with four, but the remaining eight fixtures have been lost. It was thought that they had a chance on Saturday of breaking their luck, for they were "at home •' to Rochdale Hornets, but they only drew after all. Out of the eleven matches with York- shire they have bagged as many pointo.</p>
        <p>Manningharn have endangered their prospect of keen- ing at the top of the Northern League, by succumbing by a goal to nil to the second team, Halifax, on the table. Halifax have now the best record in the competi• tion, being only a point behind Manningham,and having a match in hand. Bradford just got home in their match with Liversedge, by a try to nil, while Widnes went up two at the expense of Wigan, though by the majority of one point only.</p>
        <p>To-day, Warrington ought to bag a couple of points against Rochdale Hornets. The match at Rochdale ended in a draw,although Warrington were pressing almost the whole of the time. The advantage of ground ought to make all the difference between a draw and a win. Warrington have a good chance now of getting up in the League table, for out of the next uine matches seven are at home, and if they do not take advantage of these matches, their chance of a respectable position on the table will be gone.</p>
        <p>The following is the team appointed to meet Rochdale Hornets to-day :—Full back. Heesom ; threequarters, Carey. Barber, Cross, and Brnady ; half backs, Foden and Clark ; forwards, Nev;ns, Burrows.Fairburst,Thorn- ton, Andrews, Barrett, Swift, and raylor.</p>
        <p>The following is the present position of the Northern League :</p>
        <p>Score. -Score. Phi. Won. Lost. Drrt. For. Ag'st. Pts. V anningham 25 ... 19 ... 6 ... 0 ...187 ... 95 ... 38 Halifax Oldham 25 ... 16 ... 7 ... 2 ...215 -.107 ... 34 Runcorn 24 ... 13 ... ,4 7, '...189 ... 80 33 Beghouse Ran 25 ... 13 ... 6 6 ...127 ... 65 ...N32 Bradford Wigan 24 ... 11 ... 6 ... 7 ...113 ... 72 ... 29 Leigh Hull Tyldesley Hunslet 25 12 12 ... 1 ...149 ...129 ... 25 Liversedge 23 ... 11 ... 10 ... 2 ...183 ...128 ... 24 "St.lielens 23 ... 10 7 ... 6 ... 93 ... 94 ... 24 Leeds Widnes Warrington Stock pot t Beght'n Ran 23 ... 5 ... 13 5 ... 87 ...119 ... 15 Rochdale H..- 2b ... 3 ... 14 8 ... 53 ...167 ... 14 Wakefield T 25 -. 5 ... 16 ... 4 ... 49 193 ... 14 Batley 23 ... 4 ... 14 ... 5 ... 62 ...188 ... 13 Huddersfield 24 ... 5 ... 16 ... 3 8.3 ...143 ... 13 The Lancashire clubs are as follows :</p>
        <p>Score Score Pts.</p>
        <p>Yid. Won Lost Drn For. -Ag'st. Runcorn Oldham! l2 ... 3 ... 2 ... 83 ... 34 16 Wigan Leigh Tyldesley 11 ... 5 4 ... 2 ... 62 ... 35 12 *St. Helens Warrington Rochdale H ll 2 ... 4 ... 5 ... 29 ... 60 9 Widnes . Stockpori. B g'ht'n Ran l2 0 ... 8 ... 4 ... 14 ... 61 4</p>
        <p>The Yorkshire clubs are as follows :</p>
        <p>Score Score Fts. Pld. Won. Lost. Drn For. Ag'st. Manningham 13 ... 11 2 0 ... 93 ... 39 ... 22 Hull l3 ... 7 ... 5 ... 1 ... 83 ... 36 ... 15 Halifax 4 - ll ... 7 ... 4 ... 0 ... 73 ... 32 ... 14 Leeds B'ghouse Ran l3 ... 6 ... 5 ... 2 64 ... 36 ... 14 Liversedge l3 ... 6 ... 6 ... 1 ... 87 ... 60 ... 13 Bradford Hunslet ll 5 ... 5 ... 1 ... 58 ... 42 ... 11 Huddersfield ll ... 3 ... 7 ... 1 ... 27 ... 55 ... 7 Batley l2 ... 3 ... 8 ... 1 ... 31 ... 86 ... 7 Walaefi'd Tri'tv 12 ... 2 ... 9 ... 1 ... 20 ...119 ... 5 *St. Helens have had two points deducted for playing an ineligible man. -</p>
        <p>By their victory on Saturday at Huddersfield,Runcorn have been enabled to climb still ar other rung in the Northern URiOll ladder, and they now stand in the creditable position of fourth in the list. It was but by a narrow margin that they gained their two points on Saturday, but "'twill suffice," and considering that many of their best players were away, and that the day was one in which it was impossible to play the open game which Runcorn players revel in,it was satisfactwy and more than was expected by many of the Runcorn supporters. To-day Runcorn meet Brighouse Rangers on the Irwell-lane enclosure, and as the Yorkshire team is but one point below Runcorn in the icombinatiou com• petition a fine game may be anticipated. Runcorn,there I is every reason to believe,will place one of the strongest teams on the field they have been able to play this sea- son, as will be seen from the following list :—Full back, S. Houghton ; threequarters, T. Warder, H. Myers,,J. Butterworth, and B. West; half backs, J. Faulkner and G. Robinson; forwards, W. Faulkner, H. Farmer, R. Reece, S.Walker, A. Taylor, O. Moores, J. Langley, 0. Hughes.</p>
        <p>It will be seen that Houghton once again figures in the Runcorn team, and this decision of the well-known Eng- lish International has given the greatest satisfaction to the many followers of the Runcorn club. For some time the rumour has been current in football circles,that such an arrangement had been completed, but it was not until Monday evening that this course was decided upon. His addition to the Runcorn team will materially strengthen it. Butterworth will be relieved from discharging the Guties of full back which he has fulfilled so creditably, and thus enabled to take his accustomed place in the three-quarter line. It has already been predicted that Runcorn may now carry off the medals in the Northern Union League, as Houghton's rejoining the club will give it a further stimulus. It may be added that Houghton had been chosen to play for England against Ireland to-day (Saturday) at Leeds, but he has foregone this honour in order to assist his late Runcorn colleagues garnet Brighouse Rangers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WA RR INGTON.</head>
        <p>WA RR INGTON.</p>
        <p>SCHOLARS' TEA. PARTY AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>On Monday night the scholars attending the Primitive Methodist Sunday School were enter- tained to tea by the teachers and friends in the schoolroom, Knutsford-road. About 100 sat down to an excellent meal, at the conclusion of which various games were indulged in with vigour until about 9.30. Oranges were distributed during the after proceedings, and the scholars on breaking up expressed themselves highly gratified to those who had so generously borne the expense of the even- ing's enjoyment.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN.S MA. PARTY AT PIDGATE. The annual treat to the children attending the Wesleyan Sunday school took place in the school- room on Friday afternoon. In the evening an en- tertainment was given, at which a•number of their parents and friends were present. The programme was made up of various items by the children.and the entertainment was a decided success. During an interval the Rev. G. W. Triggs, who presided, presented the prizes to those scholars who had earned them for early and regular attendance dur- ing the past year.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY AT PADGATE. In response to the request of the Lord Bishop of Liverpool, Sunday was observed at Christ Church as Temperance Sunday, when special sermons were preached and offertories taken in behalf of this sub- ject. In the morning the service was conducted and the sermon preached by the Rev. C. C. Frost, rector of St. George's, Manchester, and in the evening the Rev. Canon Willis, M.A., rector of Warrington, preached.—The Rev. J. J, Langham, M.A., vicar of Padgate, preached tempprance sermons on Sunday, in the morning at the Churcli,,4 the Holy Intiocents Liverpool, and in the e'Veniqg -.9.t Holy Trinity. CI-Abaci), Walton Break</p>
        <p>, CAIRO-ST. AMATEUR DRAWATIC An entertainment was given at th Cairo-street I Sunday school on Monday evening, under the auspices of Cairo-st. Amateur Dramatic Society. A very large and appreciative audience assembled, and were delighted with the clever ren- dering of Mr. T. Mee Pattison's operetta. "Freddy and his Fiddle." All who took part merit great praise for the able manner in which they performed, I especially Mr. Knowles, the manager, to whose energy and enthusiasm the success is mainly due. Master Tom Ktfowles took the part of Freddy, whilst the Sheriff was taken by Mr. Emmerson. • Following this came the 'Living Wonders," which caused much amusement and laughter, and being 'full of wit and humour could not fail to please the most sober-minded. Owiug to the great de- mand for tickets it was found necessary ,to repeat the entertainment on Tuesday evening. The pro- ceeds were in aid 01 the new schools, which are now nearing completion, and Which make a very fine and imposing edifice.</p>
        <p>ST. JAMES' TEA PARTY. The St. James' annual tea meeting was held in the schoolroom on Monday evening, and tea was en the tables at half-past six, and the good things provided were partaken of by about 200 persons. The following ladies and gentlemen were givers of trays or half-trays :—Mesdames Slater, Sproule, Sharpe, C. S. Burgess, Gibson, John White, Robert Davies, Hutchinson, Warburton, Wynne Jones, H.N. Houghton, Stanstield, John Davies, Malin Edelsten, G. Venn, Herbert Woods, Seed, T. Jackson,Howard, F. Booth, W. B. Roberts, G. W. Woods, Greenough, J. A. Edelsten, Cottam, F. Pierpoint, Smith (Latch- ford). the Misses Pierpoint (St. Austin's), Parry, and Dowling; Dr, White, the Rev. B. B. Prescott, Messrs. James Tinsley, R. W. Francomb, W. Jack- son, J. H. Warburton.R.W. Rowson, Wright, Milling and Company, Jack Burgess, Ruddock, and John Coton. After tea an entertainment was given, an excellent programme having been provided. The following artistes took part :—Mrs.lierbert Woods. Miss Slater, Mr. Herbert E. Burgess, Mr. J.M.Hayes, and Mr. H.ll Thomas. Mr. Frank Booth skilfully played the accompaniments. Dancing followed, and was kept up with spirit until half-past ten. WARRINGTON AND BANK QUAY SAILING CLUB</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The above club held its annual general meeting of members at the Volunteer Inn. Cockhedge-lane, on Monday evening and was very well attended. The proceedings opened with the reading of the balance sheet which shows that the club is in a good finan- cial position. The gross receipts for the year amount to £2B 15e. 91, and the expenditure to ..V24 75., leaving a balance in hand of £4 63. 9d. The club is also in possession of a beautiful silver bowl which has been purchased out of the club funds, to be raced for annually. The first race for the cup was sailed on the usual date,the Saturday following Good Friday, but was declared void owing to a mis- take at the start and was sailed off at a later date. The" Pink,;' captained by Mr. Walter Warburton, which won on both occasions, holds the cup for 12</p>
        <p>.. .. . months. The following officials were elected for the coming season _:—Commodore, Mr.:J. Whitfield;</p>
        <p>vice commodore, Mr. Walter Knowles; treasurer, Mr. F. J. Jefferson; secretary, Mr T. G. Grounds; committee, Messrs. 11. Turner, J. Jefferson, B. Dainteth, 14. Warburton and E. Gleave. The club which in future is to be known as the Warrington Sailing Club has a good prospect before it, several new boats having joined together with nearly 20 new members. A vote of thanks to the retiring officers brought the meeting to a close. ST. PETER'S CHURCH TEA MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Monday, the annual tea perty and entertp.id: ment in connection with the above place or wor- ship was held in the schoolroom, John-st. An ex- cellent repast was provided to which about. 300 persons sat down. A large number of ladies pre- sided over the tables, and these included :—Mes- dames Bamforth. Savage, Percival, Gandy, Cox, Welsby, Blease, Edgar, Grounds, McCollom, Taylor, and Pearson. and the Misses Knott and Madeley. The following is the list of donors of trays :—Mes- dames Charlton Parr, Buckton, Beamont, Mortimer, Kay, Litton, Lyon, Percival, Heaton, ,Stanton, Brookes, Reynolds, Morris, Sharpe, Bamforth,Webs- ter, Welsby, Savage, Skelland,Anderton, Boardman, MeCollom, Edgar, Cox, Stanley, Grounds and Madeley ; Messrs. John Geddes, Robert Davies W. D. Jeans, Henry Greenall, J. D. Buckton, Davies, Roberts, J. Shillcock, C. Chorley, John Melbourne, R. W. Francomb, Peter Walker and Sons, Greenall, Whitley and Co., James Tinsley, Guest Brothers, Platt and Son, Dr. Adams, and others. At the con- clusion of the tea an entertainment was given, Captain Buckton presiding. There were also on the platform the Revs. B. Percival (vicar), J. A. Cooil (curate), and Messrs. John Roberts, Peter Edgar, and J. Taylor. Songs, recitations, and pic- colo solos were tastefully rendered by Miss Pember- ton, Miss Richardson, Mrs. Bailey, and Messrs. Tree, Hewitt, Ray and Pearson. The Brothers Grounds, and Sergeant Ruse (A.P.C.), supplied the humorous put of the programme, and their efforts were much appreciated. On Tuesday evening the annual Sun- day school scholars' tea party took place, when another enjoyable evening was spent. ODDFELLOWSLIP AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner in connection with the Loyal William Savage Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity) was held in the Lodge room at the King and Queen Inn.Padgate,on Saturday evening, when about 36 sat down to a substantial repast which had been prepared by the landlady, Mrs. Clare. Dinner over the tables were cleared, and a meeting was held over which C.S. John Emson presided, in the absence of Prov. G.M.J.F. Whittle, and P.G. J. Wills occupied the vice-chair. Amongst those present were P.P.G.M. A. Boyle, P.P.G.M. Thomas Rigby, P.G. Frederick Taylor (Constitution Lodge), Brother R. Kennan (secretary), N.G. N. McGuire, besides a large num- ber of visiting brethren and the members of the lodge. The Chairman proposed the toasts of the Qneen and Royal Family and the Army and Navy. P.G. Taylor proposed "The Order, Grand Master and Board of Directors." The Chairman, in responding, said the Grand Master Was a sterling man for the , position he held, and the office could not have been placed in better hands. (Hear, hear.) The D.G.M. _was a Lancashire man, and a very worthy Odd- fellow. The Board of Directors, of whom beetled a personal acquaintance, were well worthy of their, respective positions. Their order was making rapid stride, not by leaps and bounds, but by steady pro- gress in numbers. They were placing to their reserve fund a matter of £4,000 or £5,000 per yea' and in a few years they hoped to double that amount.(Applause.) P.P.G.M. T. Rigby proposed the district officers, P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle (in the absence of the G.M.) replied and thanked them for the toast. The district was in a flourishing posi- tion, having a total of 1,510 members, being a gain of 32 on the year. Their total funds amounted to £11,914. . The funeral fund showed a gain of £l5O on the 'year, and the lodge funds had increased by about £4OO on the year's working. (Applause.)— Several other toasts followed, and during the even- ing songs and recitations were given P.G. Sutcliffe, Mr. J. W. Brimelow, Bro G. Cade, P.G. Dyson Whit- ham, Mr. R. Young, P.G. W. Mather, Mr. Richard Whiteman, P.G. J. Melbourne, P.G. G. Buckley,P.G. S. Broadhurst, Mr.; J. Clayton, and P.P.G.M. A. Boyle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON'</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON'</p>
        <p>CONGREGA.TIONAL SUNDAY SCHOOL, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening the teachers and elder scholars connected with the above school held their annual party in the schoolroom. Uwe were 65 present, and tea, which was served at five o'clock, was heartily enjoyed. After tea an interesting programme of songs was given by Mr. R. Owen and .Nliss Randall, whilst Mr. J. Howard gave a couple of whistling solos. The evening was afterwards spent in games of varicus kinds, the party dispers- ing at 10.15. VIADUCT INSTITUTE, EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, under the auspices of the Viaduct Institute Committee, a lecture was given in the large reading-room by the Rev. G. Talalun Newton, on " Elijah : The National Reformer." There was only a moderate audience. Mr. C. W. Emmett presided, and he was accompanied on the platform by the Revs. J. Batley, E. It. Davies, Dr. Thomson, and Mr. i. Ormand. The lecturer was intently listened to by the audience, various pas- sages of oratory creating a profound impression. A vote of thanks was afterwards accorded the lecturer and chairman. EARLESTOWN WELSH WESLEY-ANS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the annual Sunday school treat took place in the Wesleyan Chapel,.when. Mr. Molyneux's Christmas cake was cut and distributed. There were about 80 to tea, at the close of which an entertainment consisting of songs, readings, re- citations, &amp;c.,were given by the scholars and mem- bers of the chapel. Mr. .r. Morris presided, and the programme which was a lengthy one was capitably submitted and much enjoyed. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE BILL AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE BILL AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual ball promoted by the committee of the Earlestown Conservative Club,took place in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Wednesday evening. The hall was tastefully decorated for the occasion, and there was a large company present, amongst others being Messrs. Borron, Stone, Airey, and Dr. Street. Dancing commenced about nine o'clock, and was kept up until four o'clock next morning to the strains of Peplow's string band. Messrs. G. Bailey and J. Haselden officiated as M C.'s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>VOLUNTEER BALL AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER BALL AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The second annual ball in connection with the Newton Detachment of the Ist V.B.S.L. Regiment, took place in the Town Hall, Earlestown,on Friday evening. The room was very nicely decorated by Mr. E. T. Evans, the orchestra platform being set off with two piles of rifles. There were about 140 persons present, and dancing commenced shortly after nine o'clock. Lieutenants Homer and Patter- son were present. the former leading the first dance, Major Widdowes was unavoidably pre- vented from being present. The music supplied was under the leadership of Mr. Doran. The M.C.'s were Lance-Sergeant Bailey and Lance-Corporal Wickham, the former efficiently discharging the ' duties of secretary. Refreshments were supplied by Mr. R. Lythgee. Dancing was kept up until 4.30 next morning.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD NEWTON RESIDENT.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon Mr.llenry Ilindley,of Mercer- st.,Newton,died at the age of 83 years. Mr. Hindley has been in failing health for some time, and on Sunday as he appeared a little better, he was re- moved downstairs,but about five o'clock he became much worse and died in his arm chair. It is some- thing like 50 years since Mr.H.indley came to New- ton, and somewhat later he. was employed as a porter on Newton Bridge station. He was after- wards transferred as a signalman, and was engaged for some years in the signal-box at liaydock cross- ing. He ultimately took up a situation at Messrs. McCorquodale and Co.'s, where he remained until declining years forced him to retire. The funeral took place on Wednesday at S. Peter's Church, Newton. EARLESTOWN WESLEY GUILD.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of this Guild was held in the lecture hall of the Weslvan Chapel on Wednesday evening. The Rev. J. Bartley presided and there was a good attendance of members. The pro- gramme was "balloted subjects." The following subjects were introduced and dealt with by Mr. Pearson (Newton). "The greatest need of the age." Miss Marrow, "Suggest improvement for the guild, and what would be the advantages of the improve- ment." Mr. Burgess, "Books that have influenced me." A discussion followed the reading of each paper, the following amongst others taking part, Rev. J. Bartley, Mrs. Burgess, Miss Newman and Mr. Wheatley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLILUS.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLILUS.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall. Earlestown, on Monday evening. Bro. James Hatton presidel and there was a good attendance of members. Circulars respecting the District Lodge were read by Bro.G.Clarke.The digest of business for No. 1 convention was submitted and representatives instructed: Three candidates were initiated, and arrangements for the monthly enter- tainment on Saturday were made. A paper was afterwards read by Bro. Barker, secretary, entitled "A talk about the stars," in which he dealt with the religious observances or star worship, by the Pagans, the distances from the earth, and size of the planets. The paper was of a most interesting character, and was listened to with the closest attention by the audience,which heartily applauded the essayist at the close. Thanks were accorded Bro. Barker on the motion of Bro. G. E. Warburton, seconded by Bro. (I.,Clarke, and supported by Bro, W. H. Dale, to which Bro. Barker suitably responded. BURGLARY AT WARGRAVE POST OFFICE.</p>
        <p>Early on Saturaay morning the shop in Wargrave- road, Earlestown, occupied by Mts. Beatty, at which the postal business for the Wargrave district is transacted, was entered by burglars, and the cash drawer stolen. It is supposed the premises were broken into between the hours of 2 and 4 o'clock. The perpetrators did their work in a most deter- mined manner. Operations appear to have been commenced at tbe back portion of the premises where a pane of glass was taken from the scullery window, and that part of the house was successfully entered, but further ingress was prevented, as the kitchen door was locked. A side window in Brad- leigh-road had also been tampered with, but the attempt here was likewise abortive. The premises were ultimately entered•by the burglars removing a pane of glass from the large shop window abut- ting the public road, and nearest the letter box. The whole of the valuables, however, such as stamps, postal orders, &amp;c., had been removed upstairs to a place of safety by Mrs. Beatty, and thus the loss was not so great as it might have been. The thieves ransacked various drawers, but the only property missing is a quantity of confec- tionery, and the cash drawer which contained copper coins to the value of 16s. The police have the matter in hand, but so far have not obtained any clue of the thieves. NEWTON-LE-WILLOW. CRICKBT, BOWLING AND TENNIS CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the members of this club was held at the Pied Bull hotel, Newton, on Thursday evening. Mr. j. Simpson presided and there was a large attendance of members. The minutes of the last meeting having been read and confirmed, Mr. Hindley presented the statement of accounts for the past year, showing a balance at the commencement of £l3 4s. Bd., and total receipts including balance £17385. id. Expenditure £175 Is. 6d. leavings balance due to the treasurer of £1 13s. sd. The balance sheet was adopted. It was de- cided on the proposition of Mr. Liptrott, that in addition to the ordinary subscription, there be an entrance fee of 5 for full members, 2s. 6d. for apprentices and tennis members. Mr. Singleton seconded and it was carried. Mr. .Wilkinson proposed and Mr. Liptrott seconded that the subscriptions be increased to 10s. 6d. for single games,ss. 6d. for tennis members, ss. 6d. for apprentice members, 12s. 6d. for two games, 15s. for three games, i.e. cricket,bowling and tennis. This was carried. Mr. Singleton moved "that should any case arise not provided for by the rules, the committee should make an order, and it should continue in force until the general meeting of the club when it would be submitted for their approval. This was also carried. In the election of officers, Mr. W. Hindley was elected treasurer, Mr. F. Brierley, general secretary ; J. Millington, bowling secretary ; tennis secretary, J. Latchford ; cricket captains, Ist team, J. Cook; 2nd team, W. Wilkinson; sub-captain, 2nd team, J. Pendry. The bowling captains were re-elected. Tennis captains, Anderton, Lane and Speakman. Auditors, Messrs. Boyer and Thompson. Groundsman, F. Hutchins, Pro. R. Bates. It was decided to join the Lancashire and Cheshire County Bowling Association and New- ton Division Bowling Association. A motion by Mr. Speakman that the grounds be open for playing all games, from 2 o'clock to 5, on Sunday afternoons was rejected by a large majority.</p>
        <p>HOUSEHOLD LlNErra,hemmed and marked free of charge —J. and W. D UTTON, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Advt OILS.—GARLAND, Importer, 51. Bridge-street, Warr ng ton. LAMPS —J.S Ci-arland Irod monger,Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>DRUNK /N CHARGE OF A HORSE AND CAB..</head>
        <p>DRUNK /N CHARGE OF A HORSE AND CAB..</p>
        <p>At the Police Court, Earlestown, on Friday morning, before Mr. J. W. Emmett, Ernest Woods, cab driver of Warrington, was charged with being drunk in charge of a horse and cab in Market-st., Eariestown, on Thursday night, and was fined 2s. 6d. and costs. T.O.G.T. EBENEZER LODGE.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the above lodge was held on Tuesday. The roll of officers was called and the minutes of last session were read and confirmed. Bro.W. Taylor read a circular from the District Lodge, and it was decided to join No. 1 Convention. Business finished the programme was proceeded with .the Sisters being responsible. Sisters Woods and Witty, duet; Sister Owen, song, and several other items were given after which the members partook of a coffee supper provided by the Sisters, a vote of thanks being tendered to tkem for. the entertainment.—On Monday the members paid a visit, t 3 Ty.desley Lodge, and were heartily received, after which they gave an enter- tainment, the programme being ; —Bre. W. Taylor, reading ; Bro. Brown, song; Bro. A. Morris, recita- tion; Bro. Layland, song ; Bro. Isherwood, fairy bells, being recalled; song, Sister Foster ; song,Bro. J.Jones ; recitation, Bro.W. Morris; musical glasses, Bro. Isherwood. Sergeant Roberts gave an address. The meeting closed with a hearty vote of thanks be- ing tendered for the visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Tlie'manthly rneetihg of this 'Council Will be' held on Nl:sl3day next at the Town Hall, Earlestown, when the following business will be submitted</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS, PUBLIC WORKS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee's minutes contain the following items : Letter read from Mr. John Parry, hall keeper, asking for an increase-of wages. and it was resolved that the application b^ acceded to, and that his wages be in- creased 53. per week. It was fesolved that the Council •be recommended to pay Mr. Beesley the sum of £2OO, in full settlement of all claims against the Council in respect of the Town Hall (Public Buildings Account). - - Resolved that Mr. Collingwood.Mr. Ormand, and Mr. Beatty be a sub-committee to consider and revise, if necessary,the charges for the hire of rooms, etc., in the Town Hall, and terms therefor, and to report to this committee.</p>
        <p>Letter read from Mr. William Clare. of 18, Nelson-st., Eirlestown, dated January 14th, 1896, and addressed to the Chairman of the Council, asking for the use of the large room in the rown Hall for a" Town's meeting to express sympathy with the suffering Christians in Armenia." The Chairman of the Council stated that he had promised Mr. Clare to bear the cost of the hire of the room.</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Sanitary Inspector's report for the past month was read as follows :—" I am very pleased to be able to again report to your Board that we are well up with our work in the sanitary department. Since my last monthly report I have visited the slaughter-houses, bakehous3s, and milk shops within your district and found all in a fair condition. lam also glad to be able to report that there is very little sickness within your district, which comes under the Notification Act,though we have still a few cases in hospital. I append several cases of unabated nuisances, the first sheet of which was reported last month. My monthly tolls for street hawking are £5 12s. 7d., market, £22 13s. 4d., total £2B 53. 11d. Since the last report, which embraces five weeks, the foreman reports that we have removed from the district 170 loads of pails,lo4 loads from ashpits,and 225 loads of dry rubbish, total 499 loads." It was re- solved that in the cases of unabated nuisances proceed- ings should be taken if there was no abatement within seven days. Resolved that the Medical Officer of Health be in- structed to visit the places named on Mr. Gregory's list of persons keeping pigs in such a manner as to be injuri- ous to the public health, and which have not been visited by the Medical Officer, and report to the Coun- cil.</p>
        <p>Letter read from Mr.G.Ronayne dated December 12th, 1895, re structure near Haydock railway bridge, it was resolved that the letter be referred to the Law Clerk with instructions to take the necessary steps to enforce the Councils' bye-laws. Building plans were considered with reference to which it was resolved that the following plans be ap- proved and passed :—Proposed street off Wargrave New Road: 17 houses on and near Wargrave-road for Mr. Beatty ; 7 houses, St. Helens-road, for Fitzgerald Bros. ; new shippon and provender house for Lord Newton at Mr. Hatton's farm, Crow-lane. Resolved that in future no plans be passed with the division walls of a less thickness than nine inches.</p>
        <p>Resolved that the follnwing • resolution be cancelled, viz., December 16th, 1895, "That the Council do not allow occupancy of any Ruts on the Market Square except on market dais." Resolved that no permanent structure be allowed on the market.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor submitted his estimate of expenditure on main roads account for year ending on March 31st, 1897, and it was resolved that the Surveyor amend his estimate so as to include tne provision of any new foot- paths, on main roads account, within the Council's district. Resolved that a communication be sent to Mr. Hull, L. &amp; N. W. Railway Company, drawing his attention to the dangerous state of the bridges over Sankey brook, near to the canal bridge, below Common Lock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>In the report of the Gas Manager it is stated that the illuminating power of the gas has averaged 20 candles. Attention is called to the "incapacity, and inconvenience of the present purifying plant. The purifiers are des- cribed as too small, and besides being in the wrong place, and additions are suggested, which are approxim- ately estimated to cost £1,700. Sulphate of ammonia had been sold to the amount of £132 14s. 4d. The Gas Manager was questioned by several mem- bers of the Committee, and requested to state his rea- sons for having placed new lamps in Common-lane on the opposite side of the road to where the majority of the cottages are situated. He stated that his reason for having done so was to be able to continue the Gas Mains in a straight line on one side of the road only; after a discussion it was resolved that three of the existing lamps be removed to the other side of the road nearest to the existing cottages.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormand gave notice that at the next meeting of the Council, he should move, that the following resolu- tion ot the Gas Committee, passed at a meeting held on April 22nd, 1895, viz.:—" That interest be allowed at the rate of four per cent, per annum on all consumers' deposits, the same to date from Lady Day last " be rescinded.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman of the meeting referred to the Gas Manager's report for the past month, and suggested•that before it was adopted it should be referred to Mr. Smith, chairman of the bias Committee, for his consideration. This was accordingly done. Resolved that the Gas Manager supply the Clerk with a list of firms supplying meters. The Clerk to invite tenders for the supply of meters to the Gas Committee of the council for year ending December 31st, 1896. Resolved that the tender of Mr. Joseph Markland for one handcart for use in the streets for pipe laying, etc., for the sum of £3 10s., be recornmended to the council for acceptance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTRE.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTRE.</p>
        <p>In the minutes of this Committee it is stated that it was proposed by !Mr. McMahon and seconded by Mr. Gilmour that the Council be recommended to make a payment of £5O on account towards the technical in- struction classes, whereupon an amendment was pro- posed by Mr. Thompson, seconded by Mr. Evans, that no money be paid until a formal application has been made to this Council by the Joint Committee of the Via- duct Technical Institution. The votes for the amend- ment were 6, and for the original motion 5. The Chair- man declared the amendment carried. Resolved that the Chairman (Rev. E. W. W. Kaye) and Messrs. Borron, Wood, and Thompson" be appointed a deputation to the Technical Instruction Committee of the Lancashire County Council, for the purpose of en- deavouring to obtain an Increased grant for the Senioll 1895.6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>SCIIOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCIIOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Th School it-tendance Officer read his monthly report and again mentioned the conduct of the Kenny boys and Albert Jones. The officer also produced his quarterly statement of attendance as follows : QUARTER ENDING DECEMBER, 1895. Name of School No. on A•zer'ge Per Corres. Books. Atten'ce. Centage: Quarter.</p>
