        <p>FURS. —SHALSKIN JACKETS and FURS of every des- cription Cleaned or Re-Dyed and Altered. Fur Skins Dressed and Made-up into Rugs, Muffs, Collars, &amp;c.—B COOKE, Manufacturing Furrier, 11, Upper Newington, and 21, Renshaw-street. J -001, Established over 21 years,</p>
        <p>Paris has seventy halls where fencing is taught. An eminent Austrian physician declares that rheu- matism can be cured by a plentiful diet of ripe fruit.</p>
        <p>THE ARMENIAN ATROCITIES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>MEETING AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>MEETING AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a public meeting in support of the Armenian relief fund, was held in rhe Town Hall, Earlestown. There was a very fair audience, and the chief speaker was Madame Thoumain, an Armenian re- fugee, who appeared in National costume. The Rev. T. R. Matthews presided, and he was accompanied on the platform by the Revs. J. Allatt,J. Bartley,E. R. Davies, F. E. Miller, J. T. Carnal, Mr. E. T. Evans, Mr. G. W. Rawlins, and a number of ladies. The Secretary (Mr. W. Clare), read a letter which he had received from Mr. C. B. F. Borrou, who was an- nounced to preside, in which he stated that having just received a telegram stating that his father had died that morning, he was precluded from being present and occupying the chair at the meeting of the Armenian re- lief fund. He trusted that the audience would subscribe liberally to the fund. Letters were also read apologising for non-attendance from the Rev. A.M. Mitchell (Burton. wood), Rev. J. Carson (rector of Wargrave), and Dr. Hayward, of Haydock, each of whom expressed sym- pathy with the cause, and liberally subscribed to the fund.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said they were all aware of the object for the promotion of which they were collected together. It was as one of the letters expressed to show sym- pathy with their suffering fellow creatures in Armenia. According to a circular for which he quoted that from 4,000 to 5,000 of these people were in a starving condi- tion in Asiatic Turkey, and unless relief was speedily sent on a large scale, a great proportion of them must soon perish. The Rev. J. Allatt moved the following resolution. "That this meeting viewing with great indignation the cruel persecution of the Armenians, deeply sympathises with these, their fellow oppressed Christians, and will heartily welcome and support such prompt measures, as our Government may he able to take in order to pre- vent them, aud if possible worse events -wnich now appear imminent, and put an end to such a deplorable state of misrule ; and further that a copy of this resolu tion be sent to the Prime Minster, Right Hon. A. J. Balfour, and Hon T. W. Legb." Let them,he said, assure the Prime 11.1inister, by such meetings as those that not Great Britain alone, but tiie whole of the English speak-- ing race stood behind him prepared to follow up and sustain every word he might utter. They read that there was a vast amount of indifference pn the Continent with respect to what was taking place in Armenia. Of course there was indifference, and there was DO knowledge, in- formation was suppressed, and the Press was gagged in France, Germany and Russia. Such was not the case in England, where we hod a free Press, free to collect its facts by commissioners of its own, free to print what it fourd to be the truth, and our responsibility was measured by these our privileges, and speak we must,or be choked with a sense of pain and indignation and of shame. (Applause.) The resolution was strictly con- fined within certain limits ; it was not political, it was not sectarian, it was not a question of Islam and its critics. Be asked them to join with him, and say that in the face of these things, they could not and dare not, and by God's help would not endure any longer,the con- tinuance of such a deplorable state of misrule. (Ap- plause.) Rev. J. Bartley seconded. He did not pretend to un- derstand all the political obligations which had rendered the European concert such a dead letter with respect to the Armenian atrocities. The responsibilities of England were heavier than the responsibility which fell upon any European Government. Our chief consideration was humanity. If the Christians were persecuting the Turks they would say in this matter,down with the Christians; and now that the Turks were persecuting the Christians, they did not hesitate to say down with the Turks. (Ap- Madame Thoumain,whO previous to her address asked the audience to join her in prayer, proceeded to give a short history of the Armenian nation, Armenian being, she said,the country of Mount Arrarat, upon which at the time of the flood, the Ark rested. The troubles be- gan because the Armenians refused to believe in Mahomet. They Were surrounded by Turks who were armed to the teeth, and who did not scruple to rob and Murder them. In England the people would not bear for one week what the Armenians were compelled to bear. She then proceeded to detail some of the methods adopted by the Turks to harass the Christians, and showed upon what trivial grounds the latter were imprisoned and put to death. She gave personal experiences, alluding to the imprison- ment of her husband, who was sentenced to death, but ultimately set at liberty. She alluded to the Sasun massacres of 18 months ago, and gave a graphic descrip- tion of some of the horrors which have already through the press become notorious. The massacres had spread all over Turkey in Asia. The Turks had not only killed, but killed cruelly, they had plundered the houses and the people had nothing to eat whatever—all had been taken from them. Trade was stopped entirely because the Turks were living in the towns, and they said "if you show yourselves too much we shall kill you all." She hoped the people of England would do all they could as they_would do to their brother. If they did their duty as a nation and save these people at any price, God , would take care of the consequences. (Applause.) Mr. G. W. Rawlins moved vote of thanksto Madame Thoumain, which the Rev. F. E. Miller seconded, and it was heartily accorded. Madame Thoumain responded, and sang an Armenian hymn. A vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman on the motion of Mr. W. Clare, seconded by Mr. J. Jones. A collection was made in aid of the fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S AMBULANCE CLASS. • PRESENTATION TO DR. ADAMS.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S AMBULANCE CLASS. • PRESENTATION TO DR. ADAMS.</p>
        <p>The certificates gained at a recent examination held in connection with the St. John Ambulance Class connected with Messrs. Crosfield's Soap Works, Bank Quay, War- rington. were presented to the successful candidates on Tuesday evening by Mrs. Croafield. The occasion was taken advantage of by the members to show their ap- preciation of the labours of the lecturer, Dr. Adams, and a surgical couch, which had been subscribed for by the class, was presented to that gentlemen by Mr. John Crosfield in the name of the class. .</p>
        <p>In making the presentation, Mr. CROSFIELD said that he had been requested by the ambulance class to thank Dr. Adams for the valuable lectures which he had given to the students, and for the ability and the self denial which he had shown in giving such valuable instruction. It reflected great credit upon his ability, and also on the intelligence of the workmen, that out of all the students everyone had passed except three. (Applause.) He (Mr: Crosfield) only attended one lecture, because he was afraid of attending any more. (Laughter.) He learned so much about the human body that night, that be thought it was enough fox him. (Renewed laughter.) He heard so much about the bones, and the muscles, and the sinews, and the arteries, and the veins, and the valves, that it made him wonder that any man could live for 24 hours and keep in order. (Loud laughter.) John Bright, who would not take off his bat to a king, said that he always felt inclined t ) take his hat off to a locomotive steam engine, as it wis such a wonderful piece of human skill and ingenuity to drive along a train at 60 miles an hour. But a locomotive steam engine was a plaything, to the human body as Dr. Adams ex- plained it,and therefore one night was enough for him and he was afraid that if he knew anymore, he would begin to think there was something the matter with him every day in the week. (Laughter.) Be had a very pleasing duty to do that night and that was to ask Dr. Adams to accept the surgical couch, as some slight recognition of the instruction he had given to the class. (Applause.) He hoped that the couch would prove a useful piece of i furniture for Dr. Adams n his house,whenever he had a case which needed it. If he did not want it for any patient,. he (Mr. Crosfield) believed that it could be ar- ranged to make a comfortable seat for himself. Be again begged to thank Dr. Adam 3 tor his kindness in instruct- ing the class. (Applause.) He had to apologise for the absence of Dr. Markel who had to leave on Monday with Mrs. Markel for Riviera as he required a change. Dr. ADAMS. in acknowledging the gift,said that he could only thank them very deeply for the kind way in which they acted towards him. The present came as a surprise, and he scarcely knew what to say. He hap- p-ned to be in particular need of a couch, and he did not know whether Mr. Crosfield had found that out. (Laughter.) With regard to the class,there was none in which he took greater interest —(applause)—and he had been told privately by the examiner at the recent exa-e- -ination—perhaps,he ought not to tell them, but they must tell nobody else—(laughter)—but he said that the answers to questions were above the average. (Ap- plause.) He again thanked them for their gift to him. (Applause.) Mrs. CROSFIELD then distributed the certificates to the successful students along with a small present. There were forty-four who had successfully passed the. rat ex- amination ; four,Messrs. John Burgess, John Rose, John Robinson, and James Tavlin, had passed the second,and Mr. William Roberts had passed the third examination, which entitled him to wear the medallion of the order for life. At the close a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield, on the motion of Mr. Newall, seconded by Dr. Adams, and to Messrs. Buckley., and Mansseur, two instructors Of the class, on the motion of Mr. Burgess, seconded by Dr. Adams. These gentlemen suitably responded, and the meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>ST. THOMAS' Y.M.C.—A meeting. of the Mutual Im- provement Society in connection with the above was held in the clubroom on Monday evening: about 30 members were present, Mr. Hallows being in the chair. Mr. Miller re-introduced the subject of the "War Scare," which had been adjourned from the last meeting. The discussion, which seemed to touch mostly upon the Transvaal, was most interesting,and was well sustained by the following :—Mr. Spensley, Mr. Welsby, Mr. C. Houghton, Mr. Dallow,Mr.C. Spencer,Mr. Joseph Miller, Mr. J. Houghton, and Mr. Brereton. So great was the interest taken in the i subject that it was unanimously agreed to again, adjourn t to the next meeting, when Mr. Dallow will open the discussion on the Armen- ian question. Votes of thanks were passed to the cbaii- man and t 3 the essayist.</p>
      </div>
